WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.630
The sun’s gone bad!

00:00:01.630 --> 00:00:03.230
People and animals are melting everywhere!

00:00:03.230 --> 00:00:06.670
The world is coming to an end and there’s
nothing I can do about it!

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:07.759
Will I be able to find food?

00:00:07.759 --> 00:00:11.719
Will I be able to defeat or avoid the horrific
flesh monsters all around me, or the desperate

00:00:11.719 --> 00:00:14.059
and hungry survivors left in this terrible
new world?

00:00:14.059 --> 00:00:16.359
Keep watching and find out.

00:00:16.359 --> 00:00:20.270
Can I survive 100 days in SCP-001 - When Day
Breaks!?

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:21.920
Hey folks, it’s ya boy, Kyle.

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:27.640
You probably know me more for gaming videos
than post-apocalyptic vlogs, but hey, I’m

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:31.390
a versatile guy, and I think I might go insane
from the fear if I don’t talk to somebody

00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:32.390
about all this craziness.

00:00:32.390 --> 00:00:36.230
If you’re alive and seeing this right now,
well, congratulations, you’re probably doing

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:40.930
a lot better than most people here, if you
can call them “People” now.

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:44.600
But if you’re seeing this a few years in
the future, like, I don’t know, you woke

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:48.489
up from a ten-year coma like Rick from The
Walking Dead and you’re wondering what the

00:00:48.489 --> 00:00:50.330
hell happened to Planet Earth…

00:00:50.330 --> 00:00:54.000
This video is probably gonna answer a lot
of your questions.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.330
First thing’s first, whatever you do, you’ve
gotta stay away from the sun.

00:00:57.330 --> 00:01:02.269
It touches you even for a second, you’re
dead, or worse.

00:01:02.269 --> 00:01:04.369
Welcome to Day One of the End of the World.

00:01:04.369 --> 00:01:08.220
For all of you who are still in a solid state
of matter, you’re probably wondering how

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:09.630
I’m still alive, too.

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:14.270
Chances are, it’s for the exact same reason
you are: Sheer, dumb luck.

00:01:14.270 --> 00:01:18.110
I was down here in my gaming basement when
day broke, just level grinding when my TV

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:22.509
got taken over by those SCP Foundation people,
telling us that the sun’s gone evil for

00:01:22.509 --> 00:01:24.689
whatever reason and now we’ve all gotta
stay inside.

00:01:24.689 --> 00:01:29.079
Hell, if I was up there making myself a sandwich
or grabbing another can of Mountain Dew, I’d

00:01:29.079 --> 00:01:30.960
be a freaking puddle right now.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:34.760
It’s funny, my mom always told me spending
all day indoors was bad for me.

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:38.090
I’ll have to mention that to her, if she’s
alive…

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:42.970
Point is, the world has gone to hell in a
handbasket and I’ve now got only one objective:

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:43.970
Survive!

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:50.460
I’m gonna see if I can survive the horrifying,
post-apocalyptic world of When Day Breaks!

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:52.460
For this first day, I’m just gonna hunker
down.

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:54.939
I kinda hope this is just a dream…

00:01:54.939 --> 00:01:55.939
Day 2.

00:01:55.939 --> 00:01:58.289
Alright, I’m up and at ‘em, baby.

00:01:58.289 --> 00:02:01.210
Sadly, I can now report that this isn’t
a dream.

00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:03.820
This really is our horrible new reality.

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:06.860
It’s the sun’s world, and we’re just
living in it!

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:11.000
I’ve been spending the last several hours
just waiting for nightfall outside.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:15.100
Against all odds, the internet and the power
grid haven’t gone down yet - Guess whatever’s

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:18.370
wrong with the sun only affects people and
animals, not objects.

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:21.150
Thank heaven for small mercies, right?

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:24.680
People on Twitter have been live-posting their
situations out there, sharing advice on how

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:28.550
we might all be able to stay out of the sun
and survive this whole crazy thing.

00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:33.610
And hey, unless they’re dead or full of
hot air, maybe those “SCP Foundation”

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:35.940
people know something about what’s going
on here.

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:40.420
If we really can get to their buildings, maybe
we can figure out how to reverse all this

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:41.420
mess.

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:42.420
Maybe.

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:43.690
For now, I’m just gonna focus on staying
alive.

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:47.239
Hopefully night hits soon, I really need the
bathroom…

00:02:47.239 --> 00:02:51.110
Oh boy, it’s Day Three and new issues are
starting to pop up.

00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:55.470
I’ve been heading upstairs to go to the
bathroom, but while I don’t want to be crude,

00:02:55.470 --> 00:03:00.319
I’m running low on toilet paper and it’s
starting to become a problem.

00:03:00.319 --> 00:03:04.650
I ran out of my last roll a few days ago,
and now, I’m starting to go to my bookshelves.

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:09.659
I had a few newspapers left that I tore up
and used for toilet paper first.

00:03:09.659 --> 00:03:13.709
They weren’t exactly comfortable, but hey,
you need to make do.

00:03:13.709 --> 00:03:18.069
But without toilet paper and without newspapers,
I need to figure out what my favorite and

00:03:18.069 --> 00:03:19.390
least favorite books are.

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:23.121
I’m starting with the prefaces of all the
books - seeing as I don’t generally need

00:03:23.121 --> 00:03:24.569
to re-read them.

00:03:24.569 --> 00:03:26.569
They’re expendable, you know?

00:03:26.569 --> 00:03:30.670
A lot of these books I haven’t read since
I was fifteen, so maybe those’ll be the

00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:31.670
ones.

00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:34.650
I can’t make my mind up on whether I’m
gonna use the Harry Potter books or the Percy

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:36.989
Jackson and the Olympian books first.

00:03:36.989 --> 00:03:40.349
Uhhh, let me know what you think in the comments,
I guess.

00:03:40.349 --> 00:03:43.060
I need to go to the bathroom.

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:46.180
Day Four, and now, I’m trying to figure
out how to pass the time.

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:49.450
As you already know, I’m an avowed, life-long
gamer.

00:03:49.450 --> 00:03:53.500
So, while the electricity and the internet
still work, I’m gonna keep gaming to pass

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:56.840
the time and keep my all-too-precious sanity.

00:03:56.840 --> 00:04:01.689
I still have no intention of going out there,
even at night, but it’s left me feeling

00:04:01.689 --> 00:04:02.720
kind of stir-crazy.

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:08.180
I want to walk around the city again, I want
to go for a drive and feel the breeze in my

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:09.180
hair.

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:14.170
But seeing as I can’t do that without experiencing
a truly horrifying transformation, I’ve

00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:16.720
been spending a lot of time on GTA V online.

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:20.639
Guess we’ll never get GTA 6 now, what a
bummer.

00:04:20.639 --> 00:04:25.660
Still, these last few days it’s felt more
like Los Santos has been my home than where

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:26.660
I actually live.

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:28.780
There were even a few other people on the
server.

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:32.690
I don’t know about you, but I take some
comfort in that.

00:04:32.690 --> 00:04:35.690
Hey folks, I’m back, thankfully.

00:04:35.690 --> 00:04:37.020
Welcome to Day Five.

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:41.570
We’ve still got electricity, thankfully,
hence why you can still see this.

00:04:41.570 --> 00:04:44.070
I haven’t heard anything from my family.

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:46.350
I don’t wanna assume the worst, but…

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:49.259
It’s probably just best not to think about
it.

00:04:49.259 --> 00:04:52.540
I’ve been heading up and downstairs to grab
more food at night.

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:55.610
You’re probably wondering, “But Kyle,
why don’t you bring it all downstairs to

00:04:55.610 --> 00:04:57.340
save going up altogether?”

00:04:57.340 --> 00:05:01.820
To which I’d say, I don’t have a fridge
downstairs, smart-ass.

00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:06.150
But I’m starting to realize food is gonna
be a real issue here.

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:11.500
It’s kinda stupid, now I think about it
- In all the zombie movies and TV shows I

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:13.910
used to watch, it was all bullets and baseball
bats.

00:05:13.910 --> 00:05:18.250
Killing your way through all those undead
freaks and worrying about the rest later.

00:05:18.250 --> 00:05:22.900
Guess they don’t want you to think about
how you’re only ever a couple of weeks away

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:27.750
from starvation, kinda ruins the badass post-apocalypse
power fantasy.

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:32.300
I only have a couple days worth of food left
here, and after that, I’m gonna need to

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:34.050
go out and search for more.

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:36.960
Or I’m gonna need to relocate.

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:39.759
I don’t feel comfortable here anymore, y’know?

00:05:39.759 --> 00:05:43.650
Early on, I thought when you got exposed to
the sunlight, it just killed you.

00:05:43.650 --> 00:05:45.690
But no, it’s worse.

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:49.350
You keep living, you’re just changed into
one of those…

00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:50.350
Things.

00:05:50.350 --> 00:05:56.030
These last few days, I’ve looked out the
windows when I’ve come up at night for food.

00:05:56.030 --> 00:05:59.190
I see them sometimes, slithering in the yard
or down the street, these things that used

00:05:59.190 --> 00:06:00.190
to be people.

00:06:00.190 --> 00:06:04.580
I wonder if they’re people that I knew once,
before all this, then I try to shove that

00:06:04.580 --> 00:06:06.050
thought out of my mind.

00:06:06.050 --> 00:06:09.540
Freaking myself out about all this doesn’t
help, I know that much.

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:15.139
I just keep thinking about how they move,
with this weird kind of… purpose?

00:06:15.139 --> 00:06:19.099
Like they’re searching for something, but
what could they be searching for?

00:06:19.099 --> 00:06:22.220
I’m just gonna go and get more food.

00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:23.220
We’ll speak again soon.

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:26.030
Stay safe, whoever you are.

00:06:26.030 --> 00:06:27.580
Welcome to Day 6.

00:06:27.580 --> 00:06:30.020
It’s nighttime now, and I’m heading out
for the first time.

00:06:30.020 --> 00:06:32.940
I keep seeing these weird, slimy creatures
everywhere.

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:37.259
They make me kinda sick to look at them, but
I try my best to just keep moving.

00:06:37.259 --> 00:06:40.870
I’m on a mission tonight: I’m gonna go
to the local supermarket and check if there’s

00:06:40.870 --> 00:06:43.530
still food there, while also grabbing myself
a quick snack.

00:06:43.530 --> 00:06:44.780
I’m gonna keep this one brief.

00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:47.900
I don’t wanna do a full shopping spree tonight,
it’s already too late.

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:50.070
I just need to know the food is there.

00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:53.830
I decided earlier in the night, if I survive
this thing, and the getting is good at the

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:59.400
Bargain Mart, I’ll make my way back tomorrow
for something a little more substantial.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:02.620
After all, the fewer trips I make out, the
safer I’m likely to be.

00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:06.220
By the time I made it to the supermarket,
though, while I was practically a nervous

00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:10.190
wreck from the fear of turning into one of
those things, I made an amazing discovery:

00:07:10.190 --> 00:07:14.699
While the windows were broken and the floor
was a mess, most of the food was still there.

00:07:14.699 --> 00:07:15.699
Day 7.

00:07:15.699 --> 00:07:17.970
Or should I say, night seven.

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:21.879
Galvanized by my success from the previous
days, I decided to come back to the supermarket

00:07:21.879 --> 00:07:22.879
with a shopping cart.

00:07:22.879 --> 00:07:26.389
I wanted to get enough food for at least a
week, so I wouldn’t need to come back out

00:07:26.389 --> 00:07:27.389
again.

00:07:27.389 --> 00:07:29.470
Hey, maybe I’m not so bad at this whole
apocalypse thing after all…

00:07:29.470 --> 00:07:32.600
I grabbed plenty of canned food from the supermarket.

00:07:32.600 --> 00:07:36.879
Most of the perishables had already gone moldy
by the time I showed up, so fresh fruit was

00:07:36.879 --> 00:07:37.879
out of the question.

00:07:37.879 --> 00:07:42.120
Suddenly, I started getting scared about the
thought of scurvy, but pushed it quickly from

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:43.120
my mind.

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:44.740
I’d cross that bridge when I got to it.

00:07:44.740 --> 00:07:48.090
Hey hey, it’s Day 8, and I’m still kicking!

00:07:48.090 --> 00:07:50.300
That’s gotta count for something, right?

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:54.909
I started taking more trips out at night time,
just to stretch my legs and keep the blood

00:07:54.909 --> 00:07:55.909
flowing.

00:07:55.909 --> 00:07:59.620
When the slithering things that were once
people passed me, I’d just make myself scarce

00:07:59.620 --> 00:08:01.240
and hide in the shadows.

00:08:01.240 --> 00:08:04.740
I hear them muttering sometimes in their melted
voices.

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:10.389
It’s unsettling, but it’s amazing what
you’ll get used to in just over a week.

00:08:10.389 --> 00:08:13.229
It’s eerie to see all these streets without
people in them.

00:08:13.229 --> 00:08:16.640
I know that I should probably just stay inside,
but I can’t.

00:08:16.640 --> 00:08:21.070
I don’t know if this will ever just stop,
and if it doesn’t, I don’t want to spend

00:08:21.070 --> 00:08:23.330
my last days cooped up in my own basement.

00:08:23.330 --> 00:08:24.990
Day 9.

00:08:24.990 --> 00:08:29.540
You know, there are some benefits to being
in the post-apocalypse, to ever so slightly

00:08:29.540 --> 00:08:31.620
offset all the utterly crushing downsides.

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:36.060
While, during the day, we’re all prisoners
of the sun in our own homes, at night, we

00:08:36.060 --> 00:08:37.690
can do whatever the hell we want.

00:08:37.690 --> 00:08:41.520
I took a baseball bat I keep in my closet
and went to a local furniture store.

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:44.980
I smashed up every single vase and all the
windows, like one of those Rage Rooms, because

00:08:44.980 --> 00:08:45.980
nobody could stop me.

00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:50.320
Then, afterwards, I went straight to the local
computer and gaming store and took all the

00:08:50.320 --> 00:08:52.490
AlienWare tech I could physically carry.

00:08:52.490 --> 00:08:54.260
There’s no value in money anymore.

00:08:54.260 --> 00:08:57.130
If you want something, you can just go and
take it.

00:08:57.130 --> 00:08:59.170
Every cloud has a silver lining.

00:08:59.170 --> 00:09:00.170
Day 10.

00:09:00.170 --> 00:09:01.510
More GTA V today.

00:09:01.510 --> 00:09:04.760
I decided to get on my headset and speak to
the few others who were still around and on

00:09:04.760 --> 00:09:05.959
the servers.

00:09:05.959 --> 00:09:10.640
It was so nice to speak to other human beings
for once - They came from all over the world,

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:13.680
and they were dealing with this same “evil
sun” insanity as me.

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:18.250
You have no idea how incredibly valuable it
is to find people to talk to in a time like

00:09:18.250 --> 00:09:19.250
this.

00:09:19.250 --> 00:09:21.380
The others players had plenty of theories
as to why all this had happened.

00:09:21.380 --> 00:09:24.700
Some thought it was some kind of mutant solar
flare they’d remembered reading about on

00:09:24.700 --> 00:09:27.160
some conspiracy forum back in the day.

00:09:27.160 --> 00:09:31.110
Others speculated that it was the result of
some weapon created by the US or Russian or

00:09:31.110 --> 00:09:33.150
Chinese military that’d gone wrong.

00:09:33.150 --> 00:09:37.769
One person said that maybe it was a punishment
from God, like maybe, on some level, we all

00:09:37.769 --> 00:09:38.769
deserved it.

00:09:38.769 --> 00:09:40.510
Things got pretty quiet after that.

00:09:40.510 --> 00:09:41.510
Day 11.

00:09:41.510 --> 00:09:44.140
I’ve been having the most terrible nightmares
lately.

00:09:44.140 --> 00:09:47.500
It’s probably just the product of all the
stress I’ve been under lately.

00:09:47.500 --> 00:09:51.790
But, in the nightmares, I’m running down
a dark street, being chased by those flesh

00:09:51.790 --> 00:09:52.790
creatures.

00:09:52.790 --> 00:09:54.860
I’m moving fast but they’re moving faster.

00:09:54.860 --> 00:09:59.339
They’re whispering to me but I can’t make
out anything they’re saying.

00:09:59.339 --> 00:10:04.110
This morning - which is to say, this evening
- I woke up screaming and drenched in sweat.

00:10:04.110 --> 00:10:11.940
I can’t really explain why, but I feel like
something terrible is going to happen soon…

00:10:11.940 --> 00:10:12.940
Okay, okay, I’m alive.

00:10:12.940 --> 00:10:13.940
That’s enough, isn’t it?

00:10:13.940 --> 00:10:15.800
And if you’re watching this, I assume you’re
alive too.

00:10:15.800 --> 00:10:19.779
Congratulations, Welcome to the nightmare
space between day 11 and day 27.

00:10:19.779 --> 00:10:22.440
Sorry that I haven’t been in contact for
so long.

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:25.220
As you can see, I’m not at home anymore.

00:10:25.220 --> 00:10:28.160
You couldn’t pay me to go back there - Ha.

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:29.551
Not that money is worth anything anymore.

00:10:29.551 --> 00:10:34.130
A lot’s happened since I last made one of
these, and I wish I could tell you any of

00:10:34.130 --> 00:10:35.130
it was good.

00:10:35.130 --> 00:10:38.540
Hell, I wish I could forget it all, but the
things I’ve seen and heard…

00:10:38.540 --> 00:10:42.571
I don’t think they’re ever gonna leave
my head, no matter what I do.

00:10:42.571 --> 00:10:46.780
I thought about making another entry now and
then, but I always found a reason to put it

00:10:46.780 --> 00:10:47.780
off.

00:10:47.780 --> 00:10:50.560
It’s remarkable how all your other priorities
fall away when you’re thinking about where

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:55.250
your next meal is coming from, it just kind
of puts everything in perspective.

00:10:55.250 --> 00:10:58.800
Of course, during my travels, I saw more of
those freaks slithering around.

00:10:58.800 --> 00:11:01.959
Sorry, sorry, I know I shouldn’t call them
that.

00:11:01.959 --> 00:11:05.430
It’s kind of a coping mechanism, you know?

00:11:05.430 --> 00:11:08.090
It all gets a lot harder when you have to
think of them as…

00:11:08.090 --> 00:11:09.090
Ex-people.

00:11:09.090 --> 00:11:14.290
That’s another thing all those goofy zombie
shows got wrong: It’s a lot harder to separate

00:11:14.290 --> 00:11:20.330
what they were from what they are now, especially
when you know these were your friends, your

00:11:20.330 --> 00:11:21.330
neighbors, your…

00:11:21.330 --> 00:11:24.290
Well, I can’t avoid talking about it forever,
can I?

00:11:24.290 --> 00:11:27.790
I stuffed my backpack with whatever I could
grab and left my home two nights ago.

00:11:27.790 --> 00:11:31.880
It wasn’t just because I was going stir
crazy back there, though I admit, that didn’t

00:11:31.880 --> 00:11:32.880
exactly help.

00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:34.890
It was what happened there.

00:11:34.890 --> 00:11:38.820
I’d just come back from a food run, put
most of it in the fridge, then retired back

00:11:38.820 --> 00:11:42.720
down into the basement to enjoy a late-night
snack and do a little gaming to keep myself

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:43.720
sane.

00:11:43.720 --> 00:11:46.779
I’d been doing everything I could to reverse
my circadian rhythms and sleep during the

00:11:46.779 --> 00:11:51.040
day, just so I could be fully operational
during the twelve-hour period that going outside

00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:52.040
wouldn’t melt me.

00:11:52.040 --> 00:11:56.950
But just like all those stories they told
us when we were kids: There are monsters out

00:11:56.950 --> 00:11:59.540
there at night, and they’re looking for
us.

00:11:59.540 --> 00:12:04.150
When I first heard the sound, I wondered if
it was something in-game, or maybe dripping

00:12:04.150 --> 00:12:05.970
from a leaky pipe.

00:12:05.970 --> 00:12:09.120
But no, it was too close to be fake and too
viscous to be water.

00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:13.100
That’s when I looked at the door and saw
this awful pink slime slithering in underneath

00:12:13.100 --> 00:12:14.100
my door.

00:12:14.100 --> 00:12:17.740
It was one of those things, those Ex-People,
trying to get in.

00:12:17.740 --> 00:12:22.070
That’d be bad enough, but then, it started
talking to me…

00:12:22.070 --> 00:12:23.070
“Kyle…

00:12:23.070 --> 00:12:24.070
My darling…

00:12:24.070 --> 00:12:29.089
Why are you all cooped up down here?

00:12:29.089 --> 00:12:31.000
It isn’t healthy.

00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:38.120
You ought to come out side, sweetie, get some
sun…”

00:12:38.120 --> 00:12:39.560
It was my mom.

00:12:39.560 --> 00:12:41.950
Well, it used to be.

00:12:41.950 --> 00:12:44.250
Guess she wanted to come over and visit me.

00:12:44.250 --> 00:12:48.019
Needless to say, I got out of there, and I’ve
got no intention of going back.

00:12:48.019 --> 00:12:49.490
That place is dead to me now.

00:12:49.490 --> 00:12:54.410
I don’t ever want to think about that voice
ever again, both her and so…

00:12:54.410 --> 00:12:55.410
Not her.

00:12:55.410 --> 00:13:00.200
So now, I’m on the move, guess I’ll speak
to you again when I stop.

00:13:00.200 --> 00:13:01.240
Stay safe out there.

00:13:01.240 --> 00:13:02.240
Day 28.

00:13:02.240 --> 00:13:04.490
I decided it was best to make my way out of
town, towards the fringes.

00:13:04.490 --> 00:13:08.730
When day first broke, the people who were
in the most densely populated areas were the

00:13:08.730 --> 00:13:09.730
first to go.

00:13:09.730 --> 00:13:13.540
That’s why I decided to hole up in a gas
station last night, just to avoid the sun.

00:13:13.540 --> 00:13:16.170
But during the night, people came.

00:13:16.170 --> 00:13:18.399
Not Ex-People, actual people.

00:13:18.399 --> 00:13:22.490
They showed up in a Jeep outside the building,
refueled, and then came in.

00:13:22.490 --> 00:13:26.270
They were wearing black, cobbled-together
outfits and hockey masks.

00:13:26.270 --> 00:13:29.140
They were all either carrying bats or axes,
too.

00:13:29.140 --> 00:13:32.860
You can probably understand why I didn’t
decide to introduce myself when they busted

00:13:32.860 --> 00:13:33.860
their way in.

00:13:33.860 --> 00:13:36.850
I concealed myself in a broom closet while
they searched around.

00:13:36.850 --> 00:13:38.250
It was nerve-shredding.

00:13:38.250 --> 00:13:41.170
I’d never been more thankful in my life
than when they left.

00:13:41.170 --> 00:13:42.170
Day 29.

00:13:42.170 --> 00:13:44.709
Coming up on a month of this madness.

00:13:44.709 --> 00:13:48.260
After the incident at the gas station, I realized
I needed some kind of defense.

00:13:48.260 --> 00:13:51.589
It’s not just the sun and those creatures
I need to worry about.

00:13:51.589 --> 00:13:53.949
Just like the old world, people could be dangerous
here too.

00:13:53.949 --> 00:13:56.430
That’s why I snuck into a gun store in the
dead of night.

00:13:56.430 --> 00:14:00.840
Some of it had been looted, but much like
the supermarket near my home, there was plenty

00:14:00.840 --> 00:14:01.840
still here.

00:14:01.840 --> 00:14:06.250
The walls were covered in all manner of rifles,
shotguns, and even submachine guns.

00:14:06.250 --> 00:14:10.380
I’d heard somewhere that revolvers are more
reliable and easy to maintain than other types,

00:14:10.380 --> 00:14:16.579
so since I’m a gun novice, I grabbed a revolver
and stuffed my pockets with as many bullets

00:14:16.579 --> 00:14:17.579
as I could carry.

00:14:17.579 --> 00:14:19.600
Let’s hope I never need to use any of them.

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:20.600
Day 30.

00:14:20.600 --> 00:14:23.400
Do I get to call this a month of survival?

00:14:23.400 --> 00:14:26.480
If we’re talking February, I’d be a month
in already.

00:14:26.480 --> 00:14:29.830
What a horribly dubious honor that is.

00:14:29.830 --> 00:14:34.079
I saw something disgusting last night and
I thought I’d share it just to get it off

00:14:34.079 --> 00:14:35.079
my mind.

00:14:35.079 --> 00:14:38.180
Last night, as I was moving through the wilderness,
I saw a group of other survivors gathered

00:14:38.180 --> 00:14:39.440
around the campfire.

00:14:39.440 --> 00:14:44.100
I remained scarce but approached, just to
see what was going on, still carrying my revolver,

00:14:44.100 --> 00:14:45.220
just to be safe.

00:14:45.220 --> 00:14:50.910
But the people around the campfire were eating
something, and when I saw what they were eating,

00:14:50.910 --> 00:14:53.029
I almost threw up.

00:14:53.029 --> 00:14:57.940
They were chopping up one of the Ex-People,
cooking the parts over the fire, and eating

00:14:57.940 --> 00:14:58.940
it.

00:14:58.940 --> 00:15:01.540
Day 31, a month by anyone’s definition.

00:15:01.540 --> 00:15:06.100
Ever since seeing those others eating one
of the Ex-people, I’ve had trouble eating

00:15:06.100 --> 00:15:07.630
even normal food myself.

00:15:07.630 --> 00:15:10.300
My stomach aches and my throat burns.

00:15:10.300 --> 00:15:11.829
I feel so weak.

00:15:11.829 --> 00:15:13.589
I keep laying down and resting.

00:15:13.589 --> 00:15:19.220
I know I need to eat soon if I want to survive
to day 32, but every time I think about eating,

00:15:19.220 --> 00:15:23.350
I think about the gooey flesh of the Ex-People.

00:15:23.350 --> 00:15:27.020
Sometimes, I wish I hadn’t survived this
long.

00:15:27.020 --> 00:15:28.400
I’ll eat soon.

00:15:28.400 --> 00:15:30.000
I just need to sleep first…

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:31.100
Day 32 to 43.

00:15:31.100 --> 00:15:34.720
If you’ve lived this long, you really ought
to be proud of yourself.

00:15:34.720 --> 00:15:40.360
I’ve seen thousands of those slimy Ex-People,
and there are probably millions more out there

00:15:40.360 --> 00:15:43.230
- Hell, maybe even billions, if we’re being
honest with ourselves here.

00:15:43.230 --> 00:15:45.580
Am I just talking into the void here?

00:15:45.580 --> 00:15:49.500
Is there even anyone else out there who’s
human enough to watch this stuff?

00:15:49.500 --> 00:15:52.570
Maybe I just need to keep thinking about posterity.

00:15:52.570 --> 00:15:56.740
On the off-chance that the world ever gets
better, and we reach some time when children

00:15:56.740 --> 00:16:01.820
are born again and all this fades from human
memory, you’ll still have these stupid,

00:16:01.820 --> 00:16:05.100
pointless little videos to remember how awful
all this was.

00:16:05.100 --> 00:16:09.930
That way, at least I can make myself believe
this all had some kind of point.

00:16:09.930 --> 00:16:12.180
So, what’s happened?

00:16:12.180 --> 00:16:14.920
With me, not much.

00:16:14.920 --> 00:16:19.079
Still moving at night, surviving, hiding in
closets and underground parking complexes

00:16:19.079 --> 00:16:20.079
during daylight.

00:16:20.079 --> 00:16:24.880
I’m down to my last few cans, so I’m hoping
to hit a supermarket soon.

00:16:24.880 --> 00:16:30.810
What a ridiculous way to go: Starving in this
new world, with so many new, interesting ways

00:16:30.810 --> 00:16:31.810
to die.

00:16:31.810 --> 00:16:35.310
With the Ex-People, things have been a little
more eventful.

00:16:35.310 --> 00:16:39.269
There used to be one blob to a person, but
they’ve started…

00:16:39.269 --> 00:16:40.269
Joining up?

00:16:40.269 --> 00:16:42.670
That’s the best way I can put it.

00:16:42.670 --> 00:16:47.100
Things that used to be people and animals
are starting to melt together, getting bigger

00:16:47.100 --> 00:16:48.100
and bigger.

00:16:48.100 --> 00:16:52.600
They’ve never been aggressive, but I think
it’s best to stay out of their way.

00:16:52.600 --> 00:16:57.050
Whatever all this is about, I’m streetwise
enough now to know it can’t be anything

00:16:57.050 --> 00:16:58.050
good.

00:16:58.050 --> 00:17:02.319
I’ll just keep moving, and I hope you can
do the same, whoever the hell you are.

00:17:02.319 --> 00:17:06.820
Hopefully, the next time I check in with you,
it’s with better news than this.

00:17:06.820 --> 00:17:07.890
Day 44.

00:17:07.890 --> 00:17:10.419
I saw a shootout on the road last night.

00:17:10.419 --> 00:17:15.750
The people who are left, the ones who are
still indeed people, are becoming less human.

00:17:15.750 --> 00:17:20.630
Something about situations like this, this
sustained stress and pain and hopelessness,

00:17:20.630 --> 00:17:21.709
it weighs on you.

00:17:21.709 --> 00:17:26.059
There are no rules in the post-apocalypse,
the only thing that can stop you from doing

00:17:26.059 --> 00:17:28.170
anything is a bullet to the head.

00:17:28.170 --> 00:17:32.679
Five or six people last night, as afraid and
desperate and hungry as me, gunned each other

00:17:32.679 --> 00:17:33.679
down.

00:17:33.679 --> 00:17:39.850
They did this for reasons I will never understand,
even if I wanted to, because there are no

00:17:39.850 --> 00:17:41.289
survivors left to tell the tale.

00:17:41.289 --> 00:17:43.910
What a funny world we live in.

00:17:43.910 --> 00:17:44.910
Day 45.

00:17:44.910 --> 00:17:45.910
I sleep when I can.

00:17:45.910 --> 00:17:49.780
It’s surreal, I remember when I feared the
dark and loved bright, sunny days.

00:17:49.780 --> 00:17:54.480
Even all this time in, I still don’t think
I’m used to the switch being flipped.

00:17:54.480 --> 00:17:56.140
I’ve been having awful dreams again.

00:17:56.140 --> 00:18:00.870
I’m still running in them, with a deep red
sun shining up in the sky.

00:18:00.870 --> 00:18:03.919
I’m being chased by a mountain of flesh
the size of Mount Everest.

00:18:03.919 --> 00:18:06.100
It’s swallowing up the city behind me.

00:18:06.100 --> 00:18:07.549
It keeps getting closer.

00:18:07.549 --> 00:18:11.010
No matter how fast I go, I just can’t escape.

00:18:11.010 --> 00:18:12.549
It’ll get me eventually.

00:18:12.549 --> 00:18:15.220
Something terrible is going to happen soon.

00:18:15.220 --> 00:18:16.659
I just know it.

00:18:16.659 --> 00:18:17.659
Day 46.

00:18:17.659 --> 00:18:19.070
I shot a man today.

00:18:19.070 --> 00:18:23.710
I don’t know if he survived; I hope he lived.

00:18:23.710 --> 00:18:26.570
We encountered each other inside an abandoned
building.

00:18:26.570 --> 00:18:31.120
I think we spooked each other and didn’t
have any time to ask if we were friends or

00:18:31.120 --> 00:18:32.120
foes.

00:18:32.120 --> 00:18:33.120
We were too afraid either way.

00:18:33.120 --> 00:18:35.980
We both drew our weapons, and I was faster
than he was.

00:18:35.980 --> 00:18:39.800
When my revolver discharged and he collapsed,
I ran off.

00:18:39.800 --> 00:18:43.700
Sun would come up in a few hours, and I just
needed to find another place to hide.

00:18:43.700 --> 00:18:45.510
What the hell have I become?

00:18:45.510 --> 00:18:48.750
I don’t know how things could get any worse
than this.

00:18:48.750 --> 00:18:53.679
Note to self, in the future, don’t even
dare to think “How could things get any

00:18:53.679 --> 00:18:54.679
worse?”

00:18:54.679 --> 00:18:59.650
Because if I’ve learned anything since this
whole nightmare started, that is never a rhetorical

00:18:59.650 --> 00:19:00.870
question.

00:19:00.870 --> 00:19:03.820
Welcome to the space between Day 47 and Day
64.

00:19:03.820 --> 00:19:08.280
If you’re still alive and watching this,
I’m so sorry.

00:19:08.280 --> 00:19:10.950
So, I’ve got good news and bad news.

00:19:10.950 --> 00:19:15.100
I’ll give you the good news first: I’ve
seen more people who haven’t been changed

00:19:15.100 --> 00:19:16.100
yet.

00:19:16.100 --> 00:19:17.150
And the bad news?

00:19:17.150 --> 00:19:21.030
Last time I saw them, they were being dragged
out into the light, kicking and screaming,

00:19:21.030 --> 00:19:25.440
in the tendrils of one of those horrible flesh
monsters I was telling you about last time.

00:19:25.440 --> 00:19:31.909
They’ve gotten a lot bigger now, and when
I told you they weren’t aggressive, well,

00:19:31.909 --> 00:19:33.900
I spoke a little too soon.

00:19:33.900 --> 00:19:36.990
I can’t just sleep during the day like I
used to.

00:19:36.990 --> 00:19:40.820
These monsters - and that’s what they are
now, they’re monsters, not people anymore

00:19:40.820 --> 00:19:43.590
- they patrol, they hunt.

00:19:43.590 --> 00:19:48.640
They actively enter buildings, searching for
hiding places, searching for people they can

00:19:48.640 --> 00:19:50.160
drag out into the light.

00:19:50.160 --> 00:19:54.830
I’ve seen it with my own two eyes - The
second they’re out, they just start melting

00:19:54.830 --> 00:19:59.390
and fusing with the mass, making it even bigger,
adding another voice to the chorus.

00:19:59.390 --> 00:20:04.730
And I hate myself, cause every time I’ve
seen it happen, all I can think is, “Thank

00:20:04.730 --> 00:20:06.850
god that’s not me.”

00:20:06.850 --> 00:20:11.510
I wish I could do something to help, to save
them, but that’s not the world we live in.

00:20:11.510 --> 00:20:14.510
The second they touch the light, it’s already
over.

00:20:14.510 --> 00:20:18.350
I wouldn’t be helping anyone by adding my
flesh to one of those things.

00:20:18.350 --> 00:20:20.890
I don’t want them using my body to get other
people.

00:20:20.890 --> 00:20:25.530
There’s only one thing I can do now: Keep
moving at night, stay hidden, get away from

00:20:25.530 --> 00:20:26.669
population centers.

00:20:26.669 --> 00:20:31.530
I’ve realized where I need to go now: I’ve
still got a distant memory of those broadcasts

00:20:31.530 --> 00:20:33.510
in the early days of the event.

00:20:33.510 --> 00:20:34.510
The SCP Foundation.

00:20:34.510 --> 00:20:40.020
I noted down coordinates to the nearest facility
they had on the books - And if I’m honest,

00:20:40.020 --> 00:20:43.990
“nearest” is only a relative term, cause
at this rate, it’s gonna take me an eternity

00:20:43.990 --> 00:20:45.440
to get there.

00:20:45.440 --> 00:20:48.470
But it’ll be worth it in the end, when I
get there.

00:20:48.470 --> 00:20:52.210
It’ll all be worth it if I can at least
get some answers, at least know why the world

00:20:52.210 --> 00:20:53.890
turned into this Hell.

00:20:53.890 --> 00:20:58.650
Those SCP folks seemed better prepared for
this than anyone, so even if they can’t

00:20:58.650 --> 00:21:02.860
fix this, they’ve at least got to have answers,
right?

00:21:02.860 --> 00:21:04.390
Somebody needs to have answers.

00:21:04.390 --> 00:21:05.950
I really want to believe that.

00:21:05.950 --> 00:21:08.690
When the sun goes down, I’ll start moving
again.

00:21:08.690 --> 00:21:11.029
If you’re watching this, wish me luck.

00:21:11.029 --> 00:21:13.130
I don’t have much food left.

00:21:13.130 --> 00:21:15.350
I’ll do what I can.

00:21:15.350 --> 00:21:21.840
Yeah, I realize I’m not looking great right
now, but trust me, you should see the other

00:21:21.840 --> 00:21:22.840
guy.

00:21:22.840 --> 00:21:24.360
Day 65 to Day 86.

00:21:24.360 --> 00:21:29.789
Never thought I’d make it this far, but
life’s just full of surprises.

00:21:29.789 --> 00:21:33.820
Before you ask - and I mean, why would you
ask?

00:21:33.820 --> 00:21:37.990
It’s not like I can hear you - it wasn’t
one of the monsters that did this to me, it

00:21:37.990 --> 00:21:40.330
was another person, just like me.

00:21:40.330 --> 00:21:42.730
Desperate, hungry, afraid.

00:21:42.730 --> 00:21:47.230
The one difference between me and them was
the fact they had a handgun, and I didn’t.

00:21:47.230 --> 00:21:51.840
They asked for all the food I had, and when
I wasn’t exactly forthcoming, they decided

00:21:51.840 --> 00:21:57.600
to shoot me, and steal the last of my food
while I lay bleeding on the ground.

00:21:57.600 --> 00:21:59.809
Well, that’s not entirely fair.

00:21:59.809 --> 00:22:04.740
They did leave me with one protein bar, which
I had to cave and eat a couple of days ago.

00:22:04.740 --> 00:22:09.630
Since then, I’ve just been foraging what
little food I can from plants along the way

00:22:09.630 --> 00:22:10.909
during my nighttime walks.

00:22:10.909 --> 00:22:14.440
But it isn’t much, and my wound is giving
me grief.

00:22:14.440 --> 00:22:22.650
I sure hope they’ve got doctors at this
SCP Foundation, or otherwise, I may be even

00:22:22.650 --> 00:22:25.110
more out of luck than I thought.

00:22:25.110 --> 00:22:28.960
Here’s the good news for you, since I know
how much you love that.

00:22:28.960 --> 00:22:31.090
I’m not far off of the Foundation site now.

00:22:31.090 --> 00:22:33.909
Even in this state, I’m probably only a
couple of weeks away.

00:22:33.909 --> 00:22:37.840
I think maybe I can will myself to live that
long, at least.

00:22:37.840 --> 00:22:45.500
If I can get some answers just before I die,
then I can die happy.

00:22:45.500 --> 00:22:50.420
And sometimes, folks, that’s all you can
ask for.

00:22:50.420 --> 00:22:51.510
Final stretch.

00:22:51.510 --> 00:22:55.720
Let’s hope I see you again on the other
end.

00:22:55.720 --> 00:22:56.720
Stay safe…

00:22:56.720 --> 00:22:57.720
But…

00:22:57.720 --> 00:22:58.720
I don’t understand.

00:22:58.720 --> 00:22:59.720
Where is everybody?

00:22:59.720 --> 00:23:00.720
Hello?

00:23:00.720 --> 00:23:01.720
Is anybody there?

00:23:01.720 --> 00:23:03.720
God damn it, why is nobody here?

00:23:03.720 --> 00:23:04.720
I don’t understand.

00:23:04.720 --> 00:23:06.820
They were meant to have the answers.

00:23:06.820 --> 00:23:10.929
They were meant to know what was going on
here, but they’re all gone too.

00:23:10.929 --> 00:23:12.580
It isn’t fair, it isn’t fair.

00:23:12.580 --> 00:23:13.580
Is this it?

00:23:13.580 --> 00:23:14.820
Is it all just over?

00:23:14.820 --> 00:23:18.590
The end of the freaking world as we know it?

00:23:18.590 --> 00:23:22.480
It isn’t fair, it isn’t fair, it isn’t
fair…

00:23:22.480 --> 00:23:23.480
Wait…

00:23:23.480 --> 00:23:24.480
Are those footsteps?

00:23:24.480 --> 00:23:26.800
Hello? Yes, hello, I’m over here!

00:23:26.800 --> 00:23:27.800
Who’s there?

00:23:27.800 --> 00:23:30.120
Oh my god, what the hell is that thing!?

00:23:30.120 --> 00:23:33.550
No, no, get away from me, oh god!

00:23:33.550 --> 00:23:34.550
“Disgusting…”

00:23:34.550 --> 00:23:36.560
Oh god, oh god, oh god.

00:23:36.560 --> 00:23:37.830
I’m trapped in the Infinite Ikea!

00:23:37.830 --> 00:23:39.510
How long have I been here?

00:23:39.510 --> 00:23:40.850
How can I get out?

00:23:40.850 --> 00:23:45.679
How can I survive the vicious Staff of the
Infinite Ikea and work with the other survivors

00:23:45.679 --> 00:23:47.910
in this terrifying, endless building?

00:23:47.910 --> 00:23:48.910
Okay.

00:23:48.910 --> 00:23:50.680
Let’s start from the beginning.

00:23:50.680 --> 00:23:51.680
Day 1.

00:23:51.680 --> 00:23:55.809
I’d come to this flat-pack nightmare with
my lovely wife, Brenda, to pick up some stupid

00:23:55.809 --> 00:23:57.740
sofa she saw online.

00:23:57.740 --> 00:24:02.429
We could have ordered it in, but me, being
a cheapskate and a fool, instead decided it’d

00:24:02.429 --> 00:24:07.400
be a dandy idea to head in and pick it up
ourselves - even though I can’t stand shopping

00:24:07.400 --> 00:24:09.050
in these giant stores.

00:24:09.050 --> 00:24:11.690
Of course, it didn’t take long for us to
get separated.

00:24:11.690 --> 00:24:15.230
I was wandering around, just pretending I
knew what I was doing.

00:24:15.230 --> 00:24:17.690
Surrounded by unfamiliar people.

00:24:17.690 --> 00:24:18.690
Then…

00:24:18.690 --> 00:24:21.210
Not surrounded by any people at all.

00:24:21.210 --> 00:24:25.030
Like the complete doofus I was, I’d somehow
gotten lost.

00:24:25.030 --> 00:24:28.909
Just needed to find my bearings again and
then I could call Brenda to come save me.

00:24:28.909 --> 00:24:31.340
But I never did find my bearings.

00:24:31.340 --> 00:24:34.200
The hours went on, and I was still lost…

00:24:34.200 --> 00:24:35.399
Day 2.

00:24:35.399 --> 00:24:40.799
My dominant emotion on this day was nothing
more than sheer humiliation, knowing I’d

00:24:40.799 --> 00:24:43.590
been bested by a damn Swedish furniture store.

00:24:43.590 --> 00:24:47.760
I spent the night before sleeping on a futon,
wondering how I’d gotten myself into this

00:24:47.760 --> 00:24:49.059
flat-pack calamity.

00:24:49.059 --> 00:24:54.380
I spent the day searching for food, my confusion
and exhaustion increasing by the moment.

00:24:54.380 --> 00:24:58.690
For a while, I even entertained the idea I
might have died and gone to some Nordic hell.

00:24:58.690 --> 00:25:03.140
That night, I went to bed hungry, knowing
that if I didn’t eat soon, I might be found

00:25:03.140 --> 00:25:05.360
as a skeleton on a dusty old futon.

00:25:05.360 --> 00:25:07.809
It can’t end like this.

00:25:07.809 --> 00:25:09.620
I can’t die on day 2…

00:25:09.620 --> 00:25:11.240
Day 3.

00:25:11.240 --> 00:25:16.630
I continued my journey through the labyrinthine
bowels of the Ikea, disoriented by the endlessly

00:25:16.630 --> 00:25:18.760
iterating collections of cheap furniture.

00:25:18.760 --> 00:25:23.760
There was something terrifying about the emptiness
of it all, this affordable but impossible

00:25:23.760 --> 00:25:25.750
to assemble void.

00:25:25.750 --> 00:25:28.520
Starvation has always been one of the most
horrific deaths, hasn’t it?

00:25:28.520 --> 00:25:32.289
You could only imagine my relief when I saw
the figure standing a few feet in front of

00:25:32.289 --> 00:25:34.630
me, dressed like a member of Ikea staff.

00:25:34.630 --> 00:25:35.730
I’d found salvation!

00:25:35.730 --> 00:25:38.610
I’d found someone who could help me out
of here!

00:25:38.610 --> 00:25:43.710
But when I approached, I realized something
was horribly wrong: This wasn’t a human

00:25:43.710 --> 00:25:46.730
being standing before me, it was a monster.

00:25:46.730 --> 00:25:51.940
A being I’d later come to know is called
The Staff by the many people who fear them.

00:25:51.940 --> 00:25:54.740
It chased me, repeating, “The store is closed.

00:25:54.740 --> 00:25:59.230
You need to vacate the premises”, flailing
for me with its long, frightening limbs.

00:25:59.230 --> 00:26:04.309
I only survived Day 3 because I locked myself
in a closet and just waited while it hammered

00:26:04.309 --> 00:26:06.360
against the wood with its fists.

00:26:06.360 --> 00:26:09.720
Once the “night” was over, it left, and
I was able to escape.

00:26:09.720 --> 00:26:11.530
Day 4.

00:26:11.530 --> 00:26:15.409
I was feeling some intense hunger pangs on
this day, not to mention the fact that I now

00:26:15.409 --> 00:26:20.100
knew there were monsters out there, just waiting
to beat me to death if they caught me when

00:26:20.100 --> 00:26:21.140
the lights turned down.

00:26:21.140 --> 00:26:26.409
Needless to say, I wasn’t in the best headspace,
and I didn’t have enough charge on my phone

00:26:26.409 --> 00:26:29.270
to justify opening up my meditation app.

00:26:29.270 --> 00:26:30.270
Then…

00:26:30.270 --> 00:26:32.220
I found Nirvana.

00:26:32.220 --> 00:26:36.890
I found the cafeteria, stocked full of delicious,
warm Swedish meatballs.

00:26:36.890 --> 00:26:40.360
No food had ever tasted so sweet to me.

00:26:40.360 --> 00:26:45.600
And this delicious meal also gave way to one
of the most exciting new developments: Gloria.

00:26:45.600 --> 00:26:47.620
Gloria was a veteran.

00:26:47.620 --> 00:26:50.730
She taught me everything I needed to know
at this place.

00:26:50.730 --> 00:26:53.870
Even on the first day I met her, she felt
like someone I’d known for years.

00:26:53.870 --> 00:26:58.620
It was her that took me back to her home in
this place: A little fortress made of Ikea

00:26:58.620 --> 00:27:01.840
furniture, filled with a whole community of
other people trapped in there.

00:27:01.840 --> 00:27:05.049
It was like being allowed into the Garden
of Eden.

00:27:05.049 --> 00:27:06.740
Day 5, and it feels good to be alive.

00:27:06.740 --> 00:27:09.580
I met up with all the different people around
the camp.

00:27:09.580 --> 00:27:12.210
They tell the most bizarre and fascinating
stories.

00:27:12.210 --> 00:27:18.360
This sounds crazy, I know, but I get the sense
not all of them came from the same place as

00:27:18.360 --> 00:27:19.360
me.

00:27:19.360 --> 00:27:24.679
Different Ikeas in different countries, or
maybe even - as nutty as it sounds - from

00:27:24.679 --> 00:27:25.679
different worlds.

00:27:25.679 --> 00:27:30.790
One guy, Tony, who’s been trapped in here
for a year and change, and we got talking

00:27:30.790 --> 00:27:32.940
about different vacations we’d taken on
the outside.

00:27:32.940 --> 00:27:36.970
He told me he was from New York, and I told
him I visited there once, and I loved going

00:27:36.970 --> 00:27:39.029
to see the Statue of Liberty up close.

00:27:39.029 --> 00:27:41.940
That’s when he told me that he’d never
heard of the Statue of Liberty.

00:27:41.940 --> 00:27:45.179
I didn’t know what to make of that.

00:27:45.179 --> 00:27:49.220
Strange little details aside, I couldn’t
be happier to be there with other people.

00:27:49.220 --> 00:27:52.419
The next step would be finding a way out of
here, and back to Brenda.

00:27:52.419 --> 00:27:53.590
Day 6.

00:27:53.590 --> 00:27:57.429
Gloria and several others led me out on our
first excursion - Missions where the goal

00:27:57.429 --> 00:28:00.730
was to collect more food and supplies, and
map the surrounding area.

00:28:00.730 --> 00:28:04.590
I nearly jumped out of my skin when I saw
a Staff member standing in our path at one

00:28:04.590 --> 00:28:06.890
point, and the others all just laughed at
me.

00:28:06.890 --> 00:28:09.980
The Staff member just stood there, placid
and still.

00:28:09.980 --> 00:28:13.620
Gloria told me that it’s okay, the Staff
are harmless during the day.

00:28:13.620 --> 00:28:16.580
It’s only nighttime when they enter their
pattern of aggression.

00:28:16.580 --> 00:28:20.300
So as long as you don’t get lost during
the daytime, you’re generally fine.

00:28:20.300 --> 00:28:24.870
The team often used string, like Theseus,
to trail behind them and ensure they don’t

00:28:24.870 --> 00:28:25.870
get lost.

00:28:25.870 --> 00:28:28.960
It was clear they’d been here long enough
to work out systems for every possibility.

00:28:28.960 --> 00:28:31.360
Felt like I was in good hands with them.

00:28:31.360 --> 00:28:35.260
We collected meatballs and some rugs to fortify
the walls and headed back.

00:28:35.260 --> 00:28:36.260
Day 7.

00:28:36.260 --> 00:28:39.850
This was the first night that we had to fend
off a full-on attack.

00:28:39.850 --> 00:28:46.020
Those monsters, the Staff, came at us in huge
groups, pounding at the outside of our perimeter

00:28:46.020 --> 00:28:47.450
with their balled fists.

00:28:47.450 --> 00:28:49.350
It was terrifying.

00:28:49.350 --> 00:28:53.120
As a way of fighting them off, we tied Ikea
kitchen knives to the end of curtain rails

00:28:53.120 --> 00:28:57.070
and speared them, one by one, until all of
them were dead.

00:28:57.070 --> 00:28:59.090
But they just kept coming.

00:28:59.090 --> 00:29:00.909
More and more and more of them.

00:29:00.909 --> 00:29:04.400
When it looked like one of the walls to the
north of the community was going to fall,

00:29:04.400 --> 00:29:05.809
everyone around me started to panic.

00:29:05.809 --> 00:29:09.390
That’s when Barry, one of the biggest men
in the camp, grabbed a hammer in each hand

00:29:09.390 --> 00:29:10.559
and when outside.

00:29:10.559 --> 00:29:16.200
He fought like a beast, taking on Staff member
after Staff member, tanking hit after hit.

00:29:16.200 --> 00:29:17.799
It was something to see.

00:29:17.799 --> 00:29:21.520
That’s when he took off into the depths
of the store, drawing the Staff away behind

00:29:21.520 --> 00:29:22.820
him and saving us all.

00:29:22.820 --> 00:29:27.799
We never saw Barry again after that, but he’s
the reason all of us made it past day 7.

00:29:27.799 --> 00:29:30.019
Thank you, Barry, wherever you are.

00:29:30.019 --> 00:29:31.019
Day 8.

00:29:31.019 --> 00:29:34.559
Gloria took me out alone today, on another
search for the escape.

00:29:34.559 --> 00:29:38.430
That’s when she told me about her sister:
She’d gone shopping in Ikea with her well

00:29:38.430 --> 00:29:43.190
over a year ago now, when she was separated
and got lost in here, just like me.

00:29:43.190 --> 00:29:47.630
I had a lot in common with her, including
my feelings of guilt for abandoning my loved

00:29:47.630 --> 00:29:50.679
ones and my drive to escape and be united
with them.

00:29:50.679 --> 00:29:54.000
While we were out that day, we didn’t find
anything useful.

00:29:54.000 --> 00:29:56.669
Gloria seemed sad but unsurprised.

00:29:56.669 --> 00:30:01.110
The Infinite Ikea had its way of slowly grinding
you down.

00:30:01.110 --> 00:30:02.440
Days 9 to 17.

00:30:02.440 --> 00:30:05.760
Despite a rocky start, I was finding my legs
in the Infinite Ikea.

00:30:05.760 --> 00:30:10.750
I started to get to know my fellow Ikea prisoners,
I started to understand and truly befriend

00:30:10.750 --> 00:30:11.750
them.

00:30:11.750 --> 00:30:15.360
We went on expeditions pretty much daily,
either to collect new food, more supplies

00:30:15.360 --> 00:30:18.940
to help build up our community, or to keep
searching for an exit.

00:30:18.940 --> 00:30:22.840
To me, it started to feel like we were making
progress, and that helped a great deal to

00:30:22.840 --> 00:30:24.360
keep my emotions semi-stable.

00:30:24.360 --> 00:30:27.470
But it wasn’t the same for Gloria.

00:30:27.470 --> 00:30:31.320
After all, she’d been here for so much longer
than me, and she had her sister to consider

00:30:31.320 --> 00:30:32.320
on the outside.

00:30:32.320 --> 00:30:37.159
To her, these routines I was becoming part
of now felt like a prison within the prison.

00:30:37.159 --> 00:30:38.159
She was trapped.

00:30:38.159 --> 00:30:40.659
Had her sister forgotten her out there?

00:30:40.659 --> 00:30:41.899
Had she been declared dead?

00:30:41.899 --> 00:30:44.100
Were people even still looking for her?

00:30:44.100 --> 00:30:48.760
And it was on Day 18 that it all got a little
too much for poor Gloria.

00:30:48.760 --> 00:30:53.010
She’d snuck out of the camp at night, when
the staff were most active, trying to find

00:30:53.010 --> 00:30:54.010
the exit.

00:30:54.010 --> 00:30:56.809
Sadly, that’d cost Gloria her life.

00:30:56.809 --> 00:31:01.059
There’s no way of knowing what happened
to her exactly, but considering how bruised

00:31:01.059 --> 00:31:05.980
up her body was when we found her, it was
easy to make an educated guess: She’d gotten

00:31:05.980 --> 00:31:10.389
bum-rushed by the Staff and beaten to death
before she could even muster up a defense.

00:31:10.389 --> 00:31:11.760
It was a horrible day to go.

00:31:11.760 --> 00:31:16.281
We tried to give her as dignified a funeral
as we could, given the circumstances: Closing

00:31:16.281 --> 00:31:18.630
her up in a body-sized box.

00:31:18.630 --> 00:31:23.380
That was the day I decided to stop just trying
to survive, and start trying to escape.

00:31:23.380 --> 00:31:27.110
I owed that to Brenda, if she really had gotten
out.

00:31:27.110 --> 00:31:28.700
I couldn’t keep her waiting.

00:31:28.700 --> 00:31:30.539
But I wouldn’t be alone.

00:31:30.539 --> 00:31:34.761
As it turns out, another two members of the
little Ikea community I’d come to know were

00:31:34.761 --> 00:31:39.170
willing to risk it all with me: A man named
Kelvin and a young woman named Vicky.

00:31:39.170 --> 00:31:43.050
They were sick of just waiting around and
fending off attacks from the Staff, night

00:31:43.050 --> 00:31:44.050
after night.

00:31:44.050 --> 00:31:47.760
They both told me they’d rather die during
an escape attempt than while cowering under

00:31:47.760 --> 00:31:49.539
a pile of cheap rugs.

00:31:49.539 --> 00:31:54.659
And so, each armed with claw hammers from
the Six-Piece Ikea Fixa tool kit and as many

00:31:54.659 --> 00:31:59.320
Pruta Tupperware containers full of meatballs
as we could carry, we set off into the great

00:31:59.320 --> 00:32:00.910
unknown… of Ikea.

00:32:00.910 --> 00:32:05.649
We traveled for weeks, marking out tracks
on the ground with the MÅLA mixed-colors

00:32:05.649 --> 00:32:08.850
chalk selection so we never got caught going
in circles.

00:32:08.850 --> 00:32:14.010
One day bled into the next: Nights were spent
trying to hide in closets and bathtub while

00:32:14.010 --> 00:32:17.090
the Staff hunted relentlessly for people just
like us.

00:32:17.090 --> 00:32:19.980
Every single time, we got lucky.

00:32:19.980 --> 00:32:22.169
Until day 41.

00:32:22.169 --> 00:32:26.240
Here’s something you need to know about
the Infinite Ikea: You’re probably already

00:32:26.240 --> 00:32:30.309
aware, if you’re watching this, that the
24-hour cycle of night and day is dictated

00:32:30.309 --> 00:32:32.000
by the store lights up above.

00:32:32.000 --> 00:32:36.999
But the space between day and night isn’t
a gradient here, it’s a cliff.

00:32:36.999 --> 00:32:41.520
You can be minding your own business, when
suddenly, pitch darkness, and now the staff

00:32:41.520 --> 00:32:42.600
are on your ass.

00:32:42.600 --> 00:32:44.370
That’s exactly what happened on Day 41.

00:32:44.370 --> 00:32:49.330
We were in the middle of a kitchenware section,
surrounded by a few docile members of staff,

00:32:49.330 --> 00:32:53.190
when suddenly, the lights switched off and
they went hostile on us.

00:32:53.190 --> 00:32:58.169
They look pretty goofy after the initial shock
has worn off, but believe me when I say that

00:32:58.169 --> 00:33:03.010
these monsters can really pack a mean wallop
when they want to, and we received a reminder

00:33:03.010 --> 00:33:05.250
of this unfortunate fact that night.

00:33:05.250 --> 00:33:09.740
The staff swarmed us, repeating that awful
phrase, “The store is closed.

00:33:09.740 --> 00:33:13.580
You need to vacate the premises”, while
they struck and flailed at us.

00:33:13.580 --> 00:33:17.350
If it wasn’t for our trusty claw hammers,
we would have been dead that night.

00:33:17.350 --> 00:33:20.090
Thankfully, we were able to give better than
we were getting.

00:33:20.090 --> 00:33:24.179
We managed to kill a decent number of staff
members and then make a run for a section

00:33:24.179 --> 00:33:25.580
with better hiding places.

00:33:25.580 --> 00:33:30.669
Myself, Vicky, and Kelvin all stowed away
in a large wardrobe until we saw light filtering

00:33:30.669 --> 00:33:35.179
through the crack in the door, like we were
rejected extras for some painful community

00:33:35.179 --> 00:33:37.450
theatre take on The Chronicles of Narnia.

00:33:37.450 --> 00:33:41.110
But while we survived that night, we didn’t
survive unscathed.

00:33:41.110 --> 00:33:45.700
My face was swollen from a nasty punch one
of the staff members dealt me, and from the

00:33:45.700 --> 00:33:49.750
pain in my chest, I might’ve been dealing
with a few broken ribs.

00:33:49.750 --> 00:33:54.040
Kelvin sprained his ankle during the escape,
and Vicky had a cut on her forehead from when

00:33:54.040 --> 00:33:56.730
one of the staff members kneed her in the
face during the fray.

00:33:56.730 --> 00:34:00.059
You never win these fights, you just survive
them.

00:34:00.059 --> 00:34:04.780
We made a temporary camp in the area where
we could rest and recover, as well as shaking

00:34:04.780 --> 00:34:09.050
off the justifiable fear of death or grievous
harm that dampened our resolve to get out

00:34:09.050 --> 00:34:10.290
of this place.

00:34:10.290 --> 00:34:12.780
That took us to day 53.

00:34:12.780 --> 00:34:15.090
Of course, food was always a concern.

00:34:15.090 --> 00:34:18.750
I don’t want to romanticize what happened
in there, as much as I’m sure someone on

00:34:18.750 --> 00:34:22.870
the outside might want to imagine this whole
experience as some kind of exciting, survival

00:34:22.870 --> 00:34:23.960
horror game.

00:34:23.960 --> 00:34:29.369
But I assure you, it was less “survival
horror” and more survival and horror.

00:34:29.369 --> 00:34:34.079
Meaning not only are we suffering from constant
fear, stress, and paranoia for our safety

00:34:34.079 --> 00:34:36.869
in here, but we also need to keep ourselves
fed and watered.

00:34:36.869 --> 00:34:41.020
You’re just as likely to die from starvation
in here as you are to be beaten to death.

00:34:41.020 --> 00:34:45.520
We went in search of another Ikea Kitchen,
where we could fill up on more water and meatballs

00:34:45.520 --> 00:34:48.169
- your lifeblood in a place like this.

00:34:48.169 --> 00:34:53.540
It took us several more days of searching
and hiding, searching and hiding before we

00:34:53.540 --> 00:34:54.730
hit paydirt.

00:34:54.730 --> 00:34:58.980
By the time we actually got our hands on the
food and water, we were starving and practically

00:34:58.980 --> 00:35:00.530
coughing up dust.

00:35:00.530 --> 00:35:04.740
Those meatballs were the most delicious food
I’ve ever tasted, and I could tell from

00:35:04.740 --> 00:35:08.450
their faces that Kelvin and Vicky felt exactly
the same way.

00:35:08.450 --> 00:35:14.839
Though at that moment, I told myself, if - no,
when - I get out of here, I’d never eat

00:35:14.839 --> 00:35:15.839
another meatball.

00:35:15.839 --> 00:35:20.070
It’d probably give me war flashbacks - or,
I guess, store flashbacks.

00:35:20.070 --> 00:35:24.280
We filled up our Tupperware and shoved them
back into our Ikea Pivring backpacks.

00:35:24.280 --> 00:35:29.450
Then, we needed to keep moving, keep searching,
and keep marking the ground behind us as we

00:35:29.450 --> 00:35:34.210
fanned out into the great flat-pack yonder,
avoiding confrontations whenever we could.

00:35:34.210 --> 00:35:38.460
The three of us still had no idea what insanity
was waiting for us out there.

00:35:38.460 --> 00:35:43.180
We had no idea that there were even more dangerous
things than the Staff lurking in the shadows.

00:35:43.180 --> 00:35:44.180
Day 68.

00:35:44.180 --> 00:35:48.600
Of course, I kept count, writing it down in
my on the back of a JÄTTELIK coloring book.

00:35:48.600 --> 00:35:53.420
Trust me, when every night could mean a horrible
death, you keep track of the nights.

00:35:53.420 --> 00:35:56.040
Not a single one of them escapes you.

00:35:56.040 --> 00:35:59.510
At a certain point, I think we all adapted,
in our own way.

00:35:59.510 --> 00:36:04.849
It was back to caveman times again, learning
to be like our primal ancestors, hiding away

00:36:04.849 --> 00:36:07.599
from the dark and the monsters that hid there.

00:36:07.599 --> 00:36:12.589
So it was extremely surprising for us to get
the most brutal attack during the day.

00:36:12.589 --> 00:36:16.710
At first, I thought we were being attacked
by the staff during daylight hours, like a

00:36:16.710 --> 00:36:17.920
bolt from the blue.

00:36:17.920 --> 00:36:22.420
That’s when we noticed they weren’t attacking
with their hands: They were all holding kitchen

00:36:22.420 --> 00:36:24.640
knives, holding us up like bandits.

00:36:24.640 --> 00:36:28.819
That’s when we realized what had actually
happened here: We weren’t being attacked

00:36:28.819 --> 00:36:34.020
by the staff, we were being attacked by other
humans dressed like the staff, wearing their

00:36:34.020 --> 00:36:36.740
hollowed-out heads like grisly masks.

00:36:36.740 --> 00:36:40.910
They told us that we were coming with them,
and if we resisted, they’d cut us to ribbons.

00:36:40.910 --> 00:36:46.310
And seeing as none of us were movie action
heroes, we thought it’d be best to do exactly

00:36:46.310 --> 00:36:47.520
what they said.

00:36:47.520 --> 00:36:52.060
This was how we fell into the clutches of
Generalissimo Vardagen.

00:36:52.060 --> 00:36:56.270
Day 69, but things were not nice.

00:36:56.270 --> 00:37:02.320
I don’t know if I mentioned this before,
but the community I became a part of in the

00:37:02.320 --> 00:37:05.720
Infinite Ikea after meeting Gloria was just
one of many.

00:37:05.720 --> 00:37:08.300
Nobody knows exactly how many people are trapped
in here.

00:37:08.300 --> 00:37:12.510
I’m hardly a martyr for spending 68 days
in there, I know people who’ve been trapped

00:37:12.510 --> 00:37:17.030
in there for years, who’ve given up all
hope of escape and accepted their lot in life.

00:37:17.030 --> 00:37:21.359
They became the elders of a lot of these communities,
helping others adjust.

00:37:21.359 --> 00:37:25.560
Though of course, Generalissimo Vardagen was
not one of these people.

00:37:25.560 --> 00:37:29.940
Myself, Vicky, and Kelvin were dragged by
the strangers dressed as staff members into

00:37:29.940 --> 00:37:35.010
a fort made of smashed-up wood, nailed together
into a huge, ominous structure.

00:37:35.010 --> 00:37:39.710
It was a far more extensive structure than
any of the communities I’d visited or even

00:37:39.710 --> 00:37:42.290
heard about before in the Infinite Ikea.

00:37:42.290 --> 00:37:47.589
It was a true fortress, guarded by many more
of those knife-wielding people, dressed in

00:37:47.589 --> 00:37:49.800
the clothes and the flesh of the staff.

00:37:49.800 --> 00:37:55.060
They looked like some evil cult straight out
of a damn horror movie; I’d never seen anything

00:37:55.060 --> 00:37:56.060
like it.

00:37:56.060 --> 00:38:00.339
We were dragged into a kind of tent in the
middle of the camp, made out of stitched-together

00:38:00.339 --> 00:38:01.339
rugs.

00:38:01.339 --> 00:38:05.619
That’s where we met Generlissimo Vardagen,
surrounded by his guards.

00:38:05.619 --> 00:38:09.810
I’d later learn his namesake was a set of
steak knives stocked in the Ikea kitchenware

00:38:09.810 --> 00:38:13.960
sections - similar to the knives being wielded
by his gallery of goons.

00:38:13.960 --> 00:38:18.930
The Generalissimo himself was dressed like
some absurd tinpot dictator, wearing a silly

00:38:18.930 --> 00:38:21.400
hat and a jacket covered in fake medals.

00:38:21.400 --> 00:38:26.240
His whole presence felt like a cosmic punishment
for daring to believe things couldn’t get

00:38:26.240 --> 00:38:28.730
any more absurd than they already were.

00:38:28.730 --> 00:38:33.160
His men forced us down onto our knees, and
Vardagen cleared his throat to speak.

00:38:33.160 --> 00:38:38.380
“Quake in fear at the sight of this Ikea’s
ruler, the great and powerful Generalissimo

00:38:38.380 --> 00:38:39.380
Vardagen.

00:38:39.380 --> 00:38:47.220
I have united kingdoms from the gardening
and bathroom supplies departments, and crushed

00:38:47.220 --> 00:38:51.910
dissenting tribes in the office furniture
section to the West.

00:38:51.910 --> 00:38:58.590
If you wish to live, you will swear fealty
to me and join my legion of servants.

00:38:58.590 --> 00:39:04.730
If you do as I say, you will be given safety
and security from the staff come nightfall.

00:39:04.730 --> 00:39:09.440
If you do not bend the knee, I will have you
destroyed!”

00:39:09.440 --> 00:39:14.710
By the time his little spiel was done, the
man was red in the face and sweating profusely.

00:39:14.710 --> 00:39:19.050
It was clear that, much like your average
Ikea shelving unit after a couple weeks of

00:39:19.050 --> 00:39:23.119
use, the great Generalissimo Vardagen had
a few screws loose.

00:39:23.119 --> 00:39:28.580
None of us liked the idea of becoming slaves
to some flat-pack ghenghis khan, so we tried

00:39:28.580 --> 00:39:33.410
to persuade him to just let us go, telling
him that we hoped to escape the store, and

00:39:33.410 --> 00:39:35.900
with our help, he could escape, too.

00:39:35.900 --> 00:39:41.150
That’s when we learned a sobering lesson:
For some people, life inside the Infinite

00:39:41.150 --> 00:39:42.869
Ikea was better than life outside.

00:39:42.869 --> 00:39:47.720
In the real world, the Generalissimo had been
a twice-divorced ex-salaryman with nothing

00:39:47.720 --> 00:39:49.720
to his name but debts and regrets.

00:39:49.720 --> 00:39:52.359
But here, he was a demi-god.

00:39:52.359 --> 00:39:55.190
A leader among the rest of us mere mortals.

00:39:55.190 --> 00:40:00.349
Why would he ever want to go back to the world
that had given him nothing and taken everything?

00:40:00.349 --> 00:40:02.540
Day 70 to Day 84.

00:40:02.540 --> 00:40:07.260
We were forced into weeks of hard labor after
that, toiling under the Generalissimo and

00:40:07.260 --> 00:40:08.910
his gang of brigands.

00:40:08.910 --> 00:40:13.940
The soldiers worked us like dogs, making us
carry food and furniture back to the Generalissimo’s

00:40:13.940 --> 00:40:16.220
Scandanavian furniture fortress.

00:40:16.220 --> 00:40:17.940
One day bled into the next.

00:40:17.940 --> 00:40:21.600
The best I could say about any of this was
that at least we were safe behind the walls

00:40:21.600 --> 00:40:25.461
of the fortress at night, so we didn’t need
to worry about getting murdered by the staff

00:40:25.461 --> 00:40:26.750
in our sleep.

00:40:26.750 --> 00:40:29.080
However, tragedy struck again on day 85.

00:40:29.080 --> 00:40:31.500
Kelvin couldn’t take the work anymore.

00:40:31.500 --> 00:40:34.770
One day, I think his mind just snapped.

00:40:34.770 --> 00:40:39.540
He refused to follow orders from the Generalissimo
and his lieutenants, even when they threatened

00:40:39.540 --> 00:40:40.540
him with death.

00:40:40.540 --> 00:40:45.000
Sadly, that night they would prove that this
wasn’t just some empty threat.

00:40:45.000 --> 00:40:49.310
When night fell, they tied up Kelvin’s arms
and legs and left him outside the fortress.

00:40:49.310 --> 00:40:53.230
We were just forced to watch as the staff
assembled and beat our friend to death while

00:40:53.230 --> 00:40:55.849
he lay there, unable to defend himself.

00:40:55.849 --> 00:41:00.810
Even in all the time I’d spent in the Infinite
Ikea, that was the most harrowing thing I’d

00:41:00.810 --> 00:41:01.839
ever seen.

00:41:01.839 --> 00:41:05.980
But in one of the few acts of righteous cosmic
justice that we’d seen since being trapped

00:41:05.980 --> 00:41:10.860
in here, all those months ago, just a few
days after Kelvin’s brutal execution.

00:41:10.860 --> 00:41:12.020
Day 90.

00:41:12.020 --> 00:41:13.490
The day of the revolution.

00:41:13.490 --> 00:41:18.869
While I’d love to tell you I started this,
there are no heroes in this story, I just

00:41:18.869 --> 00:41:21.190
happen to be the one telling you about it.

00:41:21.190 --> 00:41:25.490
Some internal conflict between the Generalissimo
and his men boiled over into a kind of civil

00:41:25.490 --> 00:41:28.020
war that tore the fortress apart from within.

00:41:28.020 --> 00:41:30.910
Vicky and I escaped, but in different directions.

00:41:30.910 --> 00:41:34.730
I like to imagine she got out in the end,
it helps me sleep at night.

00:41:34.730 --> 00:41:39.319
But one thing I will tell you: Generalissimo
Vardagen found out what happened to tyrants,

00:41:39.319 --> 00:41:43.430
big and small, when his closest confidantes
gave him the Julias Caesar treatment with

00:41:43.430 --> 00:41:45.510
the knives from which he took his name.

00:41:45.510 --> 00:41:49.210
I don’t think there’s anything wrong in
taking a little joy in that.

00:41:49.210 --> 00:41:50.980
For days afterwards, I just walked.

00:41:50.980 --> 00:41:55.560
I felt so empty, but I refused to just lay
down and let myself die.

00:41:55.560 --> 00:41:59.910
Even though so much of the hope had been beaten
out of me, I couldn’t betray Brenda by just

00:41:59.910 --> 00:42:01.099
giving up in here.

00:42:01.099 --> 00:42:02.480
It wouldn’t be right.

00:42:02.480 --> 00:42:04.119
It just wouldn’t be right.

00:42:04.119 --> 00:42:06.930
The days ticked on, and nothing changed.

00:42:06.930 --> 00:42:08.880
I had no food, no weapons.

00:42:08.880 --> 00:42:11.079
I was getting so tired.

00:42:11.079 --> 00:42:14.640
Then… night fell, and the Staff started
chasing me.

00:42:14.640 --> 00:42:17.060
They seemed even more aggressive than before.

00:42:17.060 --> 00:42:20.270
I couldn’t fight; all I could do was run.

00:42:20.270 --> 00:42:24.560
I ran and I ran and I ran, not even looking
where I was going, as the crowd of staff started

00:42:24.560 --> 00:42:25.970
catching up with me.

00:42:25.970 --> 00:42:27.470
Getting closer and closer.

00:42:27.470 --> 00:42:29.630
I ran until there was a doorway before me.

00:42:29.630 --> 00:42:32.280
I didn’t even think, I was just trying to
get away.

00:42:32.280 --> 00:42:35.920
That’s when I noticed that the store’s
roof was no longer above me.

00:42:35.920 --> 00:42:40.070
For the first time in one hundred days, I
was once again tasting fresh air.

00:42:40.070 --> 00:42:41.810
That’s right, folks.

00:42:41.810 --> 00:42:44.240
On day one hundred, I was out.

00:42:44.240 --> 00:42:46.599
I’d truly made it out.

00:42:46.599 --> 00:42:51.470
This victory, however, was short-lived: A
group of about six staff members burst out

00:42:51.470 --> 00:42:55.100
of the front door behind me, charging towards
me with ferocious speed.

00:42:55.100 --> 00:42:59.369
I couldn’t move, all I could do was grit
my teeth and wince, ready to accept my death

00:42:59.369 --> 00:43:02.770
at what had otherwise been the high point
of my recent life.

00:43:02.770 --> 00:43:05.150
What a depressing irony that would be.

00:43:05.150 --> 00:43:07.730
But instead, gunshots rang out through the
air.

00:43:07.730 --> 00:43:11.040
A hail of bullets cleaved through the staff
members, dropping them to the ground mere

00:43:11.040 --> 00:43:12.990
feet away from me, stone dead.

00:43:12.990 --> 00:43:16.710
That’s when I turned to see a group of men
with assault rifles and tactical gear walking

00:43:16.710 --> 00:43:17.710
towards me.

00:43:17.710 --> 00:43:21.560
In any other situation, this might have been
terrifying, but right then, it was the happiest

00:43:21.560 --> 00:43:23.089
moment of my life.

00:43:23.089 --> 00:43:27.020
These men took me away from the parking lot
of what was now an abandoned Ikea.

00:43:27.020 --> 00:43:31.240
They told me they were from a group called
the SCP Foundation, and that I’d been declared

00:43:31.240 --> 00:43:32.460
missing for some time now.

00:43:32.460 --> 00:43:36.380
I didn’t care about any of that: I just
asked them if Brenda had gotten out.

00:43:36.380 --> 00:43:39.800
If she hadn’t escaped too, then this was
all for nothing.

00:43:39.800 --> 00:43:43.770
You can’t even imagine my relief when they
told me that Brenda was never even trapped.

00:43:43.770 --> 00:43:47.140
She was the one who reported my disappearance
after I’d dropped off the face of the earth

00:43:47.140 --> 00:43:51.480
in what she thought was a perfectly normal
Ikea shopping session, before that building

00:43:51.480 --> 00:43:55.540
was shut down and cordoned off under some
less-insane pretense.

00:43:55.540 --> 00:44:00.140
But Brenda was alive and safe, and I’d get
to be with her again.

00:44:00.140 --> 00:44:05.059
I have no shame in telling you I cried, but
I’d like to specify that they were absolutely

00:44:05.059 --> 00:44:06.210
tears of joy.

00:44:06.210 --> 00:44:11.140
The men from the SCP Foundation told me that
they’d give me medicine that’d help me

00:44:11.140 --> 00:44:15.920
forget all this after I told them my story,
and there’s no part of me that has a problem

00:44:15.920 --> 00:44:16.920
with that.

00:44:16.920 --> 00:44:18.650
Some things are better left forgotten.

00:44:18.650 --> 00:44:23.300
But before the SCP Foundation wipes it all
from my mind and I get to go and live with

00:44:23.300 --> 00:44:29.410
my beloved wife once more, this was how I
survived 100 dreadful days in the Infinite

00:44:29.410 --> 00:44:30.410
Ikea.

00:44:30.410 --> 00:44:36.839
Glass shatters, a D-Class screams, the shrill
cry of the alarm sounds.

00:44:36.839 --> 00:44:40.580
There's been a containment breach at the SCP
Foundation!

00:44:40.580 --> 00:44:46.210
Somehow, The Plague Doctor has escaped his
cell, and is making a break for it!

00:44:46.210 --> 00:44:49.640
Against all odds, he finds the exit, and he's
out!

00:44:49.640 --> 00:44:52.270
He's free, he's on the lam!

00:44:52.270 --> 00:44:56.500
But how long can he elude the Foundation's
attempts to track him down, and recontain

00:44:56.500 --> 00:44:57.819
him at any cost?

00:44:57.819 --> 00:45:03.890
It will take all of his cunning, his intellect,
and his strange anomalous abilities, but he's

00:45:03.890 --> 00:45:08.020
going to try and hold onto his newfound freedom
as tightly as he can.

00:45:08.020 --> 00:45:10.310
I'm getting ahead of myself here, though.

00:45:10.310 --> 00:45:12.220
Let's start at the beginning.

00:45:12.220 --> 00:45:13.500
Day 1.

00:45:13.500 --> 00:45:19.850
SCP-049, or The Plague Doctor, may be an esoteric
anomaly whose grasp of medicine is practically

00:45:19.850 --> 00:45:22.510
Medieval, but he's still a doctor!

00:45:22.510 --> 00:45:25.530
He didn't get this far by not paying attention
to his surroundings.

00:45:25.530 --> 00:45:30.350
So, when an unexpected power outage, triggered
by an experiment gone wrong somewhere in the

00:45:30.350 --> 00:45:34.490
bowels of Site-19, gave him the chance to
escape captivity?

00:45:34.490 --> 00:45:35.530
He took it.

00:45:35.530 --> 00:45:40.590
The Foundation had recently revoked his access
to live experimental subjects again, and he

00:45:40.590 --> 00:45:42.260
had had enough.

00:45:42.260 --> 00:45:46.900
It was time to seize the moment and make a
mad dash for freedom.

00:45:46.900 --> 00:45:50.790
With the security system down, the Doctor
broke open the door to his cell, grabbed his

00:45:50.790 --> 00:45:54.420
trusty apothecary bag, and ran into the hall.

00:45:54.420 --> 00:45:59.080
Guards were already swarming, ready to drag
him back into captivity, but he had planned

00:45:59.080 --> 00:46:00.080
ahead.

00:46:00.080 --> 00:46:04.900
Swiftly, he uncorked a glass bottle of silvery
liquid, spilling it onto the floor, where

00:46:04.900 --> 00:46:09.880
it began to eat away at the tile, the shoes
of the guards, and, if they maintained contact

00:46:09.880 --> 00:46:13.010
for too long, the soles of their feet.

00:46:13.010 --> 00:46:15.750
This bought him just enough time to keep running.

00:46:15.750 --> 00:46:20.730
The sounds of screaming down another hall,
and the raucous laughter of one Dr. Jack Bright,

00:46:20.730 --> 00:46:26.080
signaled to the Doctor that most of the Foundation
staff were otherwise occupied.

00:46:26.080 --> 00:46:31.410
With this in mind, he sprinted on, letting
his chitinous body carry him to the exit.

00:46:31.410 --> 00:46:38.059
Then, for the first time in far too long,
fresh air on his beak, the sun in his eyes,

00:46:38.059 --> 00:46:41.720
the scent of cut grass and damp leaves from
last night's rain.

00:46:41.720 --> 00:46:47.050
The world was here, waiting for him just as
he had left it.

00:46:47.050 --> 00:46:50.119
Of course, the world was still very much in
danger.

00:46:50.119 --> 00:46:54.960
He hadn't just broken free for himself, but
to address the Pestilence head on as best

00:46:54.960 --> 00:46:56.170
he could.

00:46:56.170 --> 00:47:00.320
But for now, as he heard the sound of the
alarm, the guards gaining on him, he had to

00:47:00.320 --> 00:47:02.430
prioritize his own safety first.

00:47:02.430 --> 00:47:04.450
He had to hide.

00:47:04.450 --> 00:47:05.460
Day 2.

00:47:05.460 --> 00:47:10.619
He ran for ages, he couldn't be quite sure
how long as he had no way to measure the passage

00:47:10.619 --> 00:47:14.970
of time except the position of the sun in
the sky.

00:47:14.970 --> 00:47:21.130
It set, it rose, bathing the countryside in
soft yellow light, and set again, plunging

00:47:21.130 --> 00:47:24.680
the land into darkness, all while he continued
to run.

00:47:24.680 --> 00:47:27.690
Then, finally, the Plague Doctor stopped.

00:47:27.690 --> 00:47:28.940
He had found a farm.

00:47:28.940 --> 00:47:34.050
There was a little farmhouse, but he did not
dare disturb the sleeping family and alarm

00:47:34.050 --> 00:47:35.050
them.

00:47:35.050 --> 00:47:36.750
Instead, he turned to the barn for shelter.

00:47:36.750 --> 00:47:41.770
He let himself in, tiptoed past the horses
as they slept there, and made himself a bed

00:47:41.770 --> 00:47:43.990
from straw and a burlap sack.

00:47:43.990 --> 00:47:47.230
From his bag, he pulled a pillow, and a little
spray bottle.

00:47:47.230 --> 00:47:52.790
He misted the pillow with a lavender scented
oil, and finally allowed himself a moment

00:47:52.790 --> 00:47:53.790
to rest.

00:47:53.790 --> 00:47:58.900
He did not need to sleep like ordinary men,
but after such a long journey, he could get

00:47:58.900 --> 00:47:59.900
pretty close.

00:47:59.900 --> 00:48:01.230
Day 3.

00:48:01.230 --> 00:48:04.960
The morning of the Plague Doctor's third day
of freedom began with the high-pitched whinny

00:48:04.960 --> 00:48:08.580
of a terrified horse, and the frantic stamping
of hooves.

00:48:08.580 --> 00:48:12.920
The Doctor opened his eyes to the sight of
one of the horses, now awake and not at all

00:48:12.920 --> 00:48:17.480
happy to see a stranger in its barn, making
its fear and displeasure known.

00:48:17.480 --> 00:48:18.480
"Buttercup?"

00:48:18.480 --> 00:48:20.280
A man called from outside the barn.

00:48:20.280 --> 00:48:21.370
"That you, girl?

00:48:21.370 --> 00:48:22.720
What's got you spooked?"

00:48:22.720 --> 00:48:27.010
The door to the barn creaked open, and before
the Doctor could do anything, he was face

00:48:27.010 --> 00:48:29.240
to face with the farmer who owned the land.

00:48:29.240 --> 00:48:32.210
"Who are you and what are you doing in my
barn?"

00:48:32.210 --> 00:48:33.339
The man demanded.

00:48:33.339 --> 00:48:36.589
"Good sir, I apologize for the intrusion!

00:48:36.589 --> 00:48:38.329
I am but a humble traveler, seeking shelter-"

00:48:38.329 --> 00:48:41.400
"You need to get out of here and stop scaring
my horses, now!

00:48:41.400 --> 00:48:44.040
Don't make me come back with my shotgun."

00:48:44.040 --> 00:48:46.460
"No need for that sort of thing!

00:48:46.460 --> 00:48:47.830
I will be on my way!"

00:48:47.830 --> 00:48:51.770
The Doctor collected his things, and, deciding
to spare the old farmer rather than using

00:48:51.770 --> 00:48:57.540
more extreme methods to change his mind, left
the barn in a hurry.

00:48:57.540 --> 00:48:58.819
Day 4.

00:48:58.819 --> 00:49:03.100
With the farm far behind him, the Plague Doctor
trudged along on his journey, always keeping

00:49:03.100 --> 00:49:06.320
an eye out for unmarked vans and armed guards.

00:49:06.320 --> 00:49:08.940
So far, though, he was safe.

00:49:08.940 --> 00:49:13.089
He hadn't had a spare moment to return to
his experiments, though, which was beginning

00:49:13.089 --> 00:49:14.339
to wear on him.

00:49:14.339 --> 00:49:18.450
The work was what had motivated him to break
out in the first place, and now he wasn't

00:49:18.450 --> 00:49:21.060
sure when he would be able to make time for
it.

00:49:21.060 --> 00:49:22.660
Cruel irony indeed.

00:49:22.660 --> 00:49:28.319
But then, as if his silent prayer had been
answered, he heard a peculiar sound coming

00:49:28.319 --> 00:49:32.369
from the woods just off the dirt road he was
walking down.

00:49:32.369 --> 00:49:34.869
A sickly bray, from an injured animal.

00:49:34.869 --> 00:49:36.799
A poor soul in need.

00:49:36.799 --> 00:49:37.799
A patient!

00:49:37.799 --> 00:49:42.619
He followed the sound to its source: a deer
lay on its side, clearly sick or injured somehow.

00:49:42.619 --> 00:49:45.130
"Do not be afraid, my friend."

00:49:45.130 --> 00:49:46.480
The Doctor said softly.

00:49:46.480 --> 00:49:49.060
"You are sick, but I can make you well."

00:49:49.060 --> 00:49:51.710
He sat his bag down on the grass, and got
to work.

00:49:51.710 --> 00:49:56.520
First, he touched the deer with a gloved hand,
stopping its heart and putting it out of its

00:49:56.520 --> 00:49:57.520
misery.

00:49:57.520 --> 00:50:01.920
Then, he reached into his bag for his trusty
tools and some of his best medicines.

00:50:01.920 --> 00:50:07.330
In no time at all, the deer was reanimated,
cured, walking around again like new.

00:50:07.330 --> 00:50:12.700
Sure, it was a little bit different, stumbling
a bit, and each eye looked in a different

00:50:12.700 --> 00:50:16.109
direction, but the Doctor considered the treatment
a success.

00:50:16.109 --> 00:50:18.299
A day well-spent!

00:50:18.299 --> 00:50:19.330
Day 5

00:50:19.330 --> 00:50:23.710
The next day, feeling reinvigorated after
his successful encounter with the deer, the

00:50:23.710 --> 00:50:26.590
Plague Doctor began his quest for shelter
anew.

00:50:26.590 --> 00:50:31.860
He walked most of the day, until, as the sun
was setting, he came upon something curious:

00:50:31.860 --> 00:50:38.730
a house, dilapidated-looking, with a large
sign out front: "House of Horrors, Enter if

00:50:38.730 --> 00:50:39.790
You Dare."

00:50:39.790 --> 00:50:45.040
Though he didn't know it, the Doctor had stumbled
upon the local town's premiere seasonal haunted

00:50:45.040 --> 00:50:46.400
house attraction.

00:50:46.400 --> 00:50:50.569
To him, it was simply an empty house he could
take up residence inside.

00:50:50.569 --> 00:50:56.099
He was curious, and a bit perplexed, as he
walked past plastic skeletons, animatronic

00:50:56.099 --> 00:51:01.380
werewolves that popped out from behind styrofoam
tombstones, and giant fake spiders in cotton

00:51:01.380 --> 00:51:02.380
cobwebs.

00:51:02.380 --> 00:51:07.630
None of the fake monsters gave him trouble,
so, though he was confused by what he saw,

00:51:07.630 --> 00:51:09.640
he continued to explore the house.

00:51:09.640 --> 00:51:14.589
Then, he found something incredible: a laboratory.

00:51:14.589 --> 00:51:18.060
There was a long stainless steel table, with
a mannequin strapped to it.

00:51:18.060 --> 00:51:23.230
A row of prop surgical tools sat on a tray
next to the mannequin, and along a back shelf

00:51:23.230 --> 00:51:27.440
were rows and rows of jars filled with liquids
that looked like something the Plague Doctor

00:51:27.440 --> 00:51:29.630
himself would pull out of his bag.

00:51:29.630 --> 00:51:36.000
He couldn't believe his eyes, after wandering
aimlessly for days he had found the perfect

00:51:36.000 --> 00:51:37.000
place.

00:51:37.000 --> 00:51:40.290
Almost as if it had been made just for him.

00:51:40.290 --> 00:51:44.480
Of course, it wasn't, it was for the paying
customers who would be coming to the haunted

00:51:44.480 --> 00:51:49.960
house the next day, along with the actors
hired to perform inside, but he didn't need

00:51:49.960 --> 00:51:51.890
to know any of that yet.

00:51:51.890 --> 00:51:55.819
He replaced the jars with his own, and the
tools with functioning ones.

00:51:55.819 --> 00:51:59.720
With the exception of the plastic body on
the table, which he privately wished was a

00:51:59.720 --> 00:52:03.400
real human subject, this was absolutely perfect.

00:52:03.400 --> 00:52:08.710
Day 6, the haunt actors showed up for work,
and were surprised to find a new guy had beat

00:52:08.710 --> 00:52:11.230
them there, and he was already in costume.

00:52:11.230 --> 00:52:16.819
They shrugged it off, they were making minimum
wage and that wasn't enough to ask any questions.

00:52:16.819 --> 00:52:20.430
They were there to suit up and get spooky,
and that was it.

00:52:20.430 --> 00:52:22.780
They did appreciate his plague doctor costume,
though.

00:52:22.780 --> 00:52:25.470
It was much higher-budget than anything they
had.

00:52:25.470 --> 00:52:27.250
"Did you make that getup yourself?"

00:52:27.250 --> 00:52:28.880
Asked a friendly zombie.

00:52:28.880 --> 00:52:30.099
"Indeed, I did!"

00:52:30.099 --> 00:52:33.869
The Doctor replied cheerfully, happy to finally
encounter a group of individuals who were

00:52:33.869 --> 00:52:34.869
unafraid of him.

00:52:34.869 --> 00:52:35.920
"Nice, bro!"

00:52:35.920 --> 00:52:40.550
The zombie gave him a thumbs-up, and if the
Plague Doctor could have with his rigid beaked

00:52:40.550 --> 00:52:42.589
face, he would have smiled.

00:52:42.589 --> 00:52:48.150
These new colleagues were a bit unusual-looking,
with their ghostly white faces, vampire fangs,

00:52:48.150 --> 00:52:52.920
green-tinged makeup and excess fur, but their
kindness was encouraging.

00:52:52.920 --> 00:52:57.299
Meanwhile, the haunt actors were gossiping
amongst themselves, wondering where the new

00:52:57.299 --> 00:52:59.349
plague doctor character came from.

00:52:59.349 --> 00:53:00.730
Who approved it?

00:53:00.730 --> 00:53:01.870
Who hired this guy?

00:53:01.870 --> 00:53:03.829
Why was he doing that accent?

00:53:03.829 --> 00:53:11.329
Whatever, he was plenty scary and a nice enough
guy to work with, so Frankenstein's Monster

00:53:11.329 --> 00:53:15.990
let the Plague Doctor have the laboratory
set, and moved himself into the graveyard.

00:53:15.990 --> 00:53:20.890
The customers loved him too, shrieking with
delight as he welcomed them into his laboratory,

00:53:20.890 --> 00:53:23.980
asking if any of them would like to volunteer
to be a test subject.

00:53:23.980 --> 00:53:28.181
Lucky for them, they had already been warned
not to touch any of the "actors" while walking

00:53:28.181 --> 00:53:29.710
through the attraction.

00:53:29.710 --> 00:53:34.559
As his first night at his unintentional job
came to a close, the Plague Doctor's new friends

00:53:34.559 --> 00:53:35.559
bid him farewell.

00:53:35.559 --> 00:53:38.570
"We're going to a bar up the road for a couple
drinks!"

00:53:38.570 --> 00:53:40.230
The ghostly bridge said.

00:53:40.230 --> 00:53:41.230
"Wanna come?"

00:53:41.230 --> 00:53:43.099
"Ah, no time for revelry!"

00:53:43.099 --> 00:53:44.180
The Plague Doctor replied.

00:53:44.180 --> 00:53:45.990
"I must attend to my work.

00:53:45.990 --> 00:53:46.990
Take care!"

00:53:46.990 --> 00:53:48.120
The woman just laughed.

00:53:48.120 --> 00:53:49.569
"Okay, see you later."

00:53:49.569 --> 00:53:53.580
And so the Plague Doctor spent his night in
the haunted house again, tinkering with his

00:53:53.580 --> 00:53:59.190
tools and his tissue samples, listening to
the royalty-free scary music tape play again

00:53:59.190 --> 00:54:01.920
and again on repeat, into the night.

00:54:01.920 --> 00:54:03.090
Day 7.

00:54:03.090 --> 00:54:06.410
The next day, attendance at the haunted house
was slow.

00:54:06.410 --> 00:54:10.440
The actors, with little to do, spent their
time scrolling aimlessly on their phones,

00:54:10.440 --> 00:54:14.250
filming joke videos for social media, and
pranking each other.

00:54:14.250 --> 00:54:17.990
One of them got the bright idea to pull a
prank on the new hire, the stranger that none

00:54:17.990 --> 00:54:19.710
of them knew much about.

00:54:19.710 --> 00:54:23.420
He would have a friend, turn out the lights
in the laboratory, and when the lights were

00:54:23.420 --> 00:54:26.670
turned back on, he would have replaced the
mannequin on the table.

00:54:26.670 --> 00:54:28.920
He couldn't have known what would happen.

00:54:28.920 --> 00:54:33.950
All he wanted to do was startle the new guy,
freak him out with the sight of an unexpected

00:54:33.950 --> 00:54:35.770
werewolf on his operating table.

00:54:35.770 --> 00:54:39.380
And it was effective- sort of.

00:54:39.380 --> 00:54:43.180
The Plague Doctor froze in place when the
lights went out, calling, "Hello?

00:54:43.180 --> 00:54:44.180
What is happening?"

00:54:44.180 --> 00:54:48.210
and when the room was illuminated once more,
he took in the sight of the living being on

00:54:48.210 --> 00:54:49.210
the operating table.

00:54:49.210 --> 00:54:50.210
"My friend!

00:54:50.210 --> 00:54:52.059
You have come to me for treatment?"

00:54:52.059 --> 00:54:55.569
The werewolf started to get up, disappointed
he hadn't managed to elicit a scream, but

00:54:55.569 --> 00:54:59.810
the Plague Doctor reached out a hand to stop
him, and that was curtains for that particular

00:54:59.810 --> 00:55:00.829
actor.

00:55:00.829 --> 00:55:04.490
As soon as the Plague Doctor's gloved hand
made contact with the man in the werewolf

00:55:04.490 --> 00:55:06.460
costume, his heart stopped.

00:55:06.460 --> 00:55:07.720
"Oh dear."

00:55:07.720 --> 00:55:09.080
The Plague Doctor remarked.

00:55:09.080 --> 00:55:10.910
"Do not fear, I have the cure.

00:55:10.910 --> 00:55:14.310
You'll be alright in no time at all"
By the time the rest of the actors came to

00:55:14.310 --> 00:55:18.700
see what had happened to the werewolf, there
was a real zombie in the haunted house.

00:55:18.700 --> 00:55:21.770
Then, it was pure chaos.

00:55:21.770 --> 00:55:26.140
Actors running and screaming, knocking over
props and abandoning their seasonal gig, darting

00:55:26.140 --> 00:55:29.690
between customers as they went, shrieking
"Don't go in there!

00:55:29.690 --> 00:55:30.690
It's real!"

00:55:30.690 --> 00:55:33.839
Naturally, this only made them want to check
it out even more.

00:55:33.839 --> 00:55:38.079
But by the time they got inside, there was
no sign of the Plague Doctor.

00:55:38.079 --> 00:55:44.819
Only one very lost zombie in a werewolf costume,
a few jars broken in all the chaos, and footsteps

00:55:44.819 --> 00:55:48.830
in the dry grass outside, leading out of town.

00:55:48.830 --> 00:55:50.101
Days 8 to 18

00:55:50.101 --> 00:55:54.440
The Plague Doctor spent all of his eighth
day on the run walking, once more.

00:55:54.440 --> 00:55:58.549
He was starting to get used to this, the endless
drudgery of the path to freedom.

00:55:58.549 --> 00:56:03.450
He hated to admit it, but he was almost starting
to miss his cell back at the Foundation, the

00:56:03.450 --> 00:56:08.390
routine of it, the quiet time to work, the
three meals given to him each day even though

00:56:08.390 --> 00:56:10.430
he didn't need to eat to survive.

00:56:10.430 --> 00:56:15.069
He missed food and drink, the simple pleasures
of bread, meat, cheese, fruit.

00:56:15.069 --> 00:56:17.750
A mug of tea, a cup of wine.

00:56:17.750 --> 00:56:20.010
He sighed to himself, wistfully.

00:56:20.010 --> 00:56:23.000
Then, he caught a whiff of something lovely.

00:56:23.000 --> 00:56:26.290
He lifted his beak, taking another deep breath.

00:56:26.290 --> 00:56:30.310
The unmistakable scent of roast pheasant,
hearty and warm.

00:56:30.310 --> 00:56:32.510
Beneath it, something else.

00:56:32.510 --> 00:56:35.569
Mulled wine, spiced and inviting.

00:56:35.569 --> 00:56:39.450
Smells that reminded him of home, of days
long lost.

00:56:39.450 --> 00:56:43.540
He couldn't be certain where it would lead,
or if there would be a seat for him at the

00:56:43.540 --> 00:56:48.210
table, but he couldn't resist following the
heavenly aroma to its source.

00:56:48.210 --> 00:56:52.319
As he drew closer to the smell of food and
merriment, he could hear music.

00:56:52.319 --> 00:56:57.170
The plucking of a lyre, the high trill of
a flute, singing and clapping.

00:56:57.170 --> 00:56:59.500
Then, he finally spotted it.

00:56:59.500 --> 00:57:04.490
Rows of colorful tents, long banquet tables,
minstrels wandering about and playing ballads

00:57:04.490 --> 00:57:06.490
to anyone who would listen.

00:57:06.490 --> 00:57:12.930
Men, women, and children frolicking in elaborate
costumes, plate armor, robes, gowns, and more.

00:57:12.930 --> 00:57:16.869
Though the Plague Doctor didn't know how to
describe what he was seeing, he had wandered

00:57:16.869 --> 00:57:20.820
onto the site of one of the state's largest
Renaissance Faires.

00:57:20.820 --> 00:57:26.010
It just so happened to be opening day, and
he was just in time for the celebratory feast.

00:57:26.010 --> 00:57:27.270
"Good day, Doctor!"

00:57:27.270 --> 00:57:29.849
A nobleman tipped his hat to SCP-049.

00:57:29.849 --> 00:57:32.619
"Are you speaking to me?"

00:57:32.619 --> 00:57:34.730
The Plague Doctor was taken aback by the recognition.

00:57:34.730 --> 00:57:38.250
"Why of course, you're the only doctor I see!

00:57:38.250 --> 00:57:42.730
Her Majesty the Queen thanks you for your
service, for keeping us all safe from the

00:57:42.730 --> 00:57:43.730
great plague!"

00:57:43.730 --> 00:57:47.950
The Plague Doctor had no idea that this man
was an actor, playing along with him in what

00:57:47.950 --> 00:57:50.420
he assumed was a scene for the tourists.

00:57:50.420 --> 00:57:55.180
Instead, he took the words to heart, swelling
with pride and gratitude for the recognition.

00:57:55.180 --> 00:57:59.580
"Good sir, it is my duty and my honor."

00:57:59.580 --> 00:58:01.250
The Plague Doctor nodded politely.

00:58:01.250 --> 00:58:05.830
Luckily for him, and unbeknownst to the rest
of the cast of players at the Faire, the man

00:58:05.830 --> 00:58:09.819
originally hired to play the town Plague Doctor
had booked a commercial role that morning

00:58:09.819 --> 00:58:12.020
and had decided not to go in to work.

00:58:12.020 --> 00:58:17.880
That man's choice not to call and let anyone
know, though rude, led to a fortuitous misunderstanding

00:58:17.880 --> 00:58:18.880
for SCP-049.

00:58:18.880 --> 00:58:22.370
"Please, join us at the Royal Table!"

00:58:22.370 --> 00:58:26.770
The nobleman made a sweeping gesture with
his arm, inviting the doctor to sit and dine

00:58:26.770 --> 00:58:30.089
with him and the rest of the actors playing
members of the court.

00:58:30.089 --> 00:58:31.970
"Thank you for your kindness."

00:58:31.970 --> 00:58:34.450
If he could, the Plague Doctor would have
smiled.

00:58:34.450 --> 00:58:39.690
He dined to his heart's content that afternoon,
listening to sounds of music and laughter.

00:58:39.690 --> 00:58:44.400
He slotted into his role at the Faire even
better than his position at the Haunted House.

00:58:44.400 --> 00:58:48.990
He answered questions from tourists about
his work as a physician, warned them of the

00:58:48.990 --> 00:58:53.210
dangers of the Great Pestilence, and posed
for the occasional photograph.

00:58:53.210 --> 00:58:58.520
He spent a wonderful ten days living in a
stylized, artificial version of the past,

00:58:58.520 --> 00:59:03.240
basking in the warm glow of camaraderie - though
the other actors wondered why he never took

00:59:03.240 --> 00:59:05.430
his mask off, even to eat.

00:59:05.430 --> 00:59:08.740
Then, sadly, it was time for the Faire to
pack up for the season.

00:59:08.740 --> 00:59:13.190
The Plague Doctor bid his new friends farewell,
and declined their offers to exchange numbers

00:59:13.190 --> 00:59:14.230
and keep in touch.

00:59:14.230 --> 00:59:17.890
He would, however, always remember them fondly.

00:59:17.890 --> 00:59:19.770
Days 19-41

00:59:19.770 --> 00:59:23.760
As he was departing the Faire grounds, the
Doctor caught a glimpse of strange men approaching

00:59:23.760 --> 00:59:27.480
some of the actors, showing federal badges
and asking them questions.

00:59:27.480 --> 00:59:32.550
He couldn't be certain that it was the Foundation,
but it was more than enough to make him cautious.

00:59:32.550 --> 00:59:36.880
He would have to retreat into the woods once
more, and stay out of sight.

00:59:36.880 --> 00:59:42.859
He laid low for quite some time, for 22 days
to be exact, but then, on Day 41…

00:59:42.859 --> 00:59:45.410
Day 41 to 52.

00:59:45.410 --> 00:59:47.140
It started to snow.

00:59:47.140 --> 00:59:50.040
White blanketed the forest floor, soft and
cold.

00:59:50.040 --> 00:59:53.290
The weather wouldn't kill him, of course,
but it was uncomfortable.

00:59:53.290 --> 00:59:57.890
He would need to find some more efficient
shelter, to escape from the elements before

00:59:57.890 --> 00:59:59.700
the winter storm worsened.

00:59:59.700 --> 01:00:03.740
He trudged through the snow, into a new town,
small and quaint.

01:00:03.740 --> 01:00:08.050
There was an abandoned church at the edge
of the main road, a bit broken down, but the

01:00:08.050 --> 01:00:12.720
roof held firm against the onslaught of ice
and wind, so the Plague Doctor made it his

01:00:12.720 --> 01:00:17.950
new home for the next 12 days, working quietly
and staying out of sight.

01:00:17.950 --> 01:00:19.980
Day 53 to 57.

01:00:19.980 --> 01:00:24.859
On the eve of the Plague Doctor's 53rd day
on the run from the SCP Foundation, he was

01:00:24.859 --> 01:00:28.690
stirred from his work by the sound of a choir
of beautiful voices outside.

01:00:28.690 --> 01:00:33.970
He looked, to find a group of Christmas Carolers
singing together, the rows of houses along

01:00:33.970 --> 01:00:37.710
the streets covered in colorful lights and
glittering decorations.

01:00:37.710 --> 01:00:42.460
Wanting to get a little closer to the music,
the Doctor left his hiding spot for the first

01:00:42.460 --> 01:00:44.789
time in nearly two weeks.

01:00:44.789 --> 01:00:49.150
But as the choir saw a strangely shaped dark
shadow, emerging from the abandoned church,

01:00:49.150 --> 01:00:52.430
they screamed and scattered in every direction.

01:00:52.430 --> 01:00:56.310
One of them yelled something about "the Krampus"
as they went.

01:00:56.310 --> 01:01:01.940
Frightened by the whole interaction, SCP-049
found himself a new spot, under a bridge.

01:01:01.940 --> 01:01:04.290
It was cold, but isolated.

01:01:04.290 --> 01:01:07.339
At least, it was for five days.

01:01:07.339 --> 01:01:08.339
Day 58.

01:01:08.339 --> 01:01:13.589
On day 58, a group of local teenagers, rowdy
and looking to make some trouble, spotted

01:01:13.589 --> 01:01:15.650
the Plague Doctor squatting under the bridge.

01:01:15.650 --> 01:01:18.470
They pelted him with snowballs, laughing.

01:01:18.470 --> 01:01:23.069
Before he could do anything about it, fortunately
for the teens, they ran back to their car

01:01:23.069 --> 01:01:24.530
and sped away.

01:01:24.530 --> 01:01:29.119
Drenched in ice and cold water, miserable,
and irritated, the Plague Doctor searched

01:01:29.119 --> 01:01:31.140
for another place to stay.

01:01:31.140 --> 01:01:33.480
Day 59 to Day 84

01:01:33.480 --> 01:01:38.250
The Plague Doctor was able to slip into another
relatively quiet building: the local community

01:01:38.250 --> 01:01:43.079
theatre, which happened to be an old opera
house repurposed as the site of volunteer-run

01:01:43.079 --> 01:01:44.079
musicals.

01:01:44.079 --> 01:01:48.630
While the townsfolk began rehearsals for their
production of The Phantom of the Opera, the

01:01:48.630 --> 01:01:52.230
Plague Doctor watched from above, hiding in
the shadows.

01:01:52.230 --> 01:01:56.910
Every so often, the stagehand would catch
a glimpse of a cape, the silhouette of a face,

01:01:56.910 --> 01:02:01.380
a pair of dark eyes, but no one believed her
when she tried to tell them that the Phantom

01:02:01.380 --> 01:02:02.470
was real.

01:02:02.470 --> 01:02:07.630
At least, until a handyman went up into the
rafters to install some lights, and saw the

01:02:07.630 --> 01:02:09.200
Plague Doctor for himself.

01:02:09.200 --> 01:02:12.579
Then, it was time for him to flee to a new
town.

01:02:12.579 --> 01:02:14.900
DAY 85 TO DAY 89

01:02:14.900 --> 01:02:19.760
On Day 85, the Plague Doctor climbed up onto
the roof of a dark house to avoid a particularly

01:02:19.760 --> 01:02:23.559
aggressive dog, and let himself in through
the attic window.

01:02:23.559 --> 01:02:28.070
The next morning, a little girl came up into
the attic in search of a lost toy, and found

01:02:28.070 --> 01:02:29.070
him there.

01:02:29.070 --> 01:02:32.250
He waited for her to scream, but she didn't.

01:02:32.250 --> 01:02:36.450
She introduced herself as Abby, and asked
if he would like some cookies and milk.

01:02:36.450 --> 01:02:41.200
He accepted her gracious offer, and the two
visited with each other for several days,

01:02:41.200 --> 01:02:43.950
until Abby's parents asked who she was talking
to.

01:02:43.950 --> 01:02:48.579
They didn't take it well when she answered,
"The bird man in the attic."

01:02:48.579 --> 01:02:49.579
DAY 90

01:02:49.579 --> 01:02:53.400
On Day 90, Abby's parents called the police,
who, having been warned to look out for a

01:02:53.400 --> 01:02:58.619
man in a "bird mask," got in touch with their
contacts at the SCP Foundation.

01:02:58.619 --> 01:03:03.510
Feeling the authorities closing in, SCP-049
took to the wilderness once more.

01:03:03.510 --> 01:03:05.730
DAY 91 T0 DAY 99

01:03:05.730 --> 01:03:10.470
Though the air was still painfully cold, The
Plague Doctor managed to set up camp, using

01:03:10.470 --> 01:03:14.049
tools from his bag to build a fire and construct
a makeshift tent.

01:03:14.049 --> 01:03:18.599
He stayed there for three days before intending
to move on and throw the Foundation off of

01:03:18.599 --> 01:03:23.150
his trail, but he was slowed by the appearance
of a group of hikers, bundled up against the

01:03:23.150 --> 01:03:25.300
Winter weather and exploring the woods.

01:03:25.300 --> 01:03:29.720
Afraid he couldn't trust them, the Doctor
touched one, to convince him to leave.

01:03:29.720 --> 01:03:34.360
The man fell to the ground, dead, and as his
companions ran away screaming, the Doctor

01:03:34.360 --> 01:03:36.059
vowed to cure him.

01:03:36.059 --> 01:03:40.760
Working against the inopportune conditions,
it took him five more days to reanimate the

01:03:40.760 --> 01:03:41.760
hiker.

01:03:41.760 --> 01:03:42.760
DAY 100

01:03:42.760 --> 01:03:46.309
On Day 100 of his time on the lam, the Foundation
finally closed in.

01:03:46.309 --> 01:03:50.280
The Plague Doctor was just stitching up a
large incision in his patient's chest, when

01:03:50.280 --> 01:03:52.200
he heard the click of a gun behind him.

01:03:52.200 --> 01:03:56.609
"Come with us peacefully, and we won't have
to sedate you," the officer said.

01:03:56.609 --> 01:04:01.000
With a heavy sigh, the Plague Doctor held
up his hands, stepping back from his patient

01:04:01.000 --> 01:04:05.770
just as its undead body sat up, blinking its
unseeing eyes.

01:04:05.770 --> 01:04:06.869
"Very well.

01:04:06.869 --> 01:04:09.079
I will go back.

01:04:09.079 --> 01:04:13.420
To tell you the truth, gentlemen, this has
all been a bit too much trouble.

01:04:13.420 --> 01:04:15.440
I could use the rest."

01:04:15.440 --> 01:04:21.579
Somewhere, caught between parallel dimensions,
is the containment breach to end all containment

01:04:21.579 --> 01:04:22.710
breaches.

01:04:22.710 --> 01:04:27.160
Countless unnumbered and nameless anomalies
on a warpath to destroy what has become of

01:04:27.160 --> 01:04:29.809
a now-unrepentantly evil Foundation.

01:04:29.809 --> 01:04:35.160
A testing site rendered distorted and unstable
under the power of countless reality-warping

01:04:35.160 --> 01:04:39.180
objects, and me caught in the middle of it.

01:04:39.180 --> 01:04:41.480
You’re probably wondering how I got here.

01:04:41.480 --> 01:04:45.250
Well, for starters, I’m what is known as
an anomaly.

01:04:45.250 --> 01:04:48.130
If I can see something, I can crush it, using
my brain.

01:04:48.130 --> 01:04:53.490
I guess you’d say I have a form of telekinesis,
but fine control or remote levitation are

01:04:53.490 --> 01:04:54.799
not my forte.

01:04:54.799 --> 01:05:00.750
I’m not sure who I was before the GOC apprehended
and brought me here to this strange place.

01:05:00.750 --> 01:05:06.210
A place ruled by a cruel man named Eliott
Emerson and the even crueler Foundation that

01:05:06.210 --> 01:05:07.339
he directed.

01:05:07.339 --> 01:05:13.779
A place called Site-13, and as for the rest
of us contained here, we don’t get numbers.

01:05:13.779 --> 01:05:17.040
The only name I have is the one I gave myself.

01:05:17.040 --> 01:05:22.210
If I'm destined to be trapped in this infernal
place by a man named Emerson, I may as well

01:05:22.210 --> 01:05:25.670
self-identify as his equal and opposite.

01:05:25.670 --> 01:05:31.869
You can call me Thoreau, and this is the story
of how I managed to survive 100 days in Site-13…

01:05:31.869 --> 01:05:34.789
well, after everything went haywire.

01:05:34.789 --> 01:05:36.740
Here goes nothing…

01:05:36.740 --> 01:05:38.039
Day 1.

01:05:38.039 --> 01:05:42.380
Like I said before, this all began with the
containment breach to end all containment

01:05:42.380 --> 01:05:43.380
breaches.

01:05:43.380 --> 01:05:48.020
It is chaos in the hallways, with anomalies
running rampant everywhere.

01:05:48.020 --> 01:05:52.710
Most are taking revenge on the guards and
researchers, but not every one of the unnumbered

01:05:52.710 --> 01:05:54.410
is in solidarity.

01:05:54.410 --> 01:05:58.770
The crystal butterflies and the memetic scribe
are indiscriminately going after anything

01:05:58.770 --> 01:06:01.930
that isn’t them, including each other.

01:06:01.930 --> 01:06:05.130
As for me, I’m wearing a specialized helmet
that keeps me blindfolded.

01:06:05.130 --> 01:06:10.650
It’s a power suppressant, since my anomalous
ability only works on things I can see clearly.

01:06:10.650 --> 01:06:15.569
I’d love to remove it, but there’s no
way to find out which guard has clearance

01:06:15.569 --> 01:06:19.260
to deactivate my headgear when things are
this manic.

01:06:19.260 --> 01:06:24.099
As much as I’d love to celebrate the reckoning
of Site-13, I decided it was better to stay

01:06:24.099 --> 01:06:26.620
inside my cell for the day.

01:06:26.620 --> 01:06:31.369
Let the record show that if The Foundation
or the GOC do crack down on this, I was well-behaved

01:06:31.369 --> 01:06:32.480
during the breach.

01:06:32.480 --> 01:06:33.560
Day 2.

01:06:33.560 --> 01:06:36.119
It’s gotten marginally quieter out there.

01:06:36.119 --> 01:06:40.010
I don’t think the fighting has stopped as
much as it has moved out of the halls where

01:06:40.010 --> 01:06:41.529
I’ve been contained.

01:06:41.529 --> 01:06:45.750
The Foundation hasn’t dropped off any more
rations today, so I start to think that maybe

01:06:45.750 --> 01:06:47.809
this really was the big one.

01:06:47.809 --> 01:06:50.270
What if everyone somehow got out?

01:06:50.270 --> 01:06:54.510
I started to wonder about some of my fellow
anomalous humanoids and if the containment

01:06:54.510 --> 01:06:56.670
breach had given them a chance.

01:06:56.670 --> 01:07:00.529
I don’t really know what they look like
because most of the time, I’m forced to

01:07:00.529 --> 01:07:05.289
wear a blindfold to suppress my powers, but
I have been able to pick up a few hints through

01:07:05.289 --> 01:07:06.289
listening.

01:07:06.289 --> 01:07:09.740
There’s a clown named Bobble, he was already
here when I arrived.

01:07:09.740 --> 01:07:14.099
And no, he’s not a guy dressed as a clown,
but like a genuine actual clown.

01:07:14.099 --> 01:07:20.440
He’s nice enough, but I get the impression
he’d be a bad influence on kids if there

01:07:20.440 --> 01:07:21.960
were any here.

01:07:21.960 --> 01:07:23.340
Other than Elijah, of course.

01:07:23.340 --> 01:07:27.750
He’s known as the Leech Boy since he can
drain the blood from people with his hands.

01:07:27.750 --> 01:07:29.490
Scary stuff, really.

01:07:29.490 --> 01:07:33.250
I don’t dislike either of them, but I’d
rather not take my chances running into them

01:07:33.250 --> 01:07:35.140
during a containment breach.

01:07:35.140 --> 01:07:37.700
I guess I'll wait this one out a bit longer.

01:07:37.700 --> 01:07:42.660
I had water from the containment unit sink
if I needed to sate my thirst and a day without

01:07:42.660 --> 01:07:45.180
a meal wasn’t exactly something new.

01:07:45.180 --> 01:07:46.310
Day 3.

01:07:46.310 --> 01:07:50.819
I felt a strange rumbling in the air this
morning, like everything in the entire universe

01:07:50.819 --> 01:07:53.450
was picked up and dropped back down somewhere
else.

01:07:53.450 --> 01:07:56.020
I don’t know how else to describe it.

01:07:56.020 --> 01:08:00.299
I once heard some of the guards talking about
a reality-bending machine called the Thresher

01:08:00.299 --> 01:08:01.440
on the lower levels.

01:08:01.440 --> 01:08:03.780
Could it be the work of that thing?

01:08:03.780 --> 01:08:04.780
Day 4.

01:08:04.780 --> 01:08:07.170
My old friend Bobble The Clown actually came
through for me.

01:08:07.170 --> 01:08:12.369
He has taken up residence in the digital interface
of my cell and is giving me details of what

01:08:12.369 --> 01:08:13.570
is happening out there.

01:08:13.570 --> 01:08:18.219
The funniest thing about this clown is that
he lives inside of electronic devices.

01:08:18.219 --> 01:08:22.870
Bobble was never threatened by my abilities
because they only work on the physical world.

01:08:22.870 --> 01:08:27.190
The most I could do to him is crush a television
set where he was dwelling.

01:08:27.190 --> 01:08:31.810
Our friendship is based on the fact that the
two of us are among the only things in Site-13

01:08:31.810 --> 01:08:34.279
that couldn’t cause harm to each other.

01:08:34.279 --> 01:08:38.589
And sadly, I think that my list of enemies
is much higher than Bobble’s.

01:08:38.589 --> 01:08:41.910
And a lot of the things out there might hold
a grudge.

01:08:41.910 --> 01:08:45.970
The reason I haven’t been thrown into the
fire is that my matter compression ability

01:08:45.970 --> 01:08:48.020
is useful to the Foundation.

01:08:48.020 --> 01:08:52.279
I’m not proud to admit it, but I’ve agreed
multiple times to use my abilities to bring

01:08:52.279 --> 01:08:58.620
some of my fellow anomalies, particularly
the larger ones, down a manageable size for

01:08:58.620 --> 01:08:59.620
disposal.

01:08:59.620 --> 01:09:04.310
My reward for this is that I’m not subject
to some of the more deadly experiments here,

01:09:04.310 --> 01:09:08.830
though it's not like these evil, unethical
scientists can resist some light torture.

01:09:08.830 --> 01:09:12.799
Did I mention how much these people hate anomalies?

01:09:12.799 --> 01:09:15.620
Because make no mistake, they hate us a lot.

01:09:15.620 --> 01:09:20.230
Bobble said he couldn’t stick around, but
that there was an untouched supply of rations

01:09:20.230 --> 01:09:24.970
a few cells down that I – as an organism
that needs to eat to survive – should go

01:09:24.970 --> 01:09:26.330
and find.

01:09:26.330 --> 01:09:28.989
Even with the blindfold it shouldn’t be
too easy for me to get there.

01:09:28.989 --> 01:09:31.670
I’ll think about going tomorrow.

01:09:31.670 --> 01:09:32.739
Day 4.

01:09:32.739 --> 01:09:37.219
As I felt my way through the hallway to the
other cell, I could tell that things had gone

01:09:37.219 --> 01:09:38.779
really off the deep end.

01:09:38.779 --> 01:09:44.009
There were people fused into the walls, no
doubt because of one of the other anomalies,

01:09:44.009 --> 01:09:47.730
and I had no idea whether or not they were
on my side.

01:09:47.730 --> 01:09:52.060
There was no doubt that memetic cognitohazards
were scrawled all over the surfaces of the

01:09:52.060 --> 01:09:53.699
facility as well.

01:09:53.699 --> 01:09:58.560
If The Scribe had truly been on the loose,
he’d definitely have made those brain-destroying

01:09:58.560 --> 01:10:00.600
symbols he always did.

01:10:00.600 --> 01:10:04.240
Maybe my current lack of vision was a good
thing.

01:10:04.240 --> 01:10:09.630
Especially because spending so much of my
time blind had enhanced my sense of smell.

01:10:09.630 --> 01:10:15.340
From the inside of a nearby open cell, I caught
wind of a tray of day-old Foundation rations.

01:10:15.340 --> 01:10:20.949
And what luck, they were the same kind usually
given to humanoid anomalies like me.

01:10:20.949 --> 01:10:23.350
These must have been the ones that Bobble
had mentioned.

01:10:23.350 --> 01:10:28.510
I settled into this new cell and enjoyed my
first full meal in days.

01:10:28.510 --> 01:10:29.640
Day 5

01:10:29.640 --> 01:10:34.170
With the power-suppressing device still firmly
on my head, getting out of here seemed like

01:10:34.170 --> 01:10:35.170
a pipe dream.

01:10:35.170 --> 01:10:40.310
I spent the day thinking about the only other
person in Site-13 who was kind to me: Dr.

01:10:40.310 --> 01:10:41.310
Hadley.

01:10:41.310 --> 01:10:44.690
She was a researcher who took care of some
of the humanoid anomalies.

01:10:44.690 --> 01:10:48.469
Even though she’s working with The Foundation,
I get the feeling that she’s actually a

01:10:48.469 --> 01:10:50.920
really good person deep down.

01:10:50.920 --> 01:10:55.409
Maybe it's the Stockholm Syndrome talking,
but I really wish I could see her face.

01:10:55.409 --> 01:11:00.500
She always had sympathy for Elijah - the leech
boy - and saw him as an ordinary kid despite

01:11:00.500 --> 01:11:02.310
his blood-draining powers.

01:11:02.310 --> 01:11:05.540
I can only hope that both of them are safe.

01:11:05.540 --> 01:11:06.610
Day 6.

01:11:06.610 --> 01:11:11.710
I continued to make my way across the facility
hugging the wall as tightly as I could.

01:11:11.710 --> 01:11:16.100
I could hear weapon fire throughout the facility
and countless anomalies battling it out.

01:11:16.100 --> 01:11:21.210
All of a sudden, I was caught off guard when
I heard several footsteps coming towards me.

01:11:21.210 --> 01:11:23.750
These must have been foundation personnel,
I thought.

01:11:23.750 --> 01:11:29.360
A shot rang out, and I was hit directly in
my headgear by some kind of piercing round.

01:11:29.360 --> 01:11:33.810
Fortunately, the suppression device was strong
enough to take the hit for me, so I didn’t

01:11:33.810 --> 01:11:40.139
take direct damage, and it was equally fortunate
that being shot at had caused it to malfunction.

01:11:40.139 --> 01:11:45.190
I could now remove the headgear and blindfold
and stare down the group of armed Foundation

01:11:45.190 --> 01:11:46.719
guards in front of me.

01:11:46.719 --> 01:11:47.900
“It’s The Crusher!”

01:11:47.900 --> 01:11:48.900
One of them shouted.

01:11:48.900 --> 01:11:51.560
“My name is Thoreau,” I responded.

01:11:51.560 --> 01:11:56.410
I didn’t give them a chance to fire again,
and using my ability to defend myself was

01:11:56.410 --> 01:11:58.170
the only way out.

01:11:58.170 --> 01:12:01.980
After all the anomalies I had compressed at
their orders, doing the same to Emerson’s

01:12:01.980 --> 01:12:03.460
forces came all too naturally.

01:12:03.460 --> 01:12:07.870
To them, we were merely objects.

01:12:07.870 --> 01:12:10.380
Things to be used up before we’re incinerated.

01:12:10.380 --> 01:12:16.130
Whether we’re human, inhuman, or something
entirely indescribable: It makes no difference

01:12:16.130 --> 01:12:22.139
to Emerson and his underlings, since all of
us are going to the same place.

01:12:22.139 --> 01:12:25.699
After that encounter with the guards, I decided
that I wouldn’t use my powers to harm my

01:12:25.699 --> 01:12:27.290
fellow anomaly.

01:12:27.290 --> 01:12:32.290
We had all endured so much in Site-13 that
it felt needlessly brutal to let the one thing

01:12:32.290 --> 01:12:35.870
that I had in common with the other prisoners
be a weapon against them.

01:12:35.870 --> 01:12:40.219
I couldn’t guarantee that all of the other
anomalies would treat me the same way, but

01:12:40.219 --> 01:12:44.240
the idea of living by that principle filled
me with a sense of purpose.

01:12:44.240 --> 01:12:46.880
From now on, it was me against Site-13.

01:12:46.880 --> 01:12:51.900
I would survive and escape to the outside
world.

01:12:51.900 --> 01:12:53.140
Day 7.

01:12:53.140 --> 01:12:57.150
To my surprise, there was a mundane vending
machine in this terrible place.

01:12:57.150 --> 01:13:02.630
I used my powers to break into it: raking
in the delicious bounty of chips, protein

01:13:02.630 --> 01:13:04.090
bars, and fruit snacks.

01:13:04.090 --> 01:13:08.739
This stash would take care of my sustenance
needs for a while, so I stored them in a military

01:13:08.739 --> 01:13:11.910
pack I had taken from one of the guards I
had fought with the other day.

01:13:11.910 --> 01:13:12.910
Day 8.

01:13:12.910 --> 01:13:16.790
I began to realize that the rooms I was traveling
through were repeating themselves.

01:13:16.790 --> 01:13:22.150
It was hard to really notice at first, with
all the cognitohazards I was trying to avoid,

01:13:22.150 --> 01:13:26.900
but it seemed like Site-13 was reshuffling
itself in a way that had made my progress

01:13:26.900 --> 01:13:28.860
through it completely circuitous.

01:13:28.860 --> 01:13:34.260
While this meant that my encounters with dangerous
anomalies and guards had been basically nonexistent,

01:13:34.260 --> 01:13:37.850
it also meant that I wasn’t making any progress
towards escape.

01:13:37.850 --> 01:13:38.969
Day 9-14.

01:13:38.969 --> 01:13:43.449
Yup, I’m officially stuck in a loop now.

01:13:43.449 --> 01:13:49.110
This was probably unavoidable even if I had
a mental map of the facility or even a physical

01:13:49.110 --> 01:13:50.110
map.

01:13:50.110 --> 01:13:53.920
Reality is so broken by the effects of the
Thresher that I don’t even know if the rest

01:13:53.920 --> 01:13:56.590
of Site-13 even still exists.

01:13:56.590 --> 01:14:00.770
I honestly wouldn’t be that upset if Director
Emerson and the rest of the Foundation personnel

01:14:00.770 --> 01:14:03.010
had been flung into oblivion.

01:14:03.010 --> 01:14:07.630
That would be worth it even if I spent the
rest of my days here wandering around and

01:14:07.630 --> 01:14:09.420
eating vending machine food.

01:14:09.420 --> 01:14:10.420
Day 15.

01:14:10.420 --> 01:14:15.199
I gained access to a new area of the facility,
only to find that it was a stairwell filled

01:14:15.199 --> 01:14:18.310
with a terrifying black gooey substance.

01:14:18.310 --> 01:14:22.730
I had no idea what this stuff was, but perhaps
it was best not to touch it.

01:14:22.730 --> 01:14:26.260
Yeah, that would definitely be for the best.

01:14:26.260 --> 01:14:30.179
Still, there were stairs going away from the
goo which could lead me closer to an exit,

01:14:30.179 --> 01:14:33.540
so I decided to take that risk and sprint
up to a higher level.

01:14:33.540 --> 01:14:38.820
As if waiting for a cue, the liquid began
to rise as soon as I set foot in the stairwell.

01:14:38.820 --> 01:14:43.020
I climbed the stairs as fast as I could, but
this stuff was determined.

01:14:43.020 --> 01:14:47.620
I thought I could slow it down by crushing
some of the stairs, but using my powers caused

01:14:47.620 --> 01:14:50.220
some of the substance to splash onto my hand.

01:14:50.220 --> 01:14:52.342
It didn’t hurt, but now my hand had a hole
directly through the palm.

01:14:52.342 --> 01:14:53.342
I could still feel everything, but the affected
area was now see-through.

01:14:53.342 --> 01:14:54.370
Freaky, but it definitely could have been
worse.

01:14:54.370 --> 01:14:59.000
I made out of the stairwell otherwise intact,
and thankfully the new floor I found myself

01:14:59.000 --> 01:15:01.730
on wasn’t anywhere I had been before.

01:15:01.730 --> 01:15:07.610
There were giant leeches everywhere, crawling
through cracks in the wall.

01:15:07.610 --> 01:15:08.610
Did Elijah do this?

01:15:08.610 --> 01:15:09.610
Day 16-27.

01:15:09.610 --> 01:15:13.850
I continued making my way through the unexplored
sections, encountering a lot of the remains

01:15:13.850 --> 01:15:17.980
of dead guards but no fellow anomalies besides
the leeches.

01:15:17.980 --> 01:15:22.100
I made a point of not getting too close to
them, and they didn’t seem to bother me

01:15:22.100 --> 01:15:23.100
either.

01:15:23.100 --> 01:15:26.940
Most of the leeches seemed content to drink
the blood from the corpses of which there

01:15:26.940 --> 01:15:27.940
were plenty.

01:15:27.940 --> 01:15:31.909
There were fewer cognitohazards, and I was
grateful for that.

01:15:31.909 --> 01:15:37.179
I managed to find and crush a few more vending
machines to restock my food supplies.

01:15:37.179 --> 01:15:41.280
Compared to the rations I had been fed since
I was first brought here, these snacks practically

01:15:41.280 --> 01:15:43.080
tasted like gourmet food.

01:15:43.080 --> 01:15:44.739
On day 28.

01:15:44.739 --> 01:15:48.389
Bobble found me again, or rather I found him.

01:15:48.389 --> 01:15:52.190
He was inside of a laptop in one of the abandoned
research offices.

01:15:52.190 --> 01:15:56.620
We compared notes, and it gave me some idea
of what happened that caused so many of us

01:15:56.620 --> 01:15:57.620
to escape.

01:15:57.620 --> 01:16:02.540
Apparently, Dr. Hadley had stood up to Emerson
and sabotaged the incinerator.

01:16:02.540 --> 01:16:04.710
I guess she truly was a good person.

01:16:04.710 --> 01:16:09.790
I’ll have to thank her personally if I happen
to find her still alive somewhere in all of

01:16:09.790 --> 01:16:10.790
this.

01:16:10.790 --> 01:16:14.601
It could have also been her who activated
the Thresher machine, but I wouldn’t put

01:16:14.601 --> 01:16:16.960
it past Emerson to do the same.

01:16:16.960 --> 01:16:20.750
Of course, he’d have done so in an effort
to take the rest of us with him.

01:16:20.750 --> 01:16:25.770
Isn’t it remarkable that the people we think
we can trust and the people who hold us prisoner

01:16:25.770 --> 01:16:29.620
might wind up taking the same action for completely
different reasons?

01:16:29.620 --> 01:16:35.230
It could be that my embarrassing little crush
on Dr. Hadley was stopping me from seeing

01:16:35.230 --> 01:16:36.540
the bigger picture.

01:16:36.540 --> 01:16:39.580
In any case, I thanked Bobble and pressed
onward.

01:16:39.580 --> 01:16:40.580
Day 29-39.

01:16:40.580 --> 01:16:46.889
While the reshuffling of the hallways and
rooms has considerably slowed down my exploration

01:16:46.889 --> 01:16:51.890
of Site-13, I can safely say that I’m making
progress towards finding some kind of way

01:16:51.890 --> 01:16:52.890
out.

01:16:52.890 --> 01:16:56.110
I’ve come to realize that the further I
get from the lower levels where the Thresher

01:16:56.110 --> 01:17:01.310
is at its most powerful, the more stable the
facility’s layout becomes.

01:17:01.310 --> 01:17:04.750
If I can make it to the rooftop, then I’m
as good as free.

01:17:04.750 --> 01:17:08.739
So my guiding philosophy has become “onwards
and upwards.”

01:17:08.739 --> 01:17:10.080
Day 40.

01:17:10.080 --> 01:17:12.929
Now, this is unexpected!

01:17:12.929 --> 01:17:16.960
I was confronted by another group of armed
Foundation personnel – different uniforms

01:17:16.960 --> 01:17:22.830
- one of the Mobile Task Forces, I think – but
instead of shooting me, they just started

01:17:22.830 --> 01:17:24.340
asking questions.

01:17:24.340 --> 01:17:25.850
The most confusing of which was…

01:17:25.850 --> 01:17:30.050
“What is your object class and SCP designation
number?”

01:17:30.050 --> 01:17:32.610
I had no idea how to answer.

01:17:32.610 --> 01:17:35.739
Were things that different in the other containment
sites?

01:17:35.739 --> 01:17:41.190
Site-13 had never given us numbers or formal
classifications beyond simple nicknames derived

01:17:41.190 --> 01:17:42.720
from our anomalous properties.

01:17:42.720 --> 01:17:48.320
I wasn’t sure what was going on, but I also
didn’t want to risk getting put back into

01:17:48.320 --> 01:17:49.320
containment.

01:17:49.320 --> 01:17:52.889
I couldn’t be sure this wasn’t some kind
of foundation trick or I hadn’t somehow

01:17:52.889 --> 01:17:57.200
lost my mind because of some cognitohazard
I’d accidentally glimpsed.

01:17:57.200 --> 01:18:00.100
I crushed a doorway using my powers and ran
for it.

01:18:00.100 --> 01:18:05.910
A few shots echoed behind me, but the apparent
leader of the mobile task force called for

01:18:05.910 --> 01:18:06.912
them to hold their fire.

01:18:06.912 --> 01:18:10.780
Whoever these people were, they definitely
weren’t with Emerson.

01:18:10.780 --> 01:18:12.180
Day 41-52.

01:18:12.180 --> 01:18:17.280
If the leeches, repeating staircases, and
occasional hostile anomalies weren’t enough,

01:18:17.280 --> 01:18:22.090
now I have to deal with these alternative
Foundation personnel poking around here.

01:18:22.090 --> 01:18:27.210
It was one thing to protect myself from Site-13’s
dedicated guards, since they’d been responsible

01:18:27.210 --> 01:18:31.989
for holding me captive, but my conscience
was telling me not to attack these new mobile

01:18:31.989 --> 01:18:33.280
task agents.

01:18:33.280 --> 01:18:39.070
Instead, I hid from them as well as I could,
running to another area whenever I was discovered.

01:18:39.070 --> 01:18:43.010
I can’t remember where I heard it from,
but I know there’s some old saying about

01:18:43.010 --> 01:18:45.920
great power and great responsibility.

01:18:45.920 --> 01:18:50.400
Point is, I didn’t want to have the responsibility
of having used my powers to hurt people who

01:18:50.400 --> 01:18:51.800
didn’t deserve it.

01:18:51.800 --> 01:18:55.570
Still, it seemed like many of the anomalies
didn’t feel the same way.

01:18:55.570 --> 01:18:59.790
From what I observed during my circuitous
route around the facility, I could tell that

01:18:59.790 --> 01:19:03.030
these mobile task force agents were suffering
losses.

01:19:03.030 --> 01:19:04.930
Day 53.

01:19:04.930 --> 01:19:11.139
On Day 53, I found a lone agent huddled in
a fetal position against the wall of one of

01:19:11.139 --> 01:19:12.469
the containment corridors.

01:19:12.469 --> 01:19:17.190
It seemed that no other members of their squad
were present, so either they had been separated

01:19:17.190 --> 01:19:20.570
in the madness of Site-13, or they were the
sole survivor.

01:19:20.570 --> 01:19:25.290
Either way, I felt sympathetic, so I approached
them in an effort to communicate.

01:19:25.290 --> 01:19:29.500
What I learned during this conversation absolutely
blew my mind.

01:19:29.500 --> 01:19:34.850
It turned out that the Thresher activation
had caused Site-13 to literally jump universes

01:19:34.850 --> 01:19:40.360
and wind up in a timeline where the Foundation
and the GOC had never joined forces.

01:19:40.360 --> 01:19:45.540
In this reality, The Foundation was a comparatively
benevolent organization that only sought to

01:19:45.540 --> 01:19:48.200
secure, contain, and protect anomalies.

01:19:48.200 --> 01:19:53.210
It rarely destroyed anomalous beings unless
those beings posed an existential threat to

01:19:53.210 --> 01:19:54.260
other life.

01:19:54.260 --> 01:19:59.790
According to the agent I spoke to, if a humanoid
anomaly like me were to be contained by this

01:19:59.790 --> 01:20:04.750
version of the organization, I’d most likely
be given free room and board and three meals

01:20:04.750 --> 01:20:05.830
a day.

01:20:05.830 --> 01:20:10.450
Even if freedom was my ultimate goal, I couldn’t
say that life didn’t tempt me.

01:20:10.450 --> 01:20:15.100
I agreed to help this Foundation agent find
the rest of their team, as long as they were

01:20:15.100 --> 01:20:17.450
telling the truth.

01:20:17.450 --> 01:20:18.650
Day 54-67.

01:20:18.650 --> 01:20:23.100
The mobile task force agent and I worked together
to search the facility for other Foundation

01:20:23.100 --> 01:20:27.410
personnel - not ones on the payroll of Site-13,
I should clarify.

01:20:27.410 --> 01:20:32.150
It was nice having someone to talk to, even
if they recoiled in fear when I mentioned

01:20:32.150 --> 01:20:34.150
my best friend was a clown.

01:20:34.150 --> 01:20:38.580
I guess a lot of people in this dimension
have an irrational fear of clowns.

01:20:38.580 --> 01:20:43.290
By the way, my new friend said that their
name was Agent Ben Mcdowell, and apparently

01:20:43.290 --> 01:20:47.929
Site-13 was the most dangerous site that their
task force had ever been deployed into.

01:20:47.929 --> 01:20:52.440
I couldn’t say that I wasn’t honored,
especially after all this place had put me

01:20:52.440 --> 01:20:53.440
through.

01:20:53.440 --> 01:20:57.030
But the nightmare was far from over because
on Day 68…

01:20:57.030 --> 01:21:00.050
Oh god, Day 68.

01:21:00.050 --> 01:21:03.510
… the leeches surrounded us and were out
for blood.

01:21:03.510 --> 01:21:07.389
Agent Mcdowell had only limited ammunition,
and I was still incredibly hesitant to use

01:21:07.389 --> 01:21:09.610
my powers on other anomalies.

01:21:09.610 --> 01:21:14.520
Of course, my resolve was about to get pushed
to its breaking point as an enormous mutated

01:21:14.520 --> 01:21:16.650
leech arrived at our location.

01:21:16.650 --> 01:21:21.070
It was biggest anomaly I’d ever seen and
yet there was something uncannily familiar

01:21:21.070 --> 01:21:22.150
about it.

01:21:22.150 --> 01:21:23.150
Elijah?

01:21:23.150 --> 01:21:24.330
Could it be?

01:21:24.330 --> 01:21:28.489
The super leech lashed out at Agent McDowell
with its huge tentacles and pulled them into

01:21:28.489 --> 01:21:30.310
its terrible toothy mouth.

01:21:30.310 --> 01:21:32.830
I ran away as fast as I could.

01:21:32.830 --> 01:21:37.560
Should I have felt bad that I had left my
new ally behind with a giant monstrous anomaly?

01:21:37.560 --> 01:21:40.140
Yes, I was only human after all.

01:21:40.140 --> 01:21:44.929
But self-preservation had kicked in at the
time, and my mind hadn’t fully accepted

01:21:44.929 --> 01:21:49.900
that I should be protecting someone dressed
in a Foundation uniform over another anomaly.

01:21:49.900 --> 01:21:52.010
Sorry, Agent Mcdowell.

01:21:52.010 --> 01:21:55.980
At the very least, I won’t forget your kindness.

01:21:55.980 --> 01:21:57.800
Day 69-84.

01:21:57.800 --> 01:22:02.520
I was on my own again, wandering through the
facility as it twisted and changed.

01:22:02.520 --> 01:22:06.730
Trapped in a dimension that wasn’t my own,
with freedom feeling even less possible now

01:22:06.730 --> 01:22:10.370
that I knew there was an entirely different
Foundation after me now.

01:22:10.370 --> 01:22:16.150
I never saw Elijah again - or rather, the
horrific thing that Elijah had become.

01:22:16.150 --> 01:22:20.300
I made a guess, grimly, that Dr. Hadley was
probably gone by now.

01:22:20.300 --> 01:22:24.170
So many lives had been lost because of the
cruelty of Site Director Eliott Emerson.

01:22:24.170 --> 01:22:31.350
If I found him, I would make sure that he
paid for the hell he put us through.

01:22:31.350 --> 01:22:32.350
Day 85.

01:22:32.350 --> 01:22:35.110
On Day 85, I did find him.

01:22:35.110 --> 01:22:40.590
There he was, screaming as he was pulled along
the wheel-like face of a terrifying many-armed

01:22:40.590 --> 01:22:41.590
god.

01:22:41.590 --> 01:22:45.719
I couldn’t stare too long because the wheel
was covered in runes that were cognitohazards,

01:22:45.719 --> 01:22:48.540
but I got a good enough look.

01:22:48.540 --> 01:22:53.230
The man responsible for all of this was now
being tormented himself.

01:22:53.230 --> 01:22:59.430
I wouldn’t have been capable of a better
punishment on my own, so I left him to his

01:22:59.430 --> 01:23:00.430
fate.

01:23:00.430 --> 01:23:01.430
Day 86-100

01:23:01.430 --> 01:23:05.630
It took me fifteen more days of searching
for staircases before I made it to the rooftop

01:23:05.630 --> 01:23:07.010
of site-13.

01:23:07.010 --> 01:23:10.460
When I got there, a Foundation helicopter
was waiting for me.

01:23:10.460 --> 01:23:14.110
And fortunately, it was the foundation of
this new dimension.

01:23:14.110 --> 01:23:19.090
One that would provide food and shelter for
me like a human deserves and never subject

01:23:19.090 --> 01:23:21.670
me to experiments against my will.

01:23:21.670 --> 01:23:26.590
As I watched Site-13 get farther away from
inside the helicopter, a smile crossed my

01:23:26.590 --> 01:23:31.610
face for the first time in 100 days.

01:23:31.610 --> 01:23:32.610
I made it?

01:23:32.610 --> 01:23:33.610
I actually made it?

01:23:33.610 --> 01:23:38.340
I’m still alive, by some miracle – or
maybe by sheer luck – I made it to the end

01:23:38.340 --> 01:23:39.600
of my sentence.

01:23:39.600 --> 01:23:44.420
Over three months of the most horrific work
I’ve ever had to do, with no other alternative.

01:23:44.420 --> 01:23:48.639
But it’s paid off, because any minute now,
they’ll be coming into the dorm to let me

01:23:48.639 --> 01:23:49.639
go.

01:23:49.639 --> 01:23:51.260
I can walk out of here with my head up.

01:23:51.260 --> 01:23:54.510
I can’t wait to get out of these orange
overalls that I’ve had to live in since

01:23:54.510 --> 01:23:55.600
I arrived.

01:23:55.600 --> 01:23:58.480
It seems like so much longer, in all honesty.

01:23:58.480 --> 01:24:01.510
The only way I’ve been able to keep track
of how many days I’ve been stuck here is

01:24:01.510 --> 01:24:04.600
by putting tally marks on the wall next to
my dormitory bed.

01:24:04.600 --> 01:24:10.140
A hundred, I’ve been a member of D-Class
personnel for the SCP Foundation for the last

01:24:10.140 --> 01:24:11.740
one hundred days.

01:24:11.740 --> 01:24:13.170
And here’s how I survived…

01:24:13.170 --> 01:24:16.360
They didn’t waste any time early on; I’ll
give the Foundation that.

01:24:16.360 --> 01:24:19.540
I’d barely been out of my sentencing hearing
for a few days.

01:24:19.540 --> 01:24:22.330
Put on trial and convicted for a crime I didn’t
even commit.

01:24:22.330 --> 01:24:26.340
There had been a string of murders, and someone
who had either been involved or was responsible

01:24:26.340 --> 01:24:27.570
laid the blame on me.

01:24:27.570 --> 01:24:30.489
But whoever they were, they had powerful friends.

01:24:30.489 --> 01:24:34.540
The cops were in on it, planting evidence
to frame me and make it look like I was guilty

01:24:34.540 --> 01:24:36.400
when I was really innocent.

01:24:36.400 --> 01:24:41.540
That’s what landed me in prison, serving
a sentence of twenty-five years to life behind

01:24:41.540 --> 01:24:42.540
bars.

01:24:42.540 --> 01:24:45.810
And it was during my first week of incarceration
that he showed up.

01:24:45.810 --> 01:24:50.500
The recruitment specialist, a clean-cut, blunt,
Agent Smith type came to visit me.

01:24:50.500 --> 01:24:54.060
I hadn’t even been in prison long enough
to have visitor privileges, but the shady

01:24:54.060 --> 01:24:57.780
agent seemed to know which strings to pull
in order to ask me an important question:

01:24:57.780 --> 01:25:01.380
“What would you do to get out of here?”
he’d said.

01:25:01.380 --> 01:25:03.890
To which my answer was “Anything.”

01:25:03.890 --> 01:25:05.610
That was my first mistake.

01:25:05.610 --> 01:25:10.080
Immediately, the next day I received a letter
detailing more about what exactly Agent Smith

01:25:10.080 --> 01:25:11.080
was offering.

01:25:11.080 --> 01:25:15.250
It was folded and hidden in the spine of a
book that a guard handed me during reading

01:25:15.250 --> 01:25:16.250
hour.

01:25:16.250 --> 01:25:20.210
Unfolding the message, it all felt like an
old-school covert spy tactic.

01:25:20.210 --> 01:25:23.350
“To Mr Emil Carker,” the letter read.

01:25:23.350 --> 01:25:27.000
“We understand that you recently received
a criminal conviction.

01:25:27.000 --> 01:25:30.510
There are two options currently before you,
as detailed below.

01:25:30.510 --> 01:25:33.980
One: you can serve out the remainder of your
sentence in prison.

01:25:33.980 --> 01:25:37.739
Or two: you can be released into the care
of our organization.

01:25:37.739 --> 01:25:42.409
While with us, you will be helping to further
scientific advancements through hard work.

01:25:42.409 --> 01:25:46.950
We have devised a system that allows convicts
such as yourself to perform a vital role within

01:25:46.950 --> 01:25:51.619
our organization, in return for a reduction
in their prison sentences.

01:25:51.619 --> 01:25:56.199
“We have no interest in or intention of
determining any guilt for the crimes for which

01:25:56.199 --> 01:25:58.230
you are currently convicted.

01:25:58.230 --> 01:26:01.570
We merely seek to present this opportunity
to you.

01:26:01.570 --> 01:26:04.690
We ask that you destroy this letter once you
have finished reading.

01:26:04.690 --> 01:26:09.230
Should you be interested in our offer, then
please recite the phrase ‘it’s a yes’

01:26:09.230 --> 01:26:13.080
to a prison guard with the badge number: 47890.”

01:26:13.080 --> 01:26:17.410
Naturally, as cryptic as an offer like that
was, I didn’t need much time to mull it

01:26:17.410 --> 01:26:18.410
over.

01:26:18.410 --> 01:26:22.020
I was trapped in prison for something I didn’t
do, with no way to appeal for my freedom or

01:26:22.020 --> 01:26:23.290
prove my innocence.

01:26:23.290 --> 01:26:27.869
So, taking up a mysterious job opportunity
from a shadowy group seemed like a much better

01:26:27.869 --> 01:26:29.869
alternative at the time.

01:26:29.869 --> 01:26:33.900
After all, I could either work off my sentence
through employment, or rot away in a cell

01:26:33.900 --> 01:26:36.670
for the next two and half decades at minimum.

01:26:36.670 --> 01:26:39.240
All I had to do next was find the right guard.

01:26:39.240 --> 01:26:42.880
I tried not to make it obvious that I was
eyeing every prison guard’s badge number

01:26:42.880 --> 01:26:47.140
as they stood around, keeping a close eye
on me and the other inmates.

01:26:47.140 --> 01:26:50.960
But it was while out in the yard that I saw
him: number 47890.

01:26:50.960 --> 01:26:53.880
I had gone to bed with a knot in my stomach.

01:26:53.880 --> 01:26:58.330
Hours earlier, I had done exactly as the letter
said and approached the guard, making sure

01:26:58.330 --> 01:27:02.290
I was close enough for him to hear me, but
trying to make it clear that I wasn’t looking

01:27:02.290 --> 01:27:04.070
tor start any trouble either.

01:27:04.070 --> 01:27:06.310
“It’s a yes,” I told him.

01:27:06.310 --> 01:27:11.850
In response, 47890 had furrowed his brow and
scrunched his mustache, seemingly in disgust.

01:27:11.850 --> 01:27:14.250
“Back up, inmate,” he commanded sharply.

01:27:14.250 --> 01:27:17.750
I backed away with my hands raised, confused
as to what had just happened.

01:27:17.750 --> 01:27:21.670
I spent the entire rest of the day thinking
about it; maybe the note had been a prank,

01:27:21.670 --> 01:27:24.920
some kind of initiation, seeing as I was the
newest prisoner.

01:27:24.920 --> 01:27:27.670
But that didn’t explain the agent who had
shown up before.

01:27:27.670 --> 01:27:28.980
What was going on?

01:27:28.980 --> 01:27:32.410
The questions that were spinning around in
my head eventually wore me out.

01:27:32.410 --> 01:27:36.929
And while I slept, that’s when someone put
a bag over my head, drugged me, and snuck

01:27:36.929 --> 01:27:38.040
me out of prison.

01:27:38.040 --> 01:27:42.500
When I woke up, I was somewhere new, a room
filled with other convicts.

01:27:42.500 --> 01:27:45.870
Although not many of them seemed to have come
from the same prison I had been sentenced

01:27:45.870 --> 01:27:50.560
to; plenty were sporting different jumpsuits,
but their gruff demeanors told me all I needed

01:27:50.560 --> 01:27:51.560
to about them.

01:27:51.560 --> 01:27:55.660
I was surrounded by a more violent breed of
criminal, some of the worst of the absolute

01:27:55.660 --> 01:27:56.660
worst.

01:27:56.660 --> 01:28:00.280
The atmosphere in this pen full of murderers
and monsters was so tense that it felt like

01:28:00.280 --> 01:28:03.920
the slightest accidental bump could explode
into a full-blown fight.

01:28:03.920 --> 01:28:08.170
Suddenly, a hatch in the ceiling opened, and
a pile of clothes came thumping down from

01:28:08.170 --> 01:28:09.170
above.

01:28:09.170 --> 01:28:12.659
It was a mass of orange, enough matching overalls
for every inmate in the room.

01:28:12.659 --> 01:28:16.710
Of course, I held back from the initial clamor
some of the others made, grabbing their new

01:28:16.710 --> 01:28:20.260
prison garb, snatching orange overalls from
each other, and arguing.

01:28:20.260 --> 01:28:25.590
When I eventually got mine, I noticed an unfamiliar
logo emblazoned on the front of the uniform.

01:28:25.590 --> 01:28:29.330
After being made to wait a whole day in the
pen, the other prisoners and I were filed

01:28:29.330 --> 01:28:31.889
out of the room by heavily armed security
guards.

01:28:31.889 --> 01:28:36.410
We were all directed towards a hall, nobody
was daring enough to challenge the officers,

01:28:36.410 --> 01:28:38.270
even the more violent among the prisoners.

01:28:38.270 --> 01:28:41.989
I spotted that the guard’s uniforms bore
the same insignia as the overalls we’d been

01:28:41.989 --> 01:28:42.989
made to wear.

01:28:42.989 --> 01:28:47.150
It must’ve been the logo of the mysterious
organization that had offered me employment.

01:28:47.150 --> 01:28:51.980
We were given an orientation talk led by someone
who introduced themselves as a Junior Assistant

01:28:51.980 --> 01:28:52.980
Researcher.

01:28:52.980 --> 01:28:56.969
They explained that our current location was
highly classified, as was the true identity

01:28:56.969 --> 01:28:59.900
of the group that had arranged for us to be
released.

01:28:59.900 --> 01:29:05.290
All we were told was that this was an unspecified
form of research facility, and that we had

01:29:05.290 --> 01:29:09.310
to cooperate with the facility’s staff if
we wanted to secure our release.

01:29:09.310 --> 01:29:12.940
It seemed straightforward enough, although
the Junior Assistant Researcher seemed to

01:29:12.940 --> 01:29:16.119
make a lot of jokes about us dying during
these tests.

01:29:16.119 --> 01:29:18.820
I think they hoped it would alleviate some
of the tension…

01:29:18.820 --> 01:29:20.090
it didn’t.

01:29:20.090 --> 01:29:24.070
After sitting through the orientation talk,
I still had more questions than answers.

01:29:24.070 --> 01:29:28.100
The one thing I had learned was that being
tattooed hurt.

01:29:28.100 --> 01:29:31.980
Exiting the hall, the other prisoners and
I had been directed to get a designation number

01:29:31.980 --> 01:29:34.510
tattooed on our wrist and across our chest.

01:29:34.510 --> 01:29:38.850
When someone had asked why the chest, the
Researcher conducting orientation had answered:

01:29:38.850 --> 01:29:43.230
“Well, in the event of an explosion, it's
most likely that it'll be the largest intact

01:29:43.230 --> 01:29:44.750
chunk of meat left.”

01:29:44.750 --> 01:29:46.969
This was another of their ‘jokes’.

01:29:46.969 --> 01:29:51.159
Still feeling sore as my tattoo healed, I
read the number now permanently inked onto

01:29:51.159 --> 01:29:54.381
my body: D-2152.

01:29:54.381 --> 01:29:57.300
‘D-Class Personnel’ that’s what they
called us now.

01:29:57.300 --> 01:30:00.909
We were directed into a dormitory, a lot less
cramped than my old cell.

01:30:00.909 --> 01:30:05.610
But still, being surrounded by violent criminals
when I knew I didn’t belong there, it felt

01:30:05.610 --> 01:30:07.120
no less isolating.

01:30:07.120 --> 01:30:10.550
One of them, who took the bunk next to mine,
introduced himself to me as ‘Shiv’.

01:30:10.550 --> 01:30:14.301
Worried he might try to kill me, I did my
best to be friendly towards him.

01:30:14.301 --> 01:30:19.969
Shiv and I didn’t exactly bond, but more
sprung up conversations because there was

01:30:19.969 --> 01:30:21.350
little else to do.

01:30:21.350 --> 01:30:25.800
I was still worried about some of the flippant
comments made about us all dying, until he

01:30:25.800 --> 01:30:28.050
pointed out something far stranger.

01:30:28.050 --> 01:30:31.719
“Don’t you wonder why they’re making
us wait?” he asked.

01:30:31.719 --> 01:30:34.930
And the moment he mentioned it, I did start
to wonder about that.

01:30:34.930 --> 01:30:38.600
It seemed odd that this organization had wasted
little time in getting us all here.

01:30:38.600 --> 01:30:43.119
It had only been a week ago that I entered
my prison cell for the first time, now I was

01:30:43.119 --> 01:30:44.119
here.

01:30:44.119 --> 01:30:46.260
“I think it’s their way of telling us,”
Shiv went on.

01:30:46.260 --> 01:30:49.869
“That we aren’t here to be helpful, to
be a workforce for them.

01:30:49.869 --> 01:30:51.780
They’ve brought us all here to die.”

01:30:51.780 --> 01:30:56.280
That comment made me look around, and actually
acknowledge the people I was trapped with.

01:30:56.280 --> 01:30:59.980
They were some of the worst criminals imaginable,
irredeemable killers.

01:30:59.980 --> 01:31:04.150
Would this organization really let those people
back out into the world after helping out

01:31:04.150 --> 01:31:05.570
with a few tests?

01:31:05.570 --> 01:31:10.389
I’d soon find out, because Day 8 was the
last day before I found myself working directly

01:31:10.389 --> 01:31:11.940
for the SCP Foundation.

01:31:11.940 --> 01:31:17.580
I picked up the name from a few places around
the facility; nametags, lab coats, and security

01:31:17.580 --> 01:31:19.480
uniforms, always with the same logo.

01:31:19.480 --> 01:31:23.890
Me and the other D-Class personnel were woken
up early in the morning by a bell and issued

01:31:23.890 --> 01:31:24.890
with assignments.

01:31:24.890 --> 01:31:29.239
From the very first day the work started,
I noticed that not all the others came back

01:31:29.239 --> 01:31:30.770
to the dorm at the end of the day.

01:31:30.770 --> 01:31:32.270
Shiv was one of those.

01:31:32.270 --> 01:31:36.630
My tasks mostly consisted of cleaning up with
a mop, hosing down empty testing chambers

01:31:36.630 --> 01:31:40.660
that had a worrying amount of blood sprayed
over the walls and floors.

01:31:40.660 --> 01:31:44.719
The regiments were strict, and I made sure
to do exactly what was asked of me to avoid

01:31:44.719 --> 01:31:46.110
causing any trouble.

01:31:46.110 --> 01:31:50.739
It felt a lot like being back in prison, but
with the only added caveat of being able to

01:31:50.739 --> 01:31:55.889
move around the facility, even if it was to
go and wipe up the Foundation’s mess.

01:31:55.889 --> 01:32:00.510
Although the other classes of personnel didn’t
seem to take kindly to my help, making snide

01:32:00.510 --> 01:32:02.730
comments as they passed me in the hallways.

01:32:02.730 --> 01:32:07.030
We had been told not to address the other
staff unless spoken to, which was frustrating.

01:32:07.030 --> 01:32:09.910
I certainly had a few things I would’ve
liked to say to them.

01:32:09.910 --> 01:32:14.010
After two and a half weeks without so much
as a hint of any trouble, I was given my first

01:32:14.010 --> 01:32:16.520
testing assignment that wasn’t just cleaning
up.

01:32:16.520 --> 01:32:19.780
You would think that would be exciting, but
having seen some of the carnage left behind

01:32:19.780 --> 01:32:23.960
after other tests, I was anything but eager
to take part.

01:32:23.960 --> 01:32:28.790
Luckily for me, and pretty surprisingly, my
first test was pretty straightforward.

01:32:28.790 --> 01:32:33.871
I was shown to a room containing a white bowl
decorated with light blue flowers that the

01:32:33.871 --> 01:32:36.930
researchers referred to as SCP-348.

01:32:36.930 --> 01:32:41.050
I had received a nasty splinter from my mop’s
handle the day before, and was instructed

01:32:41.050 --> 01:32:44.469
to sit down and eat from the bowl, which was
filled with soup.

01:32:44.469 --> 01:32:47.739
Naturally, I eagerly ate it all, despite how
bitter it tasted.

01:32:47.739 --> 01:32:50.600
It was better than the food from the D-Class
canteen at least.

01:32:50.600 --> 01:32:55.639
But when I finished, I noticed a message had
appeared at the bottom of SCP-348.

01:32:55.639 --> 01:32:58.100
It read:
“I don’t believe you were framed.

01:32:58.100 --> 01:32:59.380
Goodbye, son.”

01:32:59.380 --> 01:33:03.110
After that, it was as if the floodgates had
been opened, and every day I was asked to

01:33:03.110 --> 01:33:05.470
do another test with a new SCP.

01:33:05.470 --> 01:33:09.360
Most were harmless anomalous creatures or
objects, like one of the bigger experiments

01:33:09.360 --> 01:33:15.550
I was involved in, which focused on SCP-999,
this friendly gelatinous orange blob.

01:33:15.550 --> 01:33:20.380
Electrodes were hooked up to me while I just
sat calmly in a room with SCP-999.

01:33:20.380 --> 01:33:25.310
Being around it made me feel great, the best
I had felt since arriving and joining D-Class.

01:33:25.310 --> 01:33:30.409
Although I did hear one of the researchers
making the comment that SCP-999 was ‘not

01:33:30.409 --> 01:33:33.350
quite ready yet’.

01:33:33.350 --> 01:33:37.620
Even so, the Tickle Monster had given me a
new positive outlook on my role at the Foundation.

01:33:37.620 --> 01:33:42.370
It was odd work, but I could find a rhythm
to it that would hopefully make the time pass

01:33:42.370 --> 01:33:43.770
by a little quicker.

01:33:43.770 --> 01:33:47.290
And if it meant getting out, it was worth
all the dirty looks from other classes of

01:33:47.290 --> 01:33:48.510
personnel.

01:33:48.510 --> 01:33:51.150
This was an outlook I didn’t want to lose.

01:33:51.150 --> 01:33:54.780
After a month, we were told to take a pill
that the guards handed out to us, something

01:33:54.780 --> 01:33:58.620
called an amnestic that would make us all
forget the previous month.

01:33:58.620 --> 01:34:03.710
But, wanting to hang on to my newfound optimism,
I secretly flushed mine down a toilet.

01:34:03.710 --> 01:34:05.929
In hindsight, I should have taken it.

01:34:05.929 --> 01:34:08.350
My next big test quickly brought me back down
a peg.

01:34:08.350 --> 01:34:13.780
I was handed a katana, SCP-572, and instantly
felt like I was unstoppable.

01:34:13.780 --> 01:34:17.920
The sword made me believe I was a powerful
warrior, as if I could maybe fight my way

01:34:17.920 --> 01:34:20.190
out of the Foundation facility with ease.

01:34:20.190 --> 01:34:23.550
Unfortunately, the moment I tried to use it
didn’t lead to freedom.

01:34:23.550 --> 01:34:28.179
I had an accident, broke my arm and suffered
a series of internal fractures, all while

01:34:28.179 --> 01:34:30.040
the research team mocked me.

01:34:30.040 --> 01:34:33.469
Embarrassed, I felt like a total laughing
stock.

01:34:33.469 --> 01:34:35.210
Things only got worse from there.

01:34:35.210 --> 01:34:39.280
I had barely set foot outside of the infirmary
before a different team of personnel told

01:34:39.280 --> 01:34:42.179
me I was going to be assisting them with an
important task.

01:34:42.179 --> 01:34:46.530
They dragged me to an old wooden door after
briefly explaining what was going on; although

01:34:46.530 --> 01:34:48.080
I struggled to keep up.

01:34:48.080 --> 01:34:53.860
Beyond the door – which they called SCP-2317
– was a vast salt pan.

01:34:53.860 --> 01:34:57.500
Following their instructions, I acted as an
assistant to the rest of the team as they

01:34:57.500 --> 01:34:59.810
performed a strange ritual.

01:34:59.810 --> 01:35:04.780
I had to scatter a mix of holy water and chicken
blood around a circle of seven stone pillars,

01:35:04.780 --> 01:35:08.701
then recite:
“Blood for the Old Gods, Water for the New

01:35:08.701 --> 01:35:10.020
King.”

01:35:10.020 --> 01:35:14.600
It was an unusual practice, to say the least,
but apparently, I was helping keep a powerful,

01:35:14.600 --> 01:35:16.420
vengeful demigod imprisoned.

01:35:16.420 --> 01:35:20.640
That did little to soften the news that I
heard when we returned through SCP-2317.

01:35:20.640 --> 01:35:25.300
While I was gone, one of the Foundation’s
security team had been infected by something

01:35:25.300 --> 01:35:31.180
known as SCP-2193, a phenomenon that makes
people believe that every month a large group

01:35:31.180 --> 01:35:33.170
of D-Class are to be ‘terminated’.

01:35:33.170 --> 01:35:38.400
When I heard, I actually considered taking
my amnestic for this month to forget just

01:35:38.400 --> 01:35:40.170
how many had been killed…

01:35:40.170 --> 01:35:45.190
It had been a whole two months since I had
actively started work as part of D-Class Personnel,

01:35:45.190 --> 01:35:47.830
and there weren’t many of my fellow prisoners
left.

01:35:47.830 --> 01:35:52.510
I was sent for a mandatory psychological evaluation;
the Foundation wanted to know if I would still

01:35:52.510 --> 01:35:54.610
be able to perform tests for them.

01:35:54.610 --> 01:35:59.180
I doubted it came from a place of actual concern,
it’s not like they cared all that much if

01:35:59.180 --> 01:36:01.949
the things I had seen were taking a toll on
me.

01:36:01.949 --> 01:36:06.020
Sitting across from the researcher performing
my evaluation, I did my best impression of

01:36:06.020 --> 01:36:07.300
a normal person.

01:36:07.300 --> 01:36:11.330
I had to pretend like none of it was getting
to me, all the horrors and the close brush

01:36:11.330 --> 01:36:13.690
I’d almost had with death by Monthly Termination.

01:36:13.690 --> 01:36:17.440
I wouldn’t have lied about it normally,
But I’d been told I had a month left before

01:36:17.440 --> 01:36:18.840
I was due to be released.

01:36:18.840 --> 01:36:23.810
Apparently, not many D-Class made it that
far, given the dangerous nature of testing.

01:36:23.810 --> 01:36:27.750
All I could think about was how good it would
feel to eat real food again; I started to

01:36:27.750 --> 01:36:31.179
daydream about getting a cheeseburger on my
first day out.

01:36:31.179 --> 01:36:34.830
Almost instantly, my hopes for freedom were
dashed the very next day.

01:36:34.830 --> 01:36:39.429
I was told I’d be going on a longer assignment,
this time it would be an expedition into an

01:36:39.429 --> 01:36:43.110
anomalous location called SCP-432.

01:36:43.110 --> 01:36:47.360
The Foundation’s researchers outfitted me
with a flashlight, plus extra batteries.

01:36:47.360 --> 01:36:52.030
A headset and microphone linked to their control
center were placed over my ear, along with

01:36:52.030 --> 01:36:56.350
a camera unit mounted on my shoulder; they
said it would wirelessly transmit back to

01:36:56.350 --> 01:36:58.050
them, and they’d see what I saw.

01:36:58.050 --> 01:37:03.219
I was also given a couple of bottles of water,
some energy bars, and a few sticks of luminous

01:37:03.219 --> 01:37:04.219
marker chalk.

01:37:04.219 --> 01:37:08.660
But when they had said ‘anomalous location’,
the last thing I’d been expecting was to

01:37:08.660 --> 01:37:10.719
climb into a rusty metal cabinet.

01:37:10.719 --> 01:37:14.510
The next two weeks were like living inside
a nightmare.

01:37:14.510 --> 01:37:18.730
Inside the cabinet was a huge maze, a literal
labyrinth all made out of the same rusted

01:37:18.730 --> 01:37:22.090
steel of the exterior of SCP-432.

01:37:22.090 --> 01:37:26.301
It was so dark, even with the use of my flashlight
and the few lightbulbs affixed to some of

01:37:26.301 --> 01:37:28.119
the walls within the Cabinet Maze.

01:37:28.119 --> 01:37:31.750
Before long, I ended up losing my bearings,
lost with no way out.

01:37:31.750 --> 01:37:35.180
But being trapped in the maze and the dark
wasn’t even the worst parts.

01:37:35.180 --> 01:37:39.260
There was something else in the labyrinth,
some kind of creature lurking through the

01:37:39.260 --> 01:37:40.530
metal corridors.

01:37:40.530 --> 01:37:45.100
My flashlight eventually ran out of power,
so I never actually saw it, but every time

01:37:45.100 --> 01:37:48.369
I tried to sleep, I’d just lay awake, listening
to its growls.

01:37:48.369 --> 01:37:52.830
I couldn’t help what kind of horrible thing
was out there, and might be looming over me

01:37:52.830 --> 01:37:53.850
in the dark.

01:37:53.850 --> 01:37:57.030
Even when I opened my eyes, I still wouldn’t
get to see it.

01:37:57.030 --> 01:38:00.449
Eventually, another D-Class was sent into
SCP-432.

01:38:00.449 --> 01:38:04.670
By some miracle, he was able to not only find
me, but guide me back to the entrance of the

01:38:04.670 --> 01:38:05.670
Cabinet Maze.

01:38:05.670 --> 01:38:09.820
The moment I was free, I yelled at the research
team, demanding they let me go.

01:38:09.820 --> 01:38:15.260
I didn’t just mean let out of SCP-432, I
meant free from D-Class, from the Foundation,

01:38:15.260 --> 01:38:16.260
from all of it.

01:38:16.260 --> 01:38:20.770
I was sent for another psyche evaluation and
deemed to be fine, just suffering from heightened

01:38:20.770 --> 01:38:21.770
stress.

01:38:21.770 --> 01:38:25.320
When they asked me if I’d been taking my
amnestics, I lied and told them yes.

01:38:25.320 --> 01:38:29.051
Ten days before I was set to be released and
they couldn’t help but assign me to one

01:38:29.051 --> 01:38:30.320
last test.

01:38:30.320 --> 01:38:33.719
“Don’t worry,” a researcher sarcastically
assured me.

01:38:33.719 --> 01:38:37.610
“This one will be nice and easy on you,
you’ll just have to jump into a paddling

01:38:37.610 --> 01:38:38.610
pool.

01:38:38.610 --> 01:38:40.760
You can do that, can’t you, D-2152?”

01:38:40.760 --> 01:38:44.260
The paddling pool in question was SCP-120.

01:38:44.260 --> 01:38:48.270
Every time a different glow emanated from
it, I was instructed to jump in.

01:38:48.270 --> 01:38:51.790
The exact second I did, I found myself transported
somewhere new.

01:38:51.790 --> 01:38:56.580
I got The Himalayas on my first go, Greenland
and the Sahara Desert on the next two tries.

01:38:56.580 --> 01:39:00.820
Every one of the locations I ended up at had
some kind of Foundation facility established

01:39:00.820 --> 01:39:01.820
nearby.

01:39:01.820 --> 01:39:06.389
They’d pick me up, and I’d be taken back
to SCP-120 to continue testing.

01:39:06.389 --> 01:39:09.820
It felt like I was being deliberately, intentionally
tortured.

01:39:09.820 --> 01:39:14.110
The Foundation was giving me these momentary
glimpses of freedom every time I was teleported

01:39:14.110 --> 01:39:15.110
by SCP-120.

01:39:15.110 --> 01:39:18.921
It was a carrot on the end of a stick that
they were keeping just out of my reach, and

01:39:18.921 --> 01:39:20.090
I hated it.

01:39:20.090 --> 01:39:24.930
It made me realize that I had never been there
to work for them, being part of D-Class Personnel

01:39:24.930 --> 01:39:26.060
wasn’t a job.

01:39:26.060 --> 01:39:28.860
All I was to them was a human lab rat.

01:39:28.860 --> 01:39:30.099
Now it’s my last day.

01:39:30.099 --> 01:39:33.600
They’ve promised me that by the end of today,
I won’t be here anymore.

01:39:33.600 --> 01:39:36.820
I just want to be out, done with D-Class for
good.

01:39:36.820 --> 01:39:40.739
The Foundation’s researchers have given
me one last, simple test to do before I go.

01:39:40.739 --> 01:39:43.290
They said if I do, then I’ll be gone.

01:39:43.290 --> 01:39:47.449
All they want me to do is wear this amulet
for someone called Doctor Bright.

01:39:47.449 --> 01:39:48.460
How hard can that be?

01:39:48.460 --> 01:39:52.239
And they said they’d let me go after this
one last thing, so it can’t be anything

01:39:52.239 --> 01:39:53.460
all that bad, right?

01:39:53.460 --> 01:39:54.460
Right…?

01:39:54.460 --> 01:39:58.340
It doesn’t feel quite real to say this but…
mission accomplished.

01:39:58.340 --> 01:40:00.670
I did it, I actually made it through.

01:40:00.670 --> 01:40:04.360
My extraction is currently en route, and once
they’re here, I’ll be heading back to

01:40:04.360 --> 01:40:05.450
the SCP Foundation.

01:40:05.450 --> 01:40:09.320
It doesn’t feel like a hundred days since
this all started, if anything it feels like

01:40:09.320 --> 01:40:10.620
a lot longer.

01:40:10.620 --> 01:40:14.880
But it’s done now, the mission is finally
over – and now the real work begins.

01:40:14.880 --> 01:40:19.460
I’ll be taken in for debriefing when I arrive,
so I’d better get my story straight before

01:40:19.460 --> 01:40:20.840
that transport gets here.

01:40:20.840 --> 01:40:25.540
Here’s how I Survived 100 Days Infiltrating
the Chaos Insurgency.

01:40:25.540 --> 01:40:30.330
It started with a message; a direct request
passed down straight to me from the O5 Council

01:40:30.330 --> 01:40:31.520
themselves.

01:40:31.520 --> 01:40:35.989
I was shocked initially to receive any communication
from them; the Council were hardly hands-on

01:40:35.989 --> 01:40:41.070
bosses, preferring to co-ordinate the Foundation
from a station far, far above mine.

01:40:41.070 --> 01:40:45.141
I was part of a Mobile Task Force, used to
being out in the field, so when I got the

01:40:45.141 --> 01:40:49.530
message – saying the Council wanted to talk
to me – I wasn’t quite sure what to make

01:40:49.530 --> 01:40:50.530
of it.

01:40:50.530 --> 01:40:53.710
I went through various different states of
reaction in my head, first being honored that

01:40:53.710 --> 01:40:55.400
they had singled me out.

01:40:55.400 --> 01:40:59.100
That then turned into worry – had I done
something wrong, or was this some kind of

01:40:59.100 --> 01:41:01.070
dismissal from the Foundation?

01:41:01.070 --> 01:41:02.409
Was it a performance review?

01:41:02.409 --> 01:41:06.140
When it finally came time for me to stand
before the O5 Council, the last thing that

01:41:06.140 --> 01:41:11.739
I was expecting to hear was that they had
selected me specially for a dangerous undercover

01:41:11.739 --> 01:41:12.800
assignment.

01:41:12.800 --> 01:41:16.080
Of course, I had heard of the Chaos Insurgency
before.

01:41:16.080 --> 01:41:20.320
Although I was yet to actually encounter them
in the field, the MTFs were all instructed

01:41:20.320 --> 01:41:23.280
to keep up to date on who the Foundation’s
enemies were.

01:41:23.280 --> 01:41:26.850
As a Group of Interest, it wasn’t hard to
see why they were such a threat.

01:41:26.850 --> 01:41:31.980
A heavily armed, clandestine organization
with goals of weaponizing SCPs and achieving

01:41:31.980 --> 01:41:37.179
world domination: they sounded like lunatics,
but dangerous lunatics all the same.

01:41:37.179 --> 01:41:40.440
That was the first step in my mission: know
your enemy.

01:41:40.440 --> 01:41:45.810
I was instructed to familiarise myself with
the Chaos Insurgency, to understand them inside

01:41:45.810 --> 01:41:46.810
and out.

01:41:46.810 --> 01:41:51.670
While the MTF agent in me wanted to just get
boots to ground and start the real work, this

01:41:51.670 --> 01:41:54.020
operation needed a different approach.

01:41:54.020 --> 01:42:00.270
And so, I hit the books; or, to put it more
accurately, I began scouring the SCP Foundation’s

01:42:00.270 --> 01:42:03.550
database for any and all intel on the Insurgency.

01:42:03.550 --> 01:42:06.040
All I could do was sit and read.

01:42:06.040 --> 01:42:09.580
Within forty-eight hours of being given my
assignment, I felt like I knew everything

01:42:09.580 --> 01:42:14.030
about the Chaos Insurgency back to front,
sideways, upside down, and pretty much any

01:42:14.030 --> 01:42:15.380
and every way possible.

01:42:15.380 --> 01:42:21.231
So, imagine how disheartened I felt when I
reported my research to the O5 Council, only

01:42:21.231 --> 01:42:24.170
to be told that I hadn’t done enough.

01:42:24.170 --> 01:42:28.290
Hardly the best feeling, I won’t deny it
stung to hear that my day of research wasn’t

01:42:28.290 --> 01:42:30.460
adequate prep for my assignment.

01:42:30.460 --> 01:42:36.020
But the Council did have an important point:
this was a covert, deep-cover infiltration

01:42:36.020 --> 01:42:39.560
into one of the most dangerous groups that
opposed the Foundation.

01:42:39.560 --> 01:42:44.080
Purely memorizing all manner of facts and
figures about the Insurgency, learning of

01:42:44.080 --> 01:42:48.179
all their tactical capabilities, reading up
on the various rumors about how they were

01:42:48.179 --> 01:42:49.840
founded; all that was just knowledge.

01:42:49.840 --> 01:42:55.930
If I wanted to pass myself off as a part of
the Chaos Insurgency and avoid detection,

01:42:55.930 --> 01:42:56.960
knowing wasn’t enough.

01:42:56.960 --> 01:42:59.430
I had to think like one of them.

01:42:59.430 --> 01:43:02.099
The timeline of the mission began to accelerate
rapidly.

01:43:02.099 --> 01:43:07.020
I would have to train myself to believe the
Insurgency’s core beliefs as I went along;

01:43:07.020 --> 01:43:09.740
events weren’t going to wait for me to ne
ready.

01:43:09.740 --> 01:43:14.389
The Council informed me that they were aware
of a double agent working for the Chaos Insurgency

01:43:14.389 --> 01:43:17.680
from within the SCP Foundation itself.

01:43:17.680 --> 01:43:21.770
Obviously, I had heard rumors that there were
operatives embedded within the ranks of the

01:43:21.770 --> 01:43:26.489
Foundation, but what was more surprising was
that the O5 Council seemed to already know

01:43:26.489 --> 01:43:27.550
who these agents were.

01:43:27.550 --> 01:43:32.420
I had always assumed that these Insurgents
kept their identities a secret, so to hearing

01:43:32.420 --> 01:43:37.710
that a particular researcher – Doctor Parsons
– was a known Chaos Insurgency conspirator

01:43:37.710 --> 01:43:41.780
and still had a job at the Foundation was
both startling and worrying.

01:43:41.780 --> 01:43:47.670
But he provided me a way in, and I assumed
that’s why the O5 Council allowed the Insurgency’s

01:43:47.670 --> 01:43:51.719
double agents to remain as ‘undetected’
as they thought they were.

01:43:51.719 --> 01:43:56.199
It meant they could be used as tools for the
Foundation’s benefit to get at the Insurgency

01:43:56.199 --> 01:44:00.000
– they were double agents, after all.

01:44:00.000 --> 01:44:04.940
Doctor Parsons was due to be at the same Foundation
site I was stationed at for only a few days.

01:44:04.940 --> 01:44:09.180
The upside of working directly on the orders
of the Council meant that they had the administerial

01:44:09.180 --> 01:44:14.480
reach to pull all the necessary strings to
get this duplicitous double agent in the right

01:44:14.480 --> 01:44:18.160
place for me to intercept him, but not for
long.

01:44:18.160 --> 01:44:22.570
The window of opportunity was only a small
one, I had a short time to get close enough

01:44:22.570 --> 01:44:26.889
to Parsons and get him to put me in contact
with his friends at the Chaos Insurgency.

01:44:26.889 --> 01:44:30.120
Of course, I had to be tactful about it.

01:44:30.120 --> 01:44:34.860
If I came on too strong, there was a strong
chance the double agent would easily detect

01:44:34.860 --> 01:44:38.119
that something was off and that his cover
had been blown.

01:44:38.119 --> 01:44:43.450
So, the plan was simple: strike up a conversation
with the spy, in a way that wouldn’t arouse

01:44:43.450 --> 01:44:48.320
suspicion and would compel him to assist my
joining the Chaos Insurgency.

01:44:48.320 --> 01:44:52.330
Actually, ‘simple’ might not have been
the right word.

01:44:52.330 --> 01:44:56.620
The day of my confrontation with Parsons I
was uncontrollably nervous.

01:44:56.620 --> 01:45:01.500
I could feel myself shaking and silently hoped
that it wasn’t noticeable to anyone looking

01:45:01.500 --> 01:45:02.500
at me.

01:45:02.500 --> 01:45:06.730
The mess hall, was decided as the best place
I could drum up some kind of conversation

01:45:06.730 --> 01:45:07.730
with him.

01:45:07.730 --> 01:45:12.349
As I picked up my food tray, I spotted the
doctor taking a seat in a far corner.

01:45:12.349 --> 01:45:15.120
Cautiously, I approached and asked if I could
join him.

01:45:15.120 --> 01:45:19.010
In the back of my head, I kept reminding myself
I had to play it casual.

01:45:19.010 --> 01:45:23.260
If I went in immediately with claiming I felt
the Foundation should be run differently,

01:45:23.260 --> 01:45:27.960
then Parsons would smell the deception, and
the whole mission would come apart.

01:45:27.960 --> 01:45:30.010
So instead, I started small.

01:45:30.010 --> 01:45:33.690
We drummed up a conversation about the quality
of the food the Foundation catering team had

01:45:33.690 --> 01:45:37.190
given us – or rather, the lack of quality.

01:45:37.190 --> 01:45:41.949
Then we chatted about containment procedures,
and various anomalies that we had each encountered

01:45:41.949 --> 01:45:43.199
in our different jobs.

01:45:43.199 --> 01:45:47.630
Lunch was wrapping up, we’d both finished
our food and I knew my window was about to

01:45:47.630 --> 01:45:48.630
close.

01:45:48.630 --> 01:45:52.920
And so, I threw in to conversation:
“You ever get sick of the way they do things?”

01:45:52.920 --> 01:45:58.270
The entire next day, I was on edge, worried
that I’d thrown my assignment and alerted

01:45:58.270 --> 01:46:02.240
Parsons that the Foundation was aware of his
ties to the Insurgency.

01:46:02.240 --> 01:46:06.420
I didn’t leave the Task Force barracks,
confining myself to my quarters.

01:46:06.420 --> 01:46:11.260
If I left, there was always a slim chance
of running into Doctor Parsons, or that the

01:46:11.260 --> 01:46:15.460
Council would call me in to reprimand how
I handled first contact.

01:46:15.460 --> 01:46:19.680
To my surprise, my paranoid thoughts were
broken by a direct message from Parsons.

01:46:19.680 --> 01:46:24.890
He mentioned being intrigued by some of our
conversation the previous day, and being disappointed

01:46:24.890 --> 01:46:29.850
I hadn’t been in the mess hall this lunchtime
to continue discussing certain aspects.

01:46:29.850 --> 01:46:35.030
It felt weirdly nice being friendly, only
to then remember the man was a double agent

01:46:35.030 --> 01:46:36.900
for the Chaos Insurgency.

01:46:36.900 --> 01:46:40.930
His message went on to suggest we should meet
in private in his office.

01:46:40.930 --> 01:46:43.420
And that’s when the tension really hit me.

01:46:43.420 --> 01:46:48.400
Either he wanted me dead with no witnesses,
or he had bought my line about disagreeing

01:46:48.400 --> 01:46:50.349
with the Foundation’s methods.

01:46:50.349 --> 01:46:55.949
I arrived at Parsons’ office at the agreed
time, only to be welcomed warmly by the doctor.

01:46:55.949 --> 01:46:59.630
It was instantly disarming, and intentionally
so.

01:46:59.630 --> 01:47:03.739
I tried not to fall for it, just in case he
knew what I was really up to.

01:47:03.739 --> 01:47:07.760
But at the same time, I couldn’t let it
show.

01:47:07.760 --> 01:47:12.170
Doctor Parsons seemed to pick up our discussion
from the other day, immediately talking about

01:47:12.170 --> 01:47:16.880
how the SCP Foundation would be better off
putting anomalies to use rather than keeping

01:47:16.880 --> 01:47:18.250
them contained.

01:47:18.250 --> 01:47:23.960
I was surprised how brazen he was being, but
then again, the Council knew he was a mole,

01:47:23.960 --> 01:47:27.510
maybe they had bugged his office, and he hadn’t
realized.

01:47:27.510 --> 01:47:32.240
Nodding along, pretending to agree with what
the doctor was saying, the conversation quickly

01:47:32.240 --> 01:47:33.240
progressed.

01:47:33.240 --> 01:47:38.469
I told Parsons I felt the same, and it was
frustrating to me as an MTF agent, seeing

01:47:38.469 --> 01:47:43.070
so many co-workers die in containment breaches
against SCPs.

01:47:43.070 --> 01:47:47.179
That was the moment he mentioned he had ‘some
friends I should meet.’

01:47:47.179 --> 01:47:50.159
The following night, they came for me while
I slept in the barracks.

01:47:50.159 --> 01:47:55.150
I had no idea who had drugged me, although
I suspected it was Doctor Parsons.

01:47:55.150 --> 01:47:59.679
He wasn’t there when I woke up though, in
fact, I couldn’t really tell who was at

01:47:59.679 --> 01:48:00.679
first.

01:48:00.679 --> 01:48:04.679
The combination of the bag over my face and
my head still reeling from whatever had knocked

01:48:04.679 --> 01:48:06.020
me out saw to that.

01:48:06.020 --> 01:48:08.989
Wherever I was, I didn’t get to see it.

01:48:08.989 --> 01:48:13.800
There were figures near me, at least two,
that much I could make out.

01:48:13.800 --> 01:48:16.280
Especially when the interrogation started.

01:48:16.280 --> 01:48:20.679
They were Chaos Insurgency; their line of
questioning told me that.

01:48:20.679 --> 01:48:25.230
Between punches and tasing, their questions
ranged from what I was really up to, to whether

01:48:25.230 --> 01:48:28.040
the Foundation had sent me to infiltrate their
organization.

01:48:28.040 --> 01:48:32.461
They’d anticipated the Council would use
their own tactics against them, but every

01:48:32.461 --> 01:48:36.020
time, no matter how much pain they inflicted,
I denied it.

01:48:36.020 --> 01:48:42.590
I wasn’t a mole, I told them over and over
again: I believed what they believed.

01:48:42.590 --> 01:48:47.070
After over a week of torture, the bag came
off, and I was greeted by the sight of my

01:48:47.070 --> 01:48:49.790
captors in Chaos Insurgency tactical gear.

01:48:49.790 --> 01:48:54.680
They informed me that I had passed the initial
phase of their recruitment process, and after

01:48:54.680 --> 01:48:58.100
suffering so much, that should have come as
a relief.

01:48:58.100 --> 01:49:03.360
My mission to infiltrate them was progressing,
but their interrogation had left me exhausted

01:49:03.360 --> 01:49:05.780
and in intense pain.

01:49:05.780 --> 01:49:10.349
The pair of Insurgents made it very clear
that I wasn’t one of them though, not yet.

01:49:10.349 --> 01:49:14.810
In order to fully join their ranks, they wanted
to send me on an operation with one of their

01:49:14.810 --> 01:49:16.360
armed detachments.

01:49:16.360 --> 01:49:21.349
If I complied with orders, and made a deliberate
act against the SCP Foundation, that would

01:49:21.349 --> 01:49:25.540
tell them that I was ready to become part
of the Chaos Insurgency.

01:49:25.540 --> 01:49:30.449
If I refused, or the mission was deemed to
be a failure, it would mean an on-the-spot

01:49:30.449 --> 01:49:31.540
execution.

01:49:31.540 --> 01:49:36.300
Hiding my reluctance, knowing I wouldn’t
be able to forewarn the Foundation, I agreed.

01:49:36.300 --> 01:49:39.750
I spent the next several days with a new group
of Insurgency operatives.

01:49:39.750 --> 01:49:45.040
None of them gave me their names, nor any
actionable intel I could pass on to the Council.

01:49:45.040 --> 01:49:49.980
I just had to refer to them by their various
Gamma and Beta class numbers, their ranks

01:49:49.980 --> 01:49:52.160
within the Chaos Insurgency.

01:49:52.160 --> 01:49:57.590
The mission was an extraction; as part of
their efforts to utilize anomalies, the Insurgency

01:49:57.590 --> 01:50:02.210
would often launch violent raids on the Foundation
to capture SCPs.

01:50:02.210 --> 01:50:06.770
Their target this time was SCP-2800, the Cactusman.

01:50:06.770 --> 01:50:11.619
He was mostly harmless save for his spines,
but the group’s leaders, Delta Command,

01:50:11.619 --> 01:50:15.320
wanted to force SCP-2800 to join their ranks.

01:50:15.320 --> 01:50:20.380
I had to treat it like any other operation
with my MTF, trying to force myself to forget

01:50:20.380 --> 01:50:25.830
that I was on the wrong side as we broke into
the Site containing SCP-2800.

01:50:25.830 --> 01:50:29.610
He didn’t want to leave, but I hoped that
the Foundation would eventually launch a counter

01:50:29.610 --> 01:50:31.469
mission to rescue him.

01:50:31.469 --> 01:50:36.810
All that mattered for now was making the Insurgency
see that I could be helpful, and useful to

01:50:36.810 --> 01:50:37.810
them.

01:50:37.810 --> 01:50:41.139
Sure enough, the Foundation did recover SCP-2800.

01:50:41.139 --> 01:50:45.800
And by the time they did, the Chaos Insurgency
had already welcomed me into their ranks.

01:50:45.800 --> 01:50:51.040
I’d proven myself and that I shared their
beliefs in utilizing the anomalous to realize

01:50:51.040 --> 01:50:52.179
their goals.

01:50:52.179 --> 01:50:56.830
During the mission, I had made a point of
showing my experience and skills as an MTF

01:50:56.830 --> 01:50:59.390
operative, hoping that would impress them.

01:50:59.390 --> 01:51:03.870
It seemed to have done the trick, as I was
made a member of Beta Class, still under the

01:51:03.870 --> 01:51:06.820
command of Gamma and the Delta Command above
them.

01:51:06.820 --> 01:51:11.610
But it was a start, at least, not as low in
the chain as the Alpha Class were.

01:51:11.610 --> 01:51:16.550
They were Insurgency recruits with no experience
dealing with anomalies, and who only joined

01:51:16.550 --> 01:51:19.070
to escape their miserable lives.

01:51:19.070 --> 01:51:23.469
Delta Command considered Alpha Class expendable,
as if they were little more than just cannon

01:51:23.469 --> 01:51:24.469
fodder.

01:51:24.469 --> 01:51:28.050
I couldn’t help but feel bad for them, especially
when I realized that the Foundation treated

01:51:28.050 --> 01:51:31.010
their D Class exactly the same.

01:51:31.010 --> 01:51:33.760
Even the MTFs were mostly foot soldiers to
them…

01:51:33.760 --> 01:51:38.010
Now established within the Insurgency’s
ranks, I spent the next two weeks finding

01:51:38.010 --> 01:51:42.300
out everything I could about their operations
and reporting back to the O5 Council through

01:51:42.300 --> 01:51:44.500
a secure, encrypted channel.

01:51:44.500 --> 01:51:48.700
Part of my infiltration mission was to see
if I could locate the Engine, a mysterious

01:51:48.700 --> 01:51:54.010
anomalous artifact that had apparently influenced
the earliest members – now Delta Command

01:51:54.010 --> 01:51:57.400
– to form the Chaos Insurgency in the first
place.

01:51:57.400 --> 01:52:01.170
There was no sign of the Engine anywhere in
the compound I was stationed at.

01:52:01.170 --> 01:52:05.320
Then again, its location was likely a closely
guarded secret.

01:52:05.320 --> 01:52:09.820
I did, however, encounter one of the anomalies
within the Insurgency’s possession.

01:52:09.820 --> 01:52:16.340
It was SCP-1316, what appeared to be an ordinary
and harmless kitten named Lucy.

01:52:16.340 --> 01:52:22.270
But in actuality, SCP-1316 was an intelligent
and sophisticated listening device used to

01:52:22.270 --> 01:52:27.320
spy on the Foundation and report back to the
Insurgency; I made sure to warn the Council

01:52:27.320 --> 01:52:31.430
the moment I found out, letting them know
it wasn’t just double agents being used

01:52:31.430 --> 01:52:34.320
to report on Foundation activity.

01:52:34.320 --> 01:52:38.880
Just as things were starting to form a regular
pattern, things seemed to start going south.

01:52:38.880 --> 01:52:43.659
A few of my ‘fellow’ Insurgents seemed
to mention things in conversation that made

01:52:43.659 --> 01:52:45.070
me start to feel paranoid.

01:52:45.070 --> 01:52:49.431
They weren’t directly referencing me, but
they talked openly about what they’d do

01:52:49.431 --> 01:52:55.230
if they found out the Foundation had tried
to spy on the Insurgency; and none of it was

01:52:55.230 --> 01:52:56.230
pleasant.

01:52:56.230 --> 01:52:57.349
I spent the rest of the day wracked with worry.

01:52:57.349 --> 01:53:01.960
There was no way they could have known I was
communicating with the O5 Council, or that

01:53:01.960 --> 01:53:06.850
the only reason I was there had been to gather
intelligence on the Chaos Insurgency.

01:53:06.850 --> 01:53:10.440
It could have just been what people talked
about here, although I couldn’t shake my

01:53:10.440 --> 01:53:15.210
concern that maybe my cover had been blown,
that this was their subtle way of telling

01:53:15.210 --> 01:53:18.160
me the Insurgency was onto me.

01:53:18.160 --> 01:53:22.690
The worrying left me no choice, and I raised
my concerns with the Council the first chance

01:53:22.690 --> 01:53:24.040
I got.

01:53:24.040 --> 01:53:28.280
Unfortunately, the response I received the
following day did little to subdue my worries.

01:53:28.280 --> 01:53:33.280
The O5 Council contacted me, reminding me
of the importance of my mission.

01:53:33.280 --> 01:53:38.079
I was to infiltrate and report back to them,
find the Engine or at least its location.

01:53:38.079 --> 01:53:43.120
There was an extraction planned, arriving
in secret in a month’s time.

01:53:43.120 --> 01:53:46.010
That was my time limit, and a warning came
with it:

01:53:46.010 --> 01:53:51.090
“If you can’t complete this assignment,
then don’t bother returning to the Foundation,”

01:53:51.090 --> 01:53:52.320
the Council told me.

01:53:52.320 --> 01:53:57.730
“If you are unsuccessful in carrying out
your objectives, it will be taken as an act

01:53:57.730 --> 01:53:58.860
of sedition.

01:53:58.860 --> 01:54:04.670
We will consider you an active member of the
Chaos Insurgency, and thus a threat to the

01:54:04.670 --> 01:54:05.670
Foundation.”

01:54:05.670 --> 01:54:08.690
I was furious, not to mention scared out of
my mind.

01:54:08.690 --> 01:54:12.219
The Council was willing to turn their backs
on me if I couldn’t deliver, and if the

01:54:12.219 --> 01:54:16.179
Insurgency found out they had a mole in their
ranks, then it sounded like they were ready

01:54:16.179 --> 01:54:18.079
to kill me on the spot.

01:54:18.079 --> 01:54:22.010
The Insurgency were gearing up for another
mission, this time a more aggressive attack

01:54:22.010 --> 01:54:26.829
on the Foundation as opposed to the stealthier
extraction that I’d previously been on.

01:54:26.829 --> 01:54:32.110
Surprisingly, that put me more at ease; sure,
it’d be dangerous, but this was what I was

01:54:32.110 --> 01:54:33.110
good at.

01:54:33.110 --> 01:54:36.440
In the field intensity, combating anomalies.

01:54:36.440 --> 01:54:41.159
I could use this as an opportunity to regain
some footing, remind the Chaos Insurgency

01:54:41.159 --> 01:54:44.630
what made me useful to them… or so I thought.

01:54:44.630 --> 01:54:50.520
The mission was to capture SCP-682, and fit
it with a piece of technology the Insurgency

01:54:50.520 --> 01:54:55.650
had tested on SCP-2394, a form of sapient
tree frog.

01:54:55.650 --> 01:55:00.481
Their plan was the device would enable them
to control the Hard to Destroy Reptile, meaning

01:55:00.481 --> 01:55:05.610
the Insurgency would have an unstoppable,
adaptable killing machine in their arsenal.

01:55:05.610 --> 01:55:11.950
The attack on the Foundation site was a bloodbath,
MTFs beset the Insurgents on all sides.

01:55:11.950 --> 01:55:13.230
And as for 682…

01:55:13.230 --> 01:55:18.290
well, it wasn’t exactly known for coming
quietly without putting up a fight.

01:55:18.290 --> 01:55:22.489
Returning from the field, after only a few
days, I was confronted by a higher-up, a member

01:55:22.489 --> 01:55:26.000
of Gamma Class that oversaw the Insurgency’s
operations.

01:55:26.000 --> 01:55:29.650
He told me that there had been numerous reports
from other operatives, that they hadn’t

01:55:29.650 --> 01:55:34.550
seen me killing or even injuring Foundation
personnel during the failed extraction of

01:55:34.550 --> 01:55:35.550
SCP-682.

01:55:35.550 --> 01:55:39.270
It was true, I was still loyal to the Foundation,
after all.

01:55:39.270 --> 01:55:44.849
The people working there were still my co-workers,
even if I was pretending to be an Insurgent.

01:55:44.849 --> 01:55:49.140
I’d deliberately missed shots, still using
my weapon in the firefights with MTFs, but

01:55:49.140 --> 01:55:50.429
without causing them harm.

01:55:50.429 --> 01:55:54.929
I’d hoped nobody in the Insurgency would
notice, but it seemed they had.

01:55:54.929 --> 01:55:59.840
I brushed off the accusation, made some excuse
about needing to go for an eye exam and get

01:55:59.840 --> 01:56:00.840
contact lenses.

01:56:00.840 --> 01:56:03.890
But under the surface were relentless shudders
of panic.

01:56:03.890 --> 01:56:08.960
In hindsight, the Gamma officer had told me
this so the Insurgency could see how I reacted,

01:56:08.960 --> 01:56:13.820
but at the time, I didn’t realize… and
that played right into their hand.

01:56:13.820 --> 01:56:19.030
The next day I was caught while going to send
an urgent SOS message to the O5 Council.

01:56:19.030 --> 01:56:23.870
Before I could reach the place I stashed by
encrypted comm device, a group of Chaos Insurgency

01:56:23.870 --> 01:56:25.730
Beta class operatives rushed me.

01:56:25.730 --> 01:56:30.909
I was bagged again, pulled away kicking and
screaming, and unable to call for help…

01:56:30.909 --> 01:56:34.579
Over the next few days, it was like I’d
been sent back in time to the start of my

01:56:34.579 --> 01:56:35.849
infiltration.

01:56:35.849 --> 01:56:41.040
As the Insurgency tortured me again, I refused
to give in, despite how much I wanted to just

01:56:41.040 --> 01:56:42.040
to make the pain stop.

01:56:42.040 --> 01:56:44.250
But even then, it didn’t matter.

01:56:44.250 --> 01:56:48.570
Their knowledge of the Foundation was greater
than I realized, and the Insurgents were easily

01:56:48.570 --> 01:56:50.890
able to crack the encryption of my comm unit.

01:56:50.890 --> 01:56:56.340
With that broken, they had access to all the
messages I’d been sending to the O5 Council;

01:56:56.340 --> 01:57:00.570
and realizing who had sent me made them change
their interrogation methods.

01:57:00.570 --> 01:57:05.219
The Insurgents revealed something I hadn’t
heard before, something the Council had apparently

01:57:05.219 --> 01:57:06.510
kept a secret.

01:57:06.510 --> 01:57:10.530
The Chaos Insurgency had once been a part
of the SCP Foundation.

01:57:10.530 --> 01:57:16.530
I refused to believe it at first, but with
all I’d seen as an MTF agent, even my undercover

01:57:16.530 --> 01:57:18.739
mission, it started to make sense.

01:57:18.739 --> 01:57:24.290
An off-the-record black ops unit working for
the O5 Council to circumvent the Ethics Committee.

01:57:24.290 --> 01:57:28.580
They’d grown disillusioned with how the
Foundation handled its anomalies and broken

01:57:28.580 --> 01:57:31.210
away to form… the Chaos Insurgency.

01:57:31.210 --> 01:57:36.330
When I accepted that to be true, the Insurgents
brought me to what I had been sent for.

01:57:36.330 --> 01:57:38.320
They took me to the Engine.

01:57:38.320 --> 01:57:41.590
Now I’m awaiting my extraction transport.

01:57:41.590 --> 01:57:45.460
After showing me the device that caused their
earliest founders to splinter off from the

01:57:45.460 --> 01:57:50.030
Foundation, the Chaos Insurgency took me several
miles from their compound, to the co-ordinates

01:57:50.030 --> 01:57:52.469
the O5 Council had told me to be at.

01:57:52.469 --> 01:57:57.460
The Foundation think they’re getting their
agent back, and with me, plenty of intel on

01:57:57.460 --> 01:57:58.460
their enemies.

01:57:58.460 --> 01:58:01.040
But the Engine showed me the truth.

01:58:01.040 --> 01:58:05.540
Showed me the lies and fallacies of the Foundation,
and especially of the O5 Council.

01:58:05.540 --> 01:58:12.040
They think they’re getting back their agent…
but I serve the Chaos Insurgency now.

01:58:12.040 --> 01:58:15.790
It’s hard to believe it’s been a full
one hundred days since I began my journey

01:58:15.790 --> 01:58:19.920
– no, my mission – into the beating heart
of the SCP Foundation.

01:58:19.920 --> 01:58:26.610
I, Stathis Stern, risked detection, my safety
and even my own life for this cause, and I’m

01:58:26.610 --> 01:58:31.010
sure that makes you wonder why on Earth I’d
be foolhardy enough to put so much on the

01:58:31.010 --> 01:58:32.010
line.

01:58:32.010 --> 01:58:37.600
Well, my reason is the best – no, the only
– reason that matters: for the sake of art.

01:58:37.600 --> 01:58:42.969
I decided early on that recording my findings
was essential, both for posterity and so that

01:58:42.969 --> 01:58:49.050
the art world may know just how dedicated
I have been in my pursuit of true inspiration.

01:58:49.050 --> 01:58:53.580
It all started over three months ago, that
was when I first managed to embed myself in

01:58:53.580 --> 01:58:55.290
the SCP Foundation.

01:58:55.290 --> 01:58:59.150
Naturally, I never would have jumped at the
idea of having to fraternize with the likes

01:58:59.150 --> 01:59:00.610
of the Chaos Insurgency.

01:59:00.610 --> 01:59:06.429
Their methods are far too brutal, their organization
seeped in violence that trivializes my own

01:59:06.429 --> 01:59:08.349
pursuit of a deeper meaning.

01:59:08.349 --> 01:59:13.250
But even I have to admit, the Insurgency’s
methodology can yield results.

01:59:13.250 --> 01:59:18.190
They arranged for a convenient ‘accident’
to befall a lower-level researcher, after

01:59:18.190 --> 01:59:24.760
they returned home from Site-19; the SCP Foundation’s
largest and most infamous facility, home to

01:59:24.760 --> 01:59:27.490
many of their equally-infamous anomalies.

01:59:27.490 --> 01:59:33.010
Thanks to a falsified identity and phony transfer
order from the Insurgency, I was able to slip

01:59:33.010 --> 01:59:37.640
into Site-19 undetected, so that I could begin
my search.

01:59:37.640 --> 01:59:41.130
The subterfuge was, at first, easy to maintain.

01:59:41.130 --> 01:59:42.429
I looked the part, obviously.

01:59:42.429 --> 01:59:47.510
A lab coat and name badge, so versatile and
yet completely unassuming.

01:59:47.510 --> 01:59:52.610
I was visible and still imperceptible to the
rest of the Foundation’s personnel; as far

01:59:52.610 --> 01:59:56.900
as they knew, I was just the latest transfer
to Site-19.

01:59:56.900 --> 02:00:01.010
As I spent the day getting my bearings, not
one of them could have possibly known my true

02:00:01.010 --> 02:00:02.010
intention.

02:00:02.010 --> 02:00:05.010
My goal was simple and twofold.

02:00:05.010 --> 02:00:09.849
First and foremost: I had long felt I deserved
to be accepted as a member of the art collective

02:00:09.849 --> 02:00:11.010
known as ‘AWCY?’

02:00:11.010 --> 02:00:14.580
or ‘Are We Cool Yet?’.

02:00:14.580 --> 02:00:18.510
Since the nineteenth century, they have been
championing the creation and exhibition of

02:00:18.510 --> 02:00:24.199
anomalous artwork, truly pushing the boundaries
of what can be considered artistic expression

02:00:24.199 --> 02:00:28.870
despite the Foundation’s attempts to censor
AWCY’s transcendent art.

02:00:28.870 --> 02:00:33.370
I had admired their mission statement from
afar, for a number of years, and strived to

02:00:33.370 --> 02:00:35.670
achieve much the same in my own artwork.

02:00:35.670 --> 02:00:40.180
However, none of my previous pieces quite
met the collective’s anomalous pedigree.

02:00:40.180 --> 02:00:43.219
I know a way to change that, though…

02:00:43.219 --> 02:00:48.219
Blending in at Site-19 proved to be fairly
simple; as long as you stride around with

02:00:48.219 --> 02:00:50.949
purpose, few will question what you’re doing.

02:00:50.949 --> 02:00:55.969
With my head in a notebook, or a look of conviction
on my face, I could freely make my way around

02:00:55.969 --> 02:00:56.969
the facility.

02:00:56.969 --> 02:00:58.719
But why, you might wonder?

02:00:58.719 --> 02:01:02.200
What was the actual intention behind my undercover
mission?

02:01:02.200 --> 02:01:07.820
Well, the answer lies in the second half of
my goal: recognition.

02:01:07.820 --> 02:01:13.570
Not just notoriety and acceptance into the
ranks of AWCY, but fame throughout the anomalous

02:01:13.570 --> 02:01:16.790
art world – and all the perks that could
come with.

02:01:16.790 --> 02:01:20.900
Of course, the main hurdle to overcome was
I needed to create a piece of art that was

02:01:20.900 --> 02:01:23.130
anomalous in its nature.

02:01:23.130 --> 02:01:27.760
And so, rather than wait for inspiration and
materials to fall into my lap, I chose to

02:01:27.760 --> 02:01:30.230
instead seek out what I needed.

02:01:30.230 --> 02:01:35.590
The anomalies on offer at Site-19 were abundant,
and knowing where to start was tricky.

02:01:35.590 --> 02:01:39.929
I made the decision, while still getting accustomed
to my new surroundings, that I would start

02:01:39.929 --> 02:01:42.290
wherever I felt the most drawn to.

02:01:42.290 --> 02:01:48.690
Before the end of my first week in Site-19,
I felt the first pull of an anomaly, and uncovered

02:01:48.690 --> 02:01:51.909
it in the most unlikely of places.

02:01:51.909 --> 02:01:56.630
During the day, most of the Foundation living
quarters are empty, meaning I could sneak

02:01:56.630 --> 02:01:58.000
around there undetected.

02:01:58.000 --> 02:02:05.320
It was in the personal bathroom of one Foundation
doctor that I found SCP-063.

02:02:05.320 --> 02:02:09.370
To the untrained eye, the anomaly appears
to be an ordinary toothbrush, save for the

02:02:09.370 --> 02:02:14.060
wording stenciled into its pale blue plastic:
“The World’s Best Tothbrush”.

02:02:14.060 --> 02:02:18.130
Of course, examining it closer, I learned
of its ability to cleave through inorganic

02:02:18.130 --> 02:02:20.840
matter like a hot knife through butter.

02:02:20.840 --> 02:02:25.590
It was quite the shock, slicing the porcelain
of the bathroom’s sink by accident.

02:02:25.590 --> 02:02:29.010
That would have led to my detection, had I
not left soon after.

02:02:29.010 --> 02:02:35.230
But I reflected on the toothbrush – or ‘Tothbrush’
– and what it could possibly represent.

02:02:35.230 --> 02:02:38.699
Perhaps an acknowledgment of rituals, and
their importance to human health.

02:02:38.699 --> 02:02:44.060
Yet, at the same time, being sharp enough
to cut anything not organic – anything not

02:02:44.060 --> 02:02:50.380
human – maybe SCP-063 was meant to symbolize
the possible dangers of habitual behaviors.

02:02:50.380 --> 02:02:54.420
The next day, I was able to bear witness to
an anomalous piece I had only ever heard rumors

02:02:54.420 --> 02:02:55.420
of.

02:02:55.420 --> 02:02:59.730
For a time, I had thought it to have been
the reason for AWCY’s founding; after all

02:02:59.730 --> 02:03:04.360
its nickname was derived from a particular
traditional art form.

02:03:04.360 --> 02:03:08.699
SCP-173, The Sculpture, as those at the Foundation
call it.

02:03:08.699 --> 02:03:14.320
I was able to insert myself into a group of
two other personnel; a researcher and security

02:03:14.320 --> 02:03:15.320
officer.

02:03:15.320 --> 02:03:20.570
The former was entering the container housing
SCP-173 to run tests, the latter protecting

02:03:20.570 --> 02:03:21.570
her.

02:03:21.570 --> 02:03:25.469
Naturally, I overheard them looking for a
third and offered my assistance, even if all

02:03:25.469 --> 02:03:28.340
I had to do was watch The Sculpture intently.

02:03:28.340 --> 02:03:32.730
I wasn’t even allowed to blink without getting
someone else to cover for me while I did.

02:03:32.730 --> 02:03:34.710
I adored it.

02:03:34.710 --> 02:03:38.880
The messy structure of concrete and rebar
defied all notions of what many believed art

02:03:38.880 --> 02:03:43.159
should be; it wasn’t beautiful or pristine,
it dared to be ugly.

02:03:43.159 --> 02:03:50.469
And yet, it demanded to be seen, to be looked
at and perceived, or else it would break my

02:03:50.469 --> 02:03:52.579
neck and claim my life.

02:03:52.579 --> 02:03:57.270
It was everything I’d hoped: pure art, personified.

02:03:57.270 --> 02:04:02.550
It was as I neared the end of my first week
in Site-19 that I discovered my next anomalous

02:04:02.550 --> 02:04:05.909
art piece, hidden away in a secure storage
locker.

02:04:05.909 --> 02:04:09.290
SCP-010, the Collars of Control.

02:04:09.290 --> 02:04:13.230
These were a collection of cast iron collars
that, according to their file, could be used

02:04:13.230 --> 02:04:18.679
to control the actions and movements of a
person wearing one, via a remote control.

02:04:18.679 --> 02:04:20.860
These collars fascinated me.

02:04:20.860 --> 02:04:25.909
They were, to my interpretation, tools to
control yet simultaneously a rejection of

02:04:25.909 --> 02:04:28.909
the very rules that control human beings.

02:04:28.909 --> 02:04:34.449
Someone wearing an SCP-010 collar could potentially
have their entire bodily shape reconfigured

02:04:34.449 --> 02:04:37.000
by the person wielding the remote.

02:04:37.000 --> 02:04:41.380
That alone, the fact these objects could be
used to defy the physical laws that dictate

02:04:41.380 --> 02:04:47.210
the forms we exist in, was a meta comment,
rejecting the laws of society in order for

02:04:47.210 --> 02:04:49.300
free expression.

02:04:49.300 --> 02:04:54.969
Expression only achieved by art; it all made
me appreciate AWCY’s dedication to anomalous

02:04:54.969 --> 02:04:55.969
artworks.

02:04:55.969 --> 02:04:59.710
Yet, I was still determined to be welcomed
into their collective.

02:04:59.710 --> 02:05:03.489
My first week hiding within Site-19 concluded
with a visit to SCP-107.

02:05:03.489 --> 02:05:09.389
Held on a pedestal in an otherwise empty room
was an empty, hollowed-out turtle shell.

02:05:09.389 --> 02:05:13.369
Now, I, for one, don’t respect archaeologists.

02:05:13.369 --> 02:05:18.230
Art historians are another matter; however,
I would rather focus on translating the human

02:05:18.230 --> 02:05:23.390
experience into my art than waste mental energy
on events or objects from hundreds and thousands

02:05:23.390 --> 02:05:24.909
of years ago.

02:05:24.909 --> 02:05:28.270
But this turtle shell might be the exception.

02:05:28.270 --> 02:05:33.390
Apparently, any liquid placed within the shell
will be absorbed, only to rain down from the

02:05:33.390 --> 02:05:34.560
clouds above.

02:05:34.560 --> 02:05:39.690
There’s something quite honest about SCP-107;
interpreted through the mind of an artist

02:05:39.690 --> 02:05:44.659
such as myself, it appears to be a reminder
of the natural world’s cyclical process.

02:05:44.659 --> 02:05:49.369
The liquid within it becomes rain, which falls
and feeds the plants.

02:05:49.369 --> 02:05:55.280
The plant life in turn provides food and shelter
for animals, humans too, and so on.

02:05:55.280 --> 02:06:00.570
On and on, the circle of life repeats, as
so embodied by the hollow shell of an ancient,

02:06:00.570 --> 02:06:01.570
proud turtle.

02:06:01.570 --> 02:06:06.690
I began my second week of subterfuge with
a scare, a close call that I may have been

02:06:06.690 --> 02:06:07.690
discovered.

02:06:07.690 --> 02:06:11.520
One of the Foundation doctor’s had complained
that his sink had been sliced in two, in spite

02:06:11.520 --> 02:06:14.119
of my best efforts to cover my tracks.

02:06:14.119 --> 02:06:19.699
It seemed that using his denture glue to repair
the damage SCP-063 had done didn’t quite

02:06:19.699 --> 02:06:22.320
have the effect I had intended.

02:06:22.320 --> 02:06:26.480
Nevertheless, the reaction by the Foundation
seemed to imply that that sort of thing happened

02:06:26.480 --> 02:06:27.480
all the time.

02:06:27.480 --> 02:06:32.719
An announcement was made reminding all researchers
not to toy with The World’s Best Tothbrush,

02:06:32.719 --> 02:06:35.770
“despite how fun it was to slice things
up with”.

02:06:35.770 --> 02:06:40.500
Although they requested the culprit come forward,
I decided to divert the Foundation’s suspicions

02:06:40.500 --> 02:06:45.409
away from me, hinting to a security officer
I had seen Doctor Alto Clef leaving the private

02:06:45.409 --> 02:06:48.891
bathroom where I had accidentally broken the
sink.

02:06:48.891 --> 02:06:52.630
They seemed to believe he had been capable
of causing the damage.

02:06:52.630 --> 02:06:56.140
Almost being discovered meant I had to reassess
my objective.

02:06:56.140 --> 02:07:01.170
Over the following few days, I tried to sink
into life among the Foundation’s researchers,

02:07:01.170 --> 02:07:06.690
playing the role of a helpful member of personnel
so as not to attract any further suspicions.

02:07:06.690 --> 02:07:11.600
And so, I was mostly concerned with the general
day-to-day tending to anomalies.

02:07:11.600 --> 02:07:15.960
Most of them I encountered during this time
were living creatures, which were of little

02:07:15.960 --> 02:07:17.020
interest to me.

02:07:17.020 --> 02:07:22.680
Although one, by the designation of SCP-049,
sported the rather aesthetically striking

02:07:22.680 --> 02:07:26.199
appearance of a Plague Doctor, complete with
dark robes and a beaked mask.

02:07:26.199 --> 02:07:30.309
At first, I thought him to be quite an amicable,
intelligent fellow.

02:07:30.309 --> 02:07:35.130
That is, until I had a moment alone with him
to ask his opinions on art.

02:07:35.130 --> 02:07:39.920
The Plague Doctor claimed it was ‘of little
importance’ when compared to curing ‘the

02:07:39.920 --> 02:07:40.920
pestilence.

02:07:40.920 --> 02:07:45.600
Naturally, I took that to be a personal insult,
requesting to the Research Head that I be

02:07:45.600 --> 02:07:50.369
transferred away from an anomaly that was
obviously not as intelligent as I first thought.

02:07:50.369 --> 02:07:55.840
Two and a half weeks had passed, and I had
still remained undetected by the Foundation.

02:07:55.840 --> 02:07:58.370
However, I had been forced to reassess my
mission.

02:07:58.370 --> 02:08:03.050
AWCY were hardly likely to accept me into
their group if I had nothing to show for my

02:08:03.050 --> 02:08:04.050
efforts.

02:08:04.050 --> 02:08:09.190
I needed either some materials I could use
to create an anomalous art piece or – preferably

02:08:09.190 --> 02:08:12.309
– something I could claim as my own.

02:08:12.309 --> 02:08:16.620
It wasn’t as if the upper echelons of AWCY
possessed their own detailed files on every

02:08:16.620 --> 02:08:17.620
anomaly at Site-19.

02:08:17.620 --> 02:08:23.929
They would have no idea if something I plucked
from containment was an existing SCP or a

02:08:23.929 --> 02:08:26.260
Stathis Stern original piece.

02:08:26.260 --> 02:08:32.929
So, I set about finding something that could
be my Magnum Opus, my breakthrough into the

02:08:32.929 --> 02:08:38.920
anomalous art community – my key to the
fame, fortune, and artistic acclaim I so obviously

02:08:38.920 --> 02:08:40.230
deserved.

02:08:40.230 --> 02:08:44.710
You may call it plagiarism, using another
anomaly as my own piece, but nobody technically

02:08:44.710 --> 02:08:48.059
owns the objects and entities at Site-19.

02:08:48.059 --> 02:08:53.060
Even the Foundation themselves are more jailors,
and curators, than creators.

02:08:53.060 --> 02:08:58.559
I, however, am a visionary – I just needed
something to prove it.

02:08:58.559 --> 02:09:02.230
My search took me far and wide, all over the
width and breadth of Site-19.

02:09:02.230 --> 02:09:07.449
By the time my first whole month had passed,
I had begun to walk its corridors and pass

02:09:07.449 --> 02:09:10.309
by its containment chambers just like any
other researcher.

02:09:10.309 --> 02:09:14.870
Although, not one who I interacted with had
any clue they were in the presence of the

02:09:14.870 --> 02:09:17.480
man who would reshape the anomalous art world.

02:09:17.480 --> 02:09:22.369
There was an SCP transferred from another
facility that I witnessed during that time.

02:09:22.369 --> 02:09:28.159
According to the Foundation’s files, SCP-066
had previously been a ball of braided yarn

02:09:28.159 --> 02:09:33.320
and ribbon, but had since become an amorphous
ball of meat with functioning eyes.

02:09:33.320 --> 02:09:37.930
What struck me the most about SCP-066 was
the question it asked since transforming.

02:09:37.930 --> 02:09:39.250
“Are you Eric?”

02:09:39.250 --> 02:09:40.739
At first, I thought, I’m not Eric.

02:09:40.739 --> 02:09:47.370
But the longer I pondered it, I began to wonder,
allowing my perception to be challenged.

02:09:47.370 --> 02:09:49.460
That is what art does, after all.

02:09:49.460 --> 02:09:54.400
Perhaps, much in the Marshal Mathers sense
that ‘We are all Stan’, all of us may

02:09:54.400 --> 02:09:56.110
well be Eric too…

02:09:56.110 --> 02:10:02.290
I can recall another incident encountering
some SCP-131s, creatures known as the Eye

02:10:02.290 --> 02:10:04.860
Pods, when one almost tripped me up.

02:10:04.860 --> 02:10:09.739
Their brightly-colored teardrop-shaped bodies
were certainly striking, a rather abstract,

02:10:09.739 --> 02:10:11.960
modernist biomechanical design.

02:10:11.960 --> 02:10:16.219
But it was their eyes that I found myself
ruminating on.

02:10:16.219 --> 02:10:22.550
SCP-131 instances had a single, unblinking
eye, giving a constant gaze without faltering.

02:10:22.550 --> 02:10:27.580
I likened it much to the uncritical, unthinking
eyes of certain audiences, who in the past

02:10:27.580 --> 02:10:32.730
had started at my artwork and had nothing
interesting to say about it.

02:10:32.730 --> 02:10:37.930
In other events, my attempts to avoid detection
may have proven a little detrimental.

02:10:37.930 --> 02:10:42.330
Doctor Clef cornered me, a little perturbed
that I had implicated him in the ‘sink slicing’

02:10:42.330 --> 02:10:43.330
incident.

02:10:43.330 --> 02:10:48.219
He at one point he seemed to imply a desire
to inflict far worse damage with… a ukulele

02:10:48.219 --> 02:10:49.219
of all things.

02:10:49.219 --> 02:10:51.540
Although, I’m sure he was just joking.

02:10:51.540 --> 02:10:53.230
At least, I hope so.

02:10:53.230 --> 02:10:58.579
The whole interaction reminded me of the urgency
of my mission, I still needed my anomalous

02:10:58.579 --> 02:11:03.040
masterpiece, and would need to have an escape
plan in place once I found it.

02:11:03.040 --> 02:11:07.390
During my next stint of time hidden among
the ranks of the Foundation, I tried to broaden

02:11:07.390 --> 02:11:12.140
my own interpretation of what AWCY would consider
to be ‘art’.

02:11:12.140 --> 02:11:16.270
Nothing in their admittedly vaguely defined
rules stated my anomalous piece couldn’t

02:11:16.270 --> 02:11:19.389
be created from a more modern medium.

02:11:19.389 --> 02:11:24.480
Never mind paintings that can depict a person’s
death or a sculpture that moves whenever unobserved,

02:11:24.480 --> 02:11:28.620
what about something like photography, I thought
to myself.

02:11:28.620 --> 02:11:33.290
However, it was around this time that my hopes
for such a piece were seemingly answered,

02:11:33.290 --> 02:11:39.469
in the form of SCP-105, or Iris Thompson as
she introduced herself.

02:11:39.469 --> 02:11:44.010
Iris had a rather unique relationship with
the art of photography.

02:11:44.010 --> 02:11:48.679
She possessed an ability to alter images taken
on a specific Polaroid Express camera from

02:11:48.679 --> 02:11:50.579
1982.

02:11:50.579 --> 02:11:56.920
In the hands of SCP-105, the photographs would
come to life, like slices of real-time frozen

02:11:56.920 --> 02:12:00.420
one moment then moving the second she held
them.

02:12:00.420 --> 02:12:06.440
She could even alter these images, reaching
into the Polaroid to move or retrieve objects.

02:12:06.440 --> 02:12:12.440
It instilled an idea in me, perhaps taking
a polaroid of my chosen anomalous art, would

02:12:12.440 --> 02:12:13.440
be enough.

02:12:13.440 --> 02:12:17.800
That is, until I realized the photographs
were only interactable to Iris, and breaking

02:12:17.800 --> 02:12:21.449
her out of containment was a waste of my skills
as an artist.

02:12:21.449 --> 02:12:26.280
Partway into my third month hiding out in
Site-19, part of me began to wonder if the

02:12:26.280 --> 02:12:29.910
Foundation’s security forces were as formidable
as I’d been led to believe.

02:12:29.910 --> 02:12:35.160
It seemed I barely even needed to hide my
mission to join AWCY, or that I had spent

02:12:35.160 --> 02:12:37.510
so much time in their facility undetected.

02:12:37.510 --> 02:12:42.619
It was on my sixty-eighth day that I decided
to reflect on some of the potential anomalous

02:12:42.619 --> 02:12:45.389
pieces I had encountered so far.

02:12:45.389 --> 02:12:49.570
Frustratingly so, I was still yet to discover
any that would wipe the smug grins off the

02:12:49.570 --> 02:12:53.850
AWCY members, only to replace them with looks
of awe.

02:12:53.850 --> 02:12:59.179
Something in Site-19 would guarantee my spot
among the anomalous art collective, I was

02:12:59.179 --> 02:13:00.270
certain of it.

02:13:00.270 --> 02:13:02.570
I just had to find it.

02:13:02.570 --> 02:13:07.040
A potential candidate presented itself, one
that might have even allowed me to get my

02:13:07.040 --> 02:13:11.190
own back on AWCY for denying my entry for
so long.

02:13:11.190 --> 02:13:18.619
I was shown SCP-3037, a miniature model that
depicted the city of Dubrovnik in Croatia.

02:13:18.619 --> 02:13:22.639
I was watching it and pondering the meaning
behind this sculpture, thinking about why

02:13:22.639 --> 02:13:25.960
it had been made specifically to look like
Dubrovnik, of all places.

02:13:25.960 --> 02:13:31.710
Maybe, I pondered, it was meant to be a commentary
or condemnation of urbanization, given the

02:13:31.710 --> 02:13:37.000
city’s walls keeping its residents contained,
confined, much like how we as humans have

02:13:37.000 --> 02:13:41.770
become trapped by our urban settings, finding
comfort in being confined there.

02:13:41.770 --> 02:13:48.730
However, it was while examining SCP-3037 that
I apparently suffered something of an incident.

02:13:48.730 --> 02:13:54.250
From the moment I touched it, I spoke in only
Serbo-Croatian, believing myself to actually

02:13:54.250 --> 02:13:56.310
be the city of Dubrovnik.

02:13:56.310 --> 02:14:01.099
I awoke a short while later, and was informed
by another researcher that I had been administered

02:14:01.099 --> 02:14:04.809
with an extremely high dose of amnestics to
negate the effects of SCP-3037.

02:14:04.809 --> 02:14:10.560
Although later that night, I recalled having
a dream about some delicious Black Risotto

02:14:10.560 --> 02:14:12.619
with squid ink sauce.

02:14:12.619 --> 02:14:16.909
The Research heads ordered security officers
to gather everyone in the mess hall.

02:14:16.909 --> 02:14:22.489
The Foundation was seemingly aware that they
had an imposter, a charlatan, in their midst

02:14:22.489 --> 02:14:24.540
pretending to be a member of personnel.

02:14:24.540 --> 02:14:28.480
I made sure my facial expression did not betray
that I was the one they sought.

02:14:28.480 --> 02:14:31.680
Not one of these philistines could appreciate
my goal of infiltrating Site-19.

02:14:31.680 --> 02:14:37.940
Not one had the slightest clue how revolutionary
my actions would be for the art world.

02:14:37.940 --> 02:14:42.730
However, although staying vigilant, I became
increasingly aware time might not be on my

02:14:42.730 --> 02:14:44.719
side much longer.

02:14:44.719 --> 02:14:50.340
After overhearing a conversation about something
known as SCP-035, I began conducting my own

02:14:50.340 --> 02:14:52.570
secretive research into what it was.

02:14:52.570 --> 02:14:57.250
I discovered information on the so-called
‘Possessive Mask’ within the Foundation’s

02:14:57.250 --> 02:14:58.250
archives.

02:14:58.250 --> 02:15:03.020
Immediately, my heart skipped a beat, the
porcelain mask that shifted from an exaggerated

02:15:03.020 --> 02:15:08.179
comedic grin to a wide frown; it would have
made for a perfect art piece to present to

02:15:08.179 --> 02:15:10.130
AWCY.

02:15:10.130 --> 02:15:16.010
Much to my annoyance, SCP-035 wasn’t housed
at Site-19, however.

02:15:16.010 --> 02:15:19.940
Over the next few days, I petitioned to have
the Possessive Mask transferred to the facility

02:15:19.940 --> 02:15:21.790
so I could claim it.

02:15:21.790 --> 02:15:24.780
My greatest anomalous art piece stayed out
of reach though.

02:15:24.780 --> 02:15:29.290
I was informed by senior research staff that
the mask posed a potential danger; anyone

02:15:29.290 --> 02:15:34.560
nearby was compelled to wear it and would
thus be taken over by its influence.

02:15:34.560 --> 02:15:38.349
Additionally, questions started to be raised
as to why I was requesting this transfer in

02:15:38.349 --> 02:15:39.440
the first place.

02:15:39.440 --> 02:15:42.139
I was starting to grow concerned that my cover
had been blown.

02:15:42.139 --> 02:15:45.449
By now, it very much seemed that my time was
running out.

02:15:45.449 --> 02:15:50.449
And although it shames me to admit, to say
I handled that notion with grace and professionality…

02:15:50.449 --> 02:15:53.400
well, that would be a lie.

02:15:53.400 --> 02:15:57.040
Suddenly, I found it: the perfect anomalous
art piece.

02:15:57.040 --> 02:16:01.360
While rummaging around the Foundation’s
files, I located one about something known

02:16:01.360 --> 02:16:04.099
as SCP-1379.

02:16:04.099 --> 02:16:08.719
As I read more, I realized it would secure
my spot within AWCY and forever grant me the

02:16:08.719 --> 02:16:10.630
recognition I deserved.

02:16:10.630 --> 02:16:13.300
Even better: it was a painting!

02:16:13.300 --> 02:16:16.599
Such a classical art medium, they’d never
be able to refuse me!

02:16:16.599 --> 02:16:21.880
As I looked at it directly for the first time,
I knew I had found my masterpiece.

02:16:21.880 --> 02:16:26.750
SCP-1379 depicted a clown and two children
on the canvas.

02:16:26.750 --> 02:16:31.980
But the subject of the painting would hardly
matter to AWCY, they only cared that the artwork

02:16:31.980 --> 02:16:33.700
was anomalous.

02:16:33.700 --> 02:16:37.240
And SCP-1379 certainly was.

02:16:37.240 --> 02:16:41.950
This painting would allow me to exact revenge
on the AWCY members who’d failed to recognize

02:16:41.950 --> 02:16:45.689
my artistic genius for so long.

02:16:45.689 --> 02:16:51.840
Anyone that made a critical remark on SCP-1379
– which those AWCY snobs were bound to do

02:16:51.840 --> 02:16:55.580
when they saw it – would first experience
intense pain.

02:16:55.580 --> 02:16:59.390
After twenty minutes of sharp, severe agony,
the sensations would subside.

02:16:59.390 --> 02:17:02.630
But that’d only be the start of it.

02:17:02.630 --> 02:17:08.660
Everyone at the AWCY that dared to criticize
my Magnum Opus, my SCP-1379, would rapidly

02:17:08.660 --> 02:17:10.960
lose their ability to imagine.

02:17:10.960 --> 02:17:15.099
Any of their creativity that had allowed them
to laud their artistic superiority over me

02:17:15.099 --> 02:17:17.080
would cease to exist.

02:17:17.080 --> 02:17:22.380
None of them would ever make anomalous art
again, and they’d surely be forced to finally

02:17:22.380 --> 02:17:27.330
accept that I am, in fact, the greatest artist
of all time.

02:17:27.330 --> 02:17:32.920
From the moment I saw it, I began to plan
my escape, with SCP-1379 in tow.

02:17:32.920 --> 02:17:38.740
A hundred days have passed since I arrived
here, and when I leave, it will be with SCP-1379

02:17:38.740 --> 02:17:39.740
and this journal.

02:17:39.740 --> 02:17:44.370
I’ve not only chronicled how I infiltrated
the SCP Foundation, but also cataloged more

02:17:44.370 --> 02:17:47.920
about anomalous art than anyone at AWCY ever
has.

02:17:47.920 --> 02:17:52.420
Once I’ve lifted that painting from its
containment locker, my old friends at the

02:17:52.420 --> 02:17:55.460
Insurgency have arranged for me to be extracted
from Site-19.

02:17:55.460 --> 02:18:01.729
With SCP-1379, finally I’ll achieve my dream
of-

02:18:01.729 --> 02:18:07.450
This journal – belonging to one Stathis
Sterns – has been seized by the SCP Foundation.

02:18:07.450 --> 02:18:11.810
Sterns was discovered to have infiltrated
Site-19 after only a handful of days.

02:18:11.810 --> 02:18:16.550
However, given that he posed little threat,
and never left the facility, security officers

02:18:16.550 --> 02:18:21.040
were instructed to monitor him closely until
he attempted to escape.

02:18:21.040 --> 02:18:26.030
Aware of intelligence regarding various SCPs
noted within his journal, the Foundation deemed

02:18:26.030 --> 02:18:30.160
Stathis Sterns a security risk after one-hundred
days.

02:18:30.160 --> 02:18:34.670
Subject was arrested attempting to escape
and is to be administered amnestics.

02:18:34.670 --> 02:18:38.240
His journal will remain at Site-19 until further
notice.

02:18:38.240 --> 02:18:42.960
Oh god, I’m back, I made it back and I’m
still alive.

02:18:42.960 --> 02:18:49.880
That… thing, the Old Man, it had been tormenting
me for months, following me, trapping me God

02:18:49.880 --> 02:18:50.880
knows where.

02:18:50.880 --> 02:18:54.950
But I’m out now… and yet somehow that’s
just as bad.

02:18:54.950 --> 02:18:56.240
Let me explain…

02:18:56.240 --> 02:19:00.319
It all really started about a hundred days
ago, I didn’t start keeping a tally of the

02:19:00.319 --> 02:19:03.750
days until a little after things started to
get strange.

02:19:03.750 --> 02:19:06.910
But if I think back, the first sign happened
on a completely normal day.

02:19:06.910 --> 02:19:09.700
I had been stocking up on supplies with my
eldest, Jimmy.

02:19:09.700 --> 02:19:14.210
The superstore in town had a limit on how
much you could spend in a single transaction,

02:19:14.210 --> 02:19:17.700
so we had to hit up a few different places
for canned food to store in the bunker.

02:19:17.700 --> 02:19:20.880
When we got back, my wife Janey said she’d
noticed a stain upstairs.

02:19:20.880 --> 02:19:25.730
Sure enough, she pointed it out to me; a slightly
discolored patch on the ceiling above our

02:19:25.730 --> 02:19:26.730
bed.

02:19:26.730 --> 02:19:31.410
There wasn’t much there at first, it just
looked like a little area of damp, but I promised

02:19:31.410 --> 02:19:33.090
I’d have a look at it over the weekend.

02:19:33.090 --> 02:19:36.710
No point wasting money on a handyman, I didn’t
think much of it.

02:19:36.710 --> 02:19:41.120
I definitely didn’t think of it as anything
to worry about… but I was so, so wrong.

02:19:41.120 --> 02:19:46.590
The very next day, I was outside, making a
full inventory of everything in the bunker.

02:19:46.590 --> 02:19:51.030
We had everything, all kinds of practical
equipment for any manner of disaster.

02:19:51.030 --> 02:19:56.170
Not just food and water, but ropes and tools,
tents and sleeping bags in case my family

02:19:56.170 --> 02:19:58.910
and I ever needed to abandon the bunker and
sleep in the wild.

02:19:58.910 --> 02:20:04.000
You see, my whole life I’d been sure that
something was coming, that out on the horizon,

02:20:04.000 --> 02:20:05.660
the end of the world was waiting.

02:20:05.660 --> 02:20:09.850
The way I saw it, if civilization was one
day going to completely collapse, then I needed

02:20:09.850 --> 02:20:13.060
to prepare to make sure my family and I made
it through.

02:20:13.060 --> 02:20:17.550
Maybe part of it was the fear too, but when
you’re scared of something, you make yourself

02:20:17.550 --> 02:20:18.840
ready for it, right?

02:20:18.840 --> 02:20:22.311
Anyway, it was while I was listing the food
rations and trying to figure out just how

02:20:22.311 --> 02:20:25.970
many months we’d survive on them, when Janey
came in from the house.

02:20:25.970 --> 02:20:28.270
“Jake, that stain on the ceiling’s getting
bigger!”

02:20:28.270 --> 02:20:30.670
she declared, dragging me back indoors to
show me.

02:20:30.670 --> 02:20:34.650
As far as I could see, it hadn’t changed
much since the day before.

02:20:34.650 --> 02:20:37.840
The day after that though, that was a different
story.

02:20:37.840 --> 02:20:41.580
Janey didn’t have to point it out, I could
clearly tell that something wasn’t right

02:20:41.580 --> 02:20:43.340
about that mark on our ceiling.

02:20:43.340 --> 02:20:45.570
It wasn’t just bigger, it was leaking.

02:20:45.570 --> 02:20:49.930
But not just water, it didn’t seem like
it had come from a leaky pipe somewhere in

02:20:49.930 --> 02:20:50.930
the house.

02:20:50.930 --> 02:20:55.740
There was a dark, gooey substance, almost
like tar, that looked as if it had bled through

02:20:55.740 --> 02:20:59.069
the surface of the ceiling from the floor
above.

02:20:59.069 --> 02:21:04.030
Except, that was the weird thing: there was
no floor above, and our bedroom was on the

02:21:04.030 --> 02:21:05.970
highest level of the house.

02:21:05.970 --> 02:21:07.573
Apart from the attic, of course.

02:21:07.573 --> 02:21:10.920
I climbed up on the bed to take a closer look
at the greasy patch that was now the size

02:21:10.920 --> 02:21:12.650
of two dinner plates.

02:21:12.650 --> 02:21:16.600
Wearing a thick pair of gloves, I reached
up to wipe away some of the muck.

02:21:16.600 --> 02:21:21.580
Immediately, I could smell something burning;
the fingers of my glove had started to melt.

02:21:21.580 --> 02:21:24.270
I ran into the bathroom, and pushed it off
my hand from the wrist.

02:21:24.270 --> 02:21:29.550
By now, this black ooze had started to spread
much further across the ceiling, reaching

02:21:29.550 --> 02:21:31.210
to just above our bed.

02:21:31.210 --> 02:21:35.121
Janey told me she didn’t feel comfortable
sleeping under whatever that stuff was, after

02:21:35.121 --> 02:21:36.121
it had eaten through my gloves.

02:21:36.121 --> 02:21:40.520
I told her she didn’t need to worry, it
wasn’t dripping from the ceiling, and we’d

02:21:40.520 --> 02:21:42.680
be fine as long as we just didn’t touch
it.

02:21:42.680 --> 02:21:46.160
Even then, she still opted to sleep on the
couch.

02:21:46.160 --> 02:21:48.300
That night, I had a really weird dream.

02:21:48.300 --> 02:21:52.580
I was convinced that I’d woken up in the
middle of the night, restless and uneasy knowing

02:21:52.580 --> 02:21:54.790
Janey wasn’t in the bed next to me.

02:21:54.790 --> 02:21:59.109
That’s when I looked up at that slick patch
of darkness smeared across the ceiling.

02:21:59.109 --> 02:22:05.280
But there was something else there too: a
face, a grotesque face staring down at me,

02:22:05.280 --> 02:22:07.420
grinning a wide, lipless smile.

02:22:07.420 --> 02:22:11.250
I’m sure it was a dream, it had to be, I’d
been putting off doing anything about the

02:22:11.250 --> 02:22:13.899
stain, that’s why I dreamt about it, right?

02:22:13.899 --> 02:22:17.100
Or maybe I was awake and it was just a trick
of the light…

02:22:17.100 --> 02:22:19.250
“What do you mean you won’t call someone?”

02:22:19.250 --> 02:22:20.360
Janey asked indignantly.

02:22:20.360 --> 02:22:22.000
“I just don’t see the point!”

02:22:22.000 --> 02:22:23.000
I argued.

02:22:23.000 --> 02:22:27.560
We had gotten into something of a heated debate
over what to do about the goop on our ceiling.

02:22:27.560 --> 02:22:31.362
I’d decided not to tell her about my nightmare,
but even then, she was determined that I get

02:22:31.362 --> 02:22:34.250
a handyman or someone to come and take a look
at it.

02:22:34.250 --> 02:22:37.090
“It’d be a waste of money,” I tried
to convince her.

02:22:37.090 --> 02:22:40.530
“Why should we shell out money for someone
when I can just get some disinfectant and

02:22:40.530 --> 02:22:41.630
clean it up myself?”

02:22:41.630 --> 02:22:45.590
“Because you don’t know what it is, even
if you could just wipe it off, it might come

02:22:45.590 --> 02:22:46.940
back!” she yelled.

02:22:46.940 --> 02:22:51.040
“You’d rather try to sort it out quickly
so you can get back to working on your bunker!”

02:22:51.040 --> 02:22:52.770
“That’s to keep us safe!”

02:22:52.770 --> 02:22:53.880
I retorted.

02:22:53.880 --> 02:22:54.990
“Look, Jake.

02:22:54.990 --> 02:22:59.140
I’ve humored it for long enough, but you
spend more time out there than looking after

02:22:59.140 --> 02:23:01.460
your own house,” Janey sighed.

02:23:01.460 --> 02:23:06.490
I refused, I knew it was the stubborn, petty
thing to do, but I had taken Janey’s comments

02:23:06.490 --> 02:23:08.050
about my bunker to heart.

02:23:08.050 --> 02:23:12.149
She’d never minded before, always understood
that I was only doing it for us.

02:23:12.149 --> 02:23:17.069
I think she saw the good place it came from
first, and that meant more than the actual

02:23:17.069 --> 02:23:21.490
work I’d done stockpiling supplies and making
sure we’d have working plumbing after the

02:23:21.490 --> 02:23:22.490
collapse.

02:23:22.490 --> 02:23:26.180
Later that day, I caught both Jimmy and Jake
Jr were lingering at the bedroom door, staring

02:23:26.180 --> 02:23:28.790
up at the thick, dark sludge on the ceiling.

02:23:28.790 --> 02:23:32.521
The pair of them were both keeping themselves
behind the doorframe or gripping the door

02:23:32.521 --> 02:23:33.939
itself like a shield.

02:23:33.939 --> 02:23:36.930
My boys were scared of it, it was clear as
day.

02:23:36.930 --> 02:23:38.540
“Dad, is the house sick?”

02:23:38.540 --> 02:23:39.870
Jake Jr asked.

02:23:39.870 --> 02:23:42.779
“Houses don’t get sick,” his older brother
interjected.

02:23:42.779 --> 02:23:43.779
“It’s haunted…

02:23:43.779 --> 02:23:45.950
there’s a monster in the attic, right Dad?”

02:23:45.950 --> 02:23:50.880
“No monsters and the house isn’t sick,”
I replied, forcing a smile to my sons.

02:23:50.880 --> 02:23:52.530
“I’ll take care of it.”

02:23:52.530 --> 02:23:56.550
About a week after the stain had first appeared,
it was time for the monthly drill.

02:23:56.550 --> 02:23:59.860
Every four weeks, I’d gather my family and
we’d do a little practice just in case the

02:23:59.860 --> 02:24:00.860
worst should happen.

02:24:00.860 --> 02:24:04.650
At the sound of an air horn, we’d all have
to grab the bug out bags I’d prepared for

02:24:04.650 --> 02:24:07.440
everyone, and run out to the bunker in under
five minutes.

02:24:07.440 --> 02:24:11.270
The boys were always eager, it helped that
they were both young enough that we could

02:24:11.270 --> 02:24:12.270
treat it like a game.

02:24:12.270 --> 02:24:16.470
Janey, though, she didn’t want to get involved
in this drill.

02:24:16.470 --> 02:24:19.720
She stayed indoors, trying to play it off
to the kids as if she was just busy.

02:24:19.720 --> 02:24:25.461
But the side glance she shot at me said different,
and it was hard to avoid the guilt that came

02:24:25.461 --> 02:24:26.461
with it.

02:24:26.461 --> 02:24:28.800
That, coupled with seeing the boy’s reaction
to it, convinced me that it would just be

02:24:28.800 --> 02:24:32.080
easier to have someone come take a look at
the slimy patch above the bed.

02:24:32.080 --> 02:24:36.950
I’d needed to go to town the next day, another
supply run, so I picked up the number of a

02:24:36.950 --> 02:24:40.600
repair guy while I was out, even called him
on the way home, thinking that would make

02:24:40.600 --> 02:24:41.600
it up to Janey.

02:24:41.600 --> 02:24:45.641
But as I pulled up to the driveway, I could
hear screaming coming from the house.

02:24:45.641 --> 02:24:49.359
I raced indoors and that’s when I saw my
wife, standing between our two boys and…

02:24:49.359 --> 02:24:50.359
it.

02:24:50.359 --> 02:24:55.460
It was like a desiccated corpse, an Old Man
with viscous black grease leaking out of his

02:24:55.460 --> 02:24:56.870
decaying body.

02:24:56.870 --> 02:25:02.120
The same substance that had pooled on our
bedroom ceiling… and was now covering Janey.

02:25:02.120 --> 02:25:06.210
The Old Man had her arm gripped in his body
hand, the same fluid coating her skin and

02:25:06.210 --> 02:25:07.210
burning her.

02:25:07.210 --> 02:25:12.420
She was shrieking in pain, tears running down
her face as the boys cowered behind her.

02:25:12.420 --> 02:25:14.870
I froze, I had no idea what to do… so I
ran.

02:25:14.870 --> 02:25:18.779
I had acted out of panic, and spent the next
week regretting it deeper than any mistake

02:25:18.779 --> 02:25:19.779
I’d ever made.

02:25:19.779 --> 02:25:23.511
I’d left my bugout bag in the house and
just blindly sprinted straight for the bunker

02:25:23.511 --> 02:25:25.640
while the screams of my wife and kids filled
the house.

02:25:25.640 --> 02:25:28.710
In the days that followed, everything fell
quiet, but even then, I barely slept.

02:25:28.710 --> 02:25:33.729
Through a porthole in the heavy metal door,
I watched the rear of my home.

02:25:33.729 --> 02:25:38.360
Occasionally I’d catch sight of movement
inside, some tiny bit of motion, usually at

02:25:38.360 --> 02:25:39.360
night.

02:25:39.360 --> 02:25:43.130
I kept hoping it would be Janey, or one of
the boys, having found a place to hide and

02:25:43.130 --> 02:25:45.690
then making their way out the back door to
me.

02:25:45.690 --> 02:25:48.390
But every time, it was only ever him.

02:25:48.390 --> 02:25:53.870
The Old Man slunk through my family’s home,
staring out at me with lidless eyes, his jaw

02:25:53.870 --> 02:25:56.979
locked in a twisted grin like he was enjoying
tormenting me.

02:25:56.979 --> 02:26:00.630
A sharp knock on the bunker door woke me up
from the short, restless sleep.

02:26:00.630 --> 02:26:04.620
I sat bolt upright, immediately expecting
that thing to be on the other side.

02:26:04.620 --> 02:26:08.990
It was the handyman I’d called a few days
earlier – it seemed like such a trivial

02:26:08.990 --> 02:26:09.990
matter now.

02:26:09.990 --> 02:26:12.290
“There was no answer at the front,” he
explained to me.

02:26:12.290 --> 02:26:15.370
“Hope you don’t mind me coming around
the back, your side gate was open.”

02:26:15.370 --> 02:26:17.910
Barely paying him any mind, I dashed towards
the house.

02:26:17.910 --> 02:26:22.660
There was no sign of the Old Man, but I couldn’t
find Janey’s body, or the two boys’ anywhere.

02:26:22.660 --> 02:26:26.940
I went to grab my bugout bag, only to run
into the handyman in the hallway.

02:26:26.940 --> 02:26:30.859
Before I could urge him to run, to leave the
house, the Old Man’s face appeared, pushing

02:26:30.859 --> 02:26:35.580
through another slick, oily black puddle in
the wall, grabbing the other man and dragging

02:26:35.580 --> 02:26:37.859
him in as I bolted for the door.

02:26:37.859 --> 02:26:41.069
Heading for the woods was my safest option
– no, my only option.

02:26:41.069 --> 02:26:45.770
If I went to the police, there was no way
they’d believe such an outlandish story.

02:26:45.770 --> 02:26:49.450
They’d probably suspect me of murdering
my family, or think I was crazy if I told

02:26:49.450 --> 02:26:51.090
them a zombie had attacked my home.

02:26:51.090 --> 02:26:54.280
That got me thinking, what the hell was that
Old Man?

02:26:54.280 --> 02:26:58.800
He couldn’t have been a zombie, for a start
there was only one and he wasn’t turning

02:26:58.800 --> 02:26:59.800
people.

02:26:59.800 --> 02:27:03.140
But there was something off about his actions,
almost like they weren’t mindless shuffling

02:27:03.140 --> 02:27:04.149
and attacks.

02:27:04.149 --> 02:27:05.899
There was an intent behind them.

02:27:05.899 --> 02:27:07.180
He was patient.

02:27:07.180 --> 02:27:11.580
Camping out in the woods brought me no joy,
even with all the supplies in my bag.

02:27:11.580 --> 02:27:15.200
Whenever I tried to sleep, I’d just lay
awake for hours, thinking of my family…

02:27:15.200 --> 02:27:17.800
and the grotesque face of the Old Man.

02:27:17.800 --> 02:27:22.790
Every sound in the night – the wind rustling
leaves, animals cracking sticks underfoot

02:27:22.790 --> 02:27:27.050
– in my mind, everything I heard was him
getting closer and closer.

02:27:27.050 --> 02:27:29.120
And pretty soon he did.

02:27:29.120 --> 02:27:33.109
I started keeping watch during the nights,
and I’d catch sight of a silhouetted figure

02:27:33.109 --> 02:27:34.420
in the darkness.

02:27:34.420 --> 02:27:36.300
Every few days, he would reappear.

02:27:36.300 --> 02:27:40.360
He’d never get close enough to attack me,
just enough to force me to pack up camp again

02:27:40.360 --> 02:27:41.420
and relocate.

02:27:41.420 --> 02:27:45.320
Until one night, I could hear the sound of
gurgled breathing outside my tent.

02:27:45.320 --> 02:27:49.500
I reached for my flashlight, casting its light
over the walking corpse that stood outside.

02:27:49.500 --> 02:27:53.220
It recoiled from the bright light, but as
its decaying face illuminated, I ran, leaving

02:27:53.220 --> 02:27:55.990
my campsite and bag where they were.

02:27:55.990 --> 02:28:01.180
I didn’t care, I just sprinted through the
woods, branches scratching at me like the

02:28:01.180 --> 02:28:03.870
decrepit bony fingers of the Old Man clawing
at my skin.

02:28:03.870 --> 02:28:07.660
I could hear water running, barely seeing
the stream in my flashlight before my foot

02:28:07.660 --> 02:28:08.760
sank into it.

02:28:08.760 --> 02:28:14.000
Wading through, I crawled out on the opposite
bank, cold, wet, and still trying to get away.

02:28:14.000 --> 02:28:18.290
The days that followed were hard, with no
supplies I had to survive on what little I

02:28:18.290 --> 02:28:19.290
had left in my pockets.

02:28:19.290 --> 02:28:24.340
My best bet, I thought, was to keep hiking,
keep putting distance between myself and him.

02:28:24.340 --> 02:28:28.340
And for a while, it was working… or I thought
it was.

02:28:28.340 --> 02:28:34.310
It wasn’t until I came across tracks, not
like animal tracks, not even an ordinary human’s

02:28:34.310 --> 02:28:35.310
footprints.

02:28:35.310 --> 02:28:40.109
They were thick with that black, burning tar,
melting into the forest ground in the shape

02:28:40.109 --> 02:28:41.340
of the Old Man’s foot.

02:28:41.340 --> 02:28:42.810
I’d gone around in a circle.

02:28:42.810 --> 02:28:46.760
The whole time I thought I was getting away
from him, I had just been going around and

02:28:46.760 --> 02:28:47.760
around.

02:28:47.760 --> 02:28:52.250
When I stumbled upon what was still left of
my campsite, I should have been overjoyed,

02:28:52.250 --> 02:28:55.090
but instead, my heart dropped through to the
bottom of my stomach.

02:28:55.090 --> 02:28:59.990
I shouldn’t have stopped there, that was
what he wanted.

02:28:59.990 --> 02:29:02.570
But I was hungry, exhausted, and just needed
to sleep.

02:29:02.570 --> 02:29:06.500
That was when he got me, I woke up to the
stench of some burning substance, only to

02:29:06.500 --> 02:29:11.000
find something locked tightly around my ankle;
one of the Old Man’s hands.

02:29:11.000 --> 02:29:16.170
Another of his greasy gateways had opened
on a nearby tree, and he was pulling me inside.

02:29:16.170 --> 02:29:20.870
The pain was what hit me first, before I could
even get a good look at where I was or what

02:29:20.870 --> 02:29:21.870
was around me.

02:29:21.870 --> 02:29:26.390
Instead, all I felt was the most excruciating
agony I’d ever experienced.

02:29:26.390 --> 02:29:29.030
Looking down, I saw why.

02:29:29.030 --> 02:29:33.330
The Old Man had two of his bony, clawed fingers
hooked into my ankle.

02:29:33.330 --> 02:29:36.720
Not just holding me by it, his fingers were
going through and it hurt.

02:29:36.720 --> 02:29:41.120
The black sludge was still dripping from him
and onto me.

02:29:41.120 --> 02:29:46.800
The more of it passed through my clothes,
touched my skin, and mixed with my blood,

02:29:46.800 --> 02:29:48.000
the more I felt it burning.

02:29:48.000 --> 02:29:52.100
Digging in the fingers of his other hand,
the Old Man pulled apart the bones in my lower

02:29:52.100 --> 02:29:57.630
leg like the wishbone of a turkey, only with
a much louder and far more painful snap.

02:29:57.630 --> 02:30:02.960
The shock left me unconscious again, lost
and now with half of my leg forcibly ripped

02:30:02.960 --> 02:30:03.960
away.

02:30:03.960 --> 02:30:08.350
I had no idea where I was, and for some strange
reason the Old Man had left me to wander around

02:30:08.350 --> 02:30:09.420
freely.

02:30:09.420 --> 02:30:11.149
Although I’d soon find out why.

02:30:11.149 --> 02:30:16.760
It hurt just to move, but I wrapped my leg
up and crawled my way around the vast labyrinth.

02:30:16.760 --> 02:30:21.399
Even if I still had my bag, which had a compass
in it, it still would have been impossible

02:30:21.399 --> 02:30:22.399
to navigate.

02:30:22.399 --> 02:30:27.000
I tried to take shelter under my jacket, hiding
and setting up as much of a camp as I could.

02:30:27.000 --> 02:30:31.570
Every time I thought I was safe, a greasy
black puddle would begin to form on the nearest

02:30:31.570 --> 02:30:35.640
wall, and that same decaying face with the
lipless grin came through it.

02:30:35.640 --> 02:30:40.439
But that was why he let me wander, just so
he could make sure I never slept; my suffering

02:30:40.439 --> 02:30:42.100
was a game to him.

02:30:42.100 --> 02:30:44.569
The longer I spent there, the more tired I
became.

02:30:44.569 --> 02:30:49.649
Through lack of sleep, searing pain of my
forcibly amputated leg, and the fear of trying

02:30:49.649 --> 02:30:52.859
to keep away from the Old Man, I started to
get low.

02:30:52.859 --> 02:30:55.170
I had no way of escaping.

02:30:55.170 --> 02:30:59.500
Even if I tried stepping through one of those
black puddles, it would just corrode me.

02:30:59.500 --> 02:31:03.050
But I started to think, maybe that would’ve
been better.

02:31:03.050 --> 02:31:05.530
Getting home meant having to live without
Janey.

02:31:05.530 --> 02:31:10.399
She would still be gone, Jimmy and Jake Jr
weren’t going to be waiting for me if I

02:31:10.399 --> 02:31:12.070
somehow managed to make it back.

02:31:12.070 --> 02:31:16.609
I had been keeping a tally of the days since
I’d left, adding on the week when the stain

02:31:16.609 --> 02:31:17.609
first appeared.

02:31:17.609 --> 02:31:19.670
It had been ninety days.

02:31:19.670 --> 02:31:21.320
I couldn’t take anymore.

02:31:21.320 --> 02:31:23.279
I just wanted it over.

02:31:23.279 --> 02:31:27.830
I didn’t see the point to continuing this
torture, I wanted to leave and I wanted my

02:31:27.830 --> 02:31:28.930
family back.

02:31:28.930 --> 02:31:32.560
The only way to get both was to let the Old
Man get me.

02:31:32.560 --> 02:31:36.180
But he must’ve known I was looking for him,
every time I yelled out into the confusing

02:31:36.180 --> 02:31:39.260
maze I was stuck in, I never got an answer.

02:31:39.260 --> 02:31:40.930
He wouldn’t come and find me.

02:31:40.930 --> 02:31:45.670
I couldn’t tell if he knew how badly I was
suffering stuck here and just wanted to prolong

02:31:45.670 --> 02:31:47.980
that, or if he’d gotten bored of me.

02:31:47.980 --> 02:31:51.790
Eventually, after over another week without
rest, I tried to sleep again.

02:31:51.790 --> 02:31:54.080
And that was when he came for me.

02:31:54.080 --> 02:31:58.520
Only I didn’t bother to run this time, as
I saw the corpse-like creature shambling towards

02:31:58.520 --> 02:32:00.920
me, black grease covering its body.

02:32:00.920 --> 02:32:03.620
I closed my eyes and waited for it to happen.

02:32:03.620 --> 02:32:07.550
When I came back to consciousness, my vision
was filled with nothing but bright, white

02:32:07.550 --> 02:32:08.550
light.

02:32:08.550 --> 02:32:11.920
I thought that maybe this was it, that the
Old Man had caught me and decided to put an

02:32:11.920 --> 02:32:13.820
end to the chasing and torture.

02:32:13.820 --> 02:32:17.279
For a brief few seconds, I was certain that
I was dead.

02:32:17.279 --> 02:32:20.820
What else are you supposed to think when you
wake up squinting at a blinding light?

02:32:20.820 --> 02:32:24.920
But I wasn’t dead and hadn’t been delivered
to the afterlife.

02:32:24.920 --> 02:32:29.870
And I wasn’t still in that horrible labyrinth,
that nightmarish place I had just been for

02:32:29.870 --> 02:32:31.160
over a month.

02:32:31.160 --> 02:32:35.500
Feeling the cold, hard ground, something in
the pit of my stomach told me I was back in

02:32:35.500 --> 02:32:36.740
the real world.

02:32:36.740 --> 02:32:40.729
Still unable to see properly, I raised a hand
to block out some of the glare, the sounds

02:32:40.729 --> 02:32:42.790
of heavy boots clomping towards me.

02:32:42.790 --> 02:32:47.430
Two pairs of gloved hands reached out and
grabbed my arms, dragging me out of the path

02:32:47.430 --> 02:32:48.710
of the intense light.

02:32:48.710 --> 02:32:52.610
As they did, I started blinking away the spots
in my vision, and that’s when I saw masses

02:32:52.610 --> 02:32:58.540
of machinery and the creature, the Old Man
caught in the beams of three white spotlights.

02:32:58.540 --> 02:33:03.410
I looked up at the people who had saved me,
a pair of figures in tactical gear, their

02:33:03.410 --> 02:33:07.280
uniforms bearing the insignia of something
called the ‘SCP Foundation’.

02:33:07.280 --> 02:33:11.880
As grateful as I was to this Foundation for
pulling me out of that nightmare, and as glad

02:33:11.880 --> 02:33:17.400
as I was to still be alive, I couldn’t help
but feel more and more uneasy as they debriefed

02:33:17.400 --> 02:33:18.400
me.

02:33:18.400 --> 02:33:24.189
Maybe it was just how interested they seemed
in my encounter with the Old Man – or SCP-106,

02:33:24.189 --> 02:33:28.330
as they called it – as I described what
had happened in a lengthy debrief.

02:33:28.330 --> 02:33:32.700
Or it might have been how quick they were
to shrug off what the Old Man had done to

02:33:32.700 --> 02:33:33.700
my family.

02:33:33.700 --> 02:33:38.050
Whatever it was, I didn’t trust them, even
though they seemed to believe my story.

02:33:38.050 --> 02:33:41.220
Recalling it all made me think of Janey and
my two boys again.

02:33:41.220 --> 02:33:46.520
I used to be so afraid of the world ending,
spent all that time and money making sure

02:33:46.520 --> 02:33:48.830
I was ready for when the end finally hit.

02:33:48.830 --> 02:33:54.229
But it had snuck up on me, it didn’t matter
that the Earth was still turning, my world

02:33:54.229 --> 02:33:55.229
was gone.

02:33:55.229 --> 02:33:59.149
And, I figured, that must have been why the
Old Man kept me alive.

02:33:59.149 --> 02:34:05.600
It could’ve easily killed me if it really
wanted to, but it let me live on, not just

02:34:05.600 --> 02:34:10.410
so it could torture me, but so I’d be alive
to know my family were gone.

02:34:10.410 --> 02:34:14.350
It wasn’t long after I realized this that
the Foundation offered me something, medicine

02:34:14.350 --> 02:34:16.510
they said would help me forget about it all.

02:34:16.510 --> 02:34:20.970
If I didn’t take it, I’d at least get
to remember what I used to have, but also

02:34:20.970 --> 02:34:23.600
live with the pain of not having it anymore.

02:34:23.600 --> 02:34:26.550
That was the Old Man’s real torture... so
I had to.

02:34:26.550 --> 02:34:32.030
As I washed down the pill with a swig of water,
I thought back to Janey and my sons with tears

02:34:32.030 --> 02:34:36.250
in my eyes, wishing them goodbye before they
were really gone forever.

02:34:36.250 --> 02:34:40.300
It’s hard to believe it’s been over three
whole months since those monsters came to

02:34:40.300 --> 02:34:41.300
town.

02:34:41.300 --> 02:34:45.370
If only I had known back then what they were
really doing here, maybe I could have stopped

02:34:45.370 --> 02:34:46.370
it.

02:34:46.370 --> 02:34:48.670
I don’t know how, so much of this is beyond
me.

02:34:48.670 --> 02:34:49.779
What could I have done?

02:34:49.779 --> 02:34:53.870
Even if I had known about it, could I have
stopped what happened to all those people…

02:34:53.870 --> 02:34:56.420
Sickness, Sarkicism, the SCP Foundation.

02:34:56.420 --> 02:35:03.040
It’s all still such a blur, but for a while,
I can still remember how I Spent 100 Days

02:35:03.040 --> 02:35:04.359
Infiltrating a Sarkic Cult.

02:35:04.359 --> 02:35:06.479
It all began on a pretty ordinary day.

02:35:06.479 --> 02:35:11.149
I live in a small lumber town, most of the
area is cut off from major cities, connected

02:35:11.149 --> 02:35:14.260
only by miles and miles of winding road through
the forest.

02:35:14.260 --> 02:35:18.100
It’s a quiet rural place, and a low-tech
way of life.

02:35:18.100 --> 02:35:23.090
Our main business has been timber, generations
of tree cutters; even in the modern age, we

02:35:23.090 --> 02:35:24.090
keep that going.

02:35:24.090 --> 02:35:29.250
Although my home had always been off the beaten
track, we often had our fair share of visitors.

02:35:29.250 --> 02:35:33.290
Hikers and wanderers would sometimes stumble
upon the town quite by accident, yet be so

02:35:33.290 --> 02:35:37.550
enthralled by all the natural beauty on offer
that they’d just have to spend a night at

02:35:37.550 --> 02:35:41.689
the only hotel in town, the ‘Woodsman’s
Lodge’, run by my friend Andreas.

02:35:41.689 --> 02:35:44.340
That’s what we thought they were at first.

02:35:44.340 --> 02:35:45.340
Just visitors.

02:35:45.340 --> 02:35:47.700
That’s why we welcomed them when they arrived.

02:35:47.700 --> 02:35:52.620
Because not one of us had any idea who these
really were… what they really were.

02:35:52.620 --> 02:35:55.800
We could never have known that they had chosen
our town specifically.

02:35:55.800 --> 02:35:58.890
They had come here on purpose.

02:35:58.890 --> 02:36:02.600
Many of the warier among our town thought
these newcomers were strange.

02:36:02.600 --> 02:36:07.390
They were a group of five, very obviously
monied, but less obvious was their reasons

02:36:07.390 --> 02:36:08.390
for coming here.

02:36:08.390 --> 02:36:12.130
When friendly townsfolk tried to drum up a
conversation with any of them, they all seemed

02:36:12.130 --> 02:36:13.430
to report the same thing.

02:36:13.430 --> 02:36:17.950
That these strangers were aloof, and each
one gave a dramatically different answer as

02:36:17.950 --> 02:36:21.479
to why they had come to our quite little logging
community.

02:36:21.479 --> 02:36:25.561
One said their group were just sightseeing,
another said they had gotten lost.

02:36:25.561 --> 02:36:29.240
Someone else from their group told a local
man they were here on a mission from someone

02:36:29.240 --> 02:36:34.630
called Karcist Varis, and was rumored to have
been quickly silenced by another in the visitors’

02:36:34.630 --> 02:36:35.630
party.

02:36:35.630 --> 02:36:39.460
But the one thing they all seemingly agreed
on was that they wouldn’t be any trouble.

02:36:39.460 --> 02:36:43.720
They insisted on that, in fact, and said the
five of them were just looking for somewhere

02:36:43.720 --> 02:36:44.720
to sleep.

02:36:44.720 --> 02:36:46.980
Bed and board in exchange for cash.

02:36:46.980 --> 02:36:50.130
And so, someone pointed them in the direction
of Andreas’ hotel.

02:36:50.130 --> 02:36:54.910
I was often there myself, not as a guest,
but helping my friend maintain the place.

02:36:54.910 --> 02:36:59.030
He’d inherited the hotel from his family,
but it was always in need of repair.

02:36:59.030 --> 02:37:02.880
Luckily, my background was in carpentry, and
I was more than happy to help old Andreas

02:37:02.880 --> 02:37:03.880
out.

02:37:03.880 --> 02:37:08.330
It wasn’t one of your fancy, furnished hotels,
the kind you’d find in a big city, all slick

02:37:08.330 --> 02:37:10.120
and sleek and owned by a big chain.

02:37:10.120 --> 02:37:14.010
It was homely, a more bed-and-breakfast type
of place.

02:37:14.010 --> 02:37:15.520
It was, anyway.

02:37:15.520 --> 02:37:19.279
Whenever I was working over at the Woodsman’s,
I’d observe the strange group of visitors

02:37:19.279 --> 02:37:20.279
from a distance.

02:37:20.279 --> 02:37:23.790
They didn’t seem to me like average tourists
or sightseers.

02:37:23.790 --> 02:37:27.110
To me, they looked wealthy, very wealthy.

02:37:27.110 --> 02:37:31.780
It wasn’t hard to picture any one of them
as a politician or a businessman, the way

02:37:31.780 --> 02:37:36.120
they dressed and carried themselves made it
pretty clear they were people of influence.

02:37:36.120 --> 02:37:40.430
Of course, that begged the question: what
on Earth were people like that doing in a

02:37:40.430 --> 02:37:41.430
town like this?

02:37:41.430 --> 02:37:44.029
I first got to speak with one of them after
a few days.

02:37:44.029 --> 02:37:48.180
The five newcomers were down a man, and didn’t
seem at all happy about that.

02:37:48.180 --> 02:37:51.710
The remaining four were grumpy, looking angered
and agitated.

02:37:51.710 --> 02:37:55.850
I wondered if maybe their fifth compatriot
had left in the night, but when I asked around

02:37:55.850 --> 02:37:58.090
town, apparently, nobody had seen him.

02:37:58.090 --> 02:38:01.470
“Unless he’s crazy and decided to hike
through the woods on his own in the middle

02:38:01.470 --> 02:38:05.550
of the night,” one of my friends scoffed,
after telling me that no one had even heard

02:38:05.550 --> 02:38:07.750
any cars leaving the night before.

02:38:07.750 --> 02:38:11.580
I decided to ask one of the visitors when
he passed me in the hallway of the Woodsman’s

02:38:11.580 --> 02:38:14.189
Lodge, trying to broach the topic as tactfully
as I could.

02:38:14.189 --> 02:38:17.040
“Sorry to see you’re down a man,” I
told him.

02:38:17.040 --> 02:38:20.560
He stopped in his tracks, like I’d just
caught him doing something he shouldn’t

02:38:20.560 --> 02:38:21.560
be.

02:38:21.560 --> 02:38:24.710
“Yes, yes,” he replied, not turning around
to face me.

02:38:24.710 --> 02:38:26.120
“It’s a terrible shame.

02:38:26.120 --> 02:38:27.529
A terrible shame indeed.

02:38:27.529 --> 02:38:28.710
But he had to leave us.

02:38:28.710 --> 02:38:30.460
It was for his own good.

02:38:30.460 --> 02:38:31.479
He knew that.

02:38:31.479 --> 02:38:32.720
We all do.”

02:38:32.720 --> 02:38:36.260
Following that odd encounter, things got all
the stranger the following day.

02:38:36.260 --> 02:38:39.899
I arrived at the Woodsman’s again, expecting
to start another day of work.

02:38:39.899 --> 02:38:43.800
I’d gotten a call from Andreas earlier that
morning; he was complaining about some kind

02:38:43.800 --> 02:38:48.271
of squelching noise coming from the basement
under the hotel, that had only started last

02:38:48.271 --> 02:38:49.271
night.

02:38:49.271 --> 02:38:51.319
I told him I wasn’t a plumber, but that
I’d take a look.

02:38:51.319 --> 02:38:55.891
So, imagine my surprise when I saw Andreas
loading most of his worldly possessions up

02:38:55.891 --> 02:38:58.060
into a truck outside the front of his hotel.

02:38:58.060 --> 02:39:02.069
He excitedly explained to me that he’d been
bought out, that the remaining four newcomers

02:39:02.069 --> 02:39:04.560
had offered to buy the Woodsman’s on the
spot.

02:39:04.560 --> 02:39:06.399
“But that’s your family’s hotel, Andreas!”

02:39:06.399 --> 02:39:08.740
I reminded him, as if it wasn’t already
too late.

02:39:08.740 --> 02:39:11.250
“I was never going to be able to keep it
afloat forever,” he shrugged.

02:39:11.250 --> 02:39:14.300
“When they told me how much they were willing
to pay, I couldn’t refuse.

02:39:14.300 --> 02:39:15.900
You would have done the same, Ingvar.

02:39:15.900 --> 02:39:18.340
I have never been offered so much money!”

02:39:18.340 --> 02:39:21.670
This all made it very clear: these guests
were here to stay.

02:39:21.670 --> 02:39:25.040
It had taken less than a week for me to grow
suspicious of these people.

02:39:25.040 --> 02:39:29.920
It wasn’t in my nature to pry, but so much
about them just didn’t make any sense.

02:39:29.920 --> 02:39:34.410
They were clearly rich enough to buy Andreas’
hotel like it was nothing, just an ordinary

02:39:34.410 --> 02:39:36.670
everyday purchase like buying a loaf of bread.

02:39:36.670 --> 02:39:41.070
But they were far too insular to seemingly
want to run a hotel for our friendly logging

02:39:41.070 --> 02:39:42.070
town.

02:39:42.070 --> 02:39:45.979
It left me scratching my head over the whole
thing, making the question of what they were

02:39:45.979 --> 02:39:48.770
really up to stick out like an unignorable
splinter.

02:39:48.770 --> 02:39:52.529
Were these just careless rich folk with more
money than sense?

02:39:52.529 --> 02:39:56.930
Had they somehow grown disillusioned with
their wealthy affluent lifestyles and were

02:39:56.930 --> 02:40:00.910
now using this hotel to learn how to connect
with people on a more fulfilling personal

02:40:00.910 --> 02:40:01.910
level?

02:40:01.910 --> 02:40:05.771
Or did they have a different agenda, something
else that they were trying to hide?

02:40:05.771 --> 02:40:07.979
It turns out, they did…

02:40:07.979 --> 02:40:11.830
I decided to formally introduce myself to
the new owners of the Woodsman’s.

02:40:11.830 --> 02:40:15.450
After all, I knew the place well; it always
needed something repairing.

02:40:15.450 --> 02:40:19.860
Plus, if I could keep my little side job of
working there as a handyman going, then maybe

02:40:19.860 --> 02:40:21.890
I could find out exactly what they were doing.

02:40:21.890 --> 02:40:25.430
I was greeted by the man of their group I’d
spoken to briefly the other day, looking at

02:40:25.430 --> 02:40:28.280
me with a scowl as I stood in the doorway.

02:40:28.280 --> 02:40:29.280
“Hello, sir!”

02:40:29.280 --> 02:40:30.880
I greeted warmly, met with silence.

02:40:30.880 --> 02:40:32.790
“We, uh, we met the other day?”

02:40:32.790 --> 02:40:34.710
“What do you want?”

02:40:34.710 --> 02:40:37.670
“Oh, well,” I continued, disarmed by the
bluntness.

02:40:37.670 --> 02:40:40.351
“I understand your group just bought this
place.

02:40:40.351 --> 02:40:42.350
I had a gentlemen’s agreement with the last
owner.

02:40:42.350 --> 02:40:44.710
I’d help him out, fix things that need fixing.

02:40:44.710 --> 02:40:47.920
I just thought I should offer the same service
to you if you’d be interested.

02:40:47.920 --> 02:40:51.189
Might help get this hotel up and running nice
and quick for you, so you can start making

02:40:51.189 --> 02:40:52.550
a return on your investment.”

02:40:52.550 --> 02:40:56.300
With that, the door was unceremoniously slammed
in my face.

02:40:56.300 --> 02:41:00.790
At first, it certainly seemed like my options
for uncovering more about this strange group

02:41:00.790 --> 02:41:02.160
were all but depleted.

02:41:02.160 --> 02:41:04.900
Or at least, doing so through any legitimate
means.

02:41:04.900 --> 02:41:09.410
But just as I was reaching a desperate point
where I was considering donning a balaclava

02:41:09.410 --> 02:41:14.030
and breaking into the hotel in the dead of
night, opportunity remerged from behind that

02:41:14.030 --> 02:41:15.040
closed door.

02:41:15.040 --> 02:41:20.210
“We were sorry to turn you away yesterday,”
one of the other visitors apologized somewhat

02:41:20.210 --> 02:41:21.210
insincerely.

02:41:21.210 --> 02:41:24.160
“But we would be delighted to take you up
on your offer.

02:41:24.160 --> 02:41:27.050
Provided you accept a few… conditions.”

02:41:27.050 --> 02:41:28.050
“Conditions?”

02:41:28.050 --> 02:41:29.050
I echoed.

02:41:29.050 --> 02:41:30.470
“What would these be?”

02:41:30.470 --> 02:41:35.311
“We would need you to start on repairs immediately,
we want to open up the hotel’s doors to

02:41:35.311 --> 02:41:39.080
guests in the next few days as a matter of
urgency,” he explained.

02:41:39.080 --> 02:41:41.720
“But you will need to be discreet.

02:41:41.720 --> 02:41:45.530
Privacy is very important to us, so we hope
to maintain that for our guests.”

02:41:45.530 --> 02:41:49.960
It all seemed like a lot of hoops to jump
through, certainly more than Andreas had given

02:41:49.960 --> 02:41:51.970
me when he used to run the Woodsman’s.

02:41:51.970 --> 02:41:55.689
But it felt like the right call, if it at
least got me closer to answers.

02:41:55.689 --> 02:41:58.550
In hindsight, I should’ve turned them down…

02:41:58.550 --> 02:42:01.800
Over the course of the next week, things seemed
to run relatively normally.

02:42:01.800 --> 02:42:06.070
I’d be given a checklist of things to fix
around the hotel, and for the most part, just

02:42:06.070 --> 02:42:08.210
focused purely on the tasks at hand.

02:42:08.210 --> 02:42:13.340
But whenever the strange new owners were near,
I’d try to subtly listen in on their conversations.

02:42:13.340 --> 02:42:16.970
They seemed to mostly talk about running the
hotel though, like they knew I was around.

02:42:16.970 --> 02:42:21.710
A few tourists started showing up, in fact,
it seemed to get much busier than usual very

02:42:21.710 --> 02:42:22.710
quickly.

02:42:22.710 --> 02:42:26.479
I asked one of the odd owners about it, and
he just brushed it off as the result of their

02:42:26.479 --> 02:42:29.200
group marketing the Woodsman’s Lodge more
widely on the Internet.

02:42:29.200 --> 02:42:34.040
I didn’t see much of the hotel’s guests,
staying out of their way as I had been instructed

02:42:34.040 --> 02:42:35.040
to.

02:42:35.040 --> 02:42:38.270
But it did strike me as odd how few of them
seemed to leave.

02:42:38.270 --> 02:42:42.899
Some I spotted around town, usually those
traveling as a couple or in a larger group.

02:42:42.899 --> 02:42:47.109
Any that were staying alone though, they didn’t
seem to venture beyond the Woodsman’s.

02:42:47.109 --> 02:42:51.939
None of them checked out either, but their
rooms would be empty, and often needed repairs

02:42:51.939 --> 02:42:55.189
– mainly scratch marks on the wooden floorboards.

02:42:55.189 --> 02:42:58.811
Two and a half weeks after the weird group
had arrived and bought up my friend’s hotel,

02:42:58.811 --> 02:43:01.830
I got a call from someone I knew who lived
next door to the Woodsman’s.

02:43:01.830 --> 02:43:04.270
“The hotel, someone was screaming!”

02:43:04.270 --> 02:43:05.609
she said frantically over the line.

02:43:05.609 --> 02:43:07.170
“Get here quick, they might need you!”

02:43:07.170 --> 02:43:11.189
I checked the time; it was the dead of night,
so late that it was almost the start of the

02:43:11.189 --> 02:43:12.189
next day.

02:43:12.189 --> 02:43:14.770
Racing quickly over to the hotel, I was greeted
by one of the owners.

02:43:14.770 --> 02:43:18.250
He had rushed to the door, wide awake like
he hadn’t even been sleeping.

02:43:18.250 --> 02:43:21.700
When I told him there were reports of blood-curdling
screams, he reprimanded me.

02:43:21.700 --> 02:43:25.750
“You were told, our guests’ privacy must
be maintained!” he scolded.

02:43:25.750 --> 02:43:29.830
“If you must know, one of the hikers staying
with us tripped over and had a nasty fall.

02:43:29.830 --> 02:43:34.560
He landed downstairs, in the basement, and
he’s in a lot of pain, not to be moved.

02:43:34.560 --> 02:43:35.840
We’ve called for an ambulance.”

02:43:35.840 --> 02:43:39.721
I wanted to take what he had said at face
value, but the fact he wouldn’t let me in

02:43:39.721 --> 02:43:41.920
to see the injured man felt suspicious.

02:43:41.920 --> 02:43:45.920
I spent the night with the person who lived
next door to the hotel, watching the Woodsman’s

02:43:45.920 --> 02:43:47.720
Lodge for any sign of an ambulance.

02:43:47.720 --> 02:43:49.290
It never arrived.

02:43:49.290 --> 02:43:53.360
That was when I remembered: he had said the
guest fell into the basement.

02:43:53.360 --> 02:43:57.100
The same basement Andreas had heard a strange
squelching coming from.

02:43:57.100 --> 02:44:01.250
And not once had these new owners asked me
to take a look down there…

02:44:01.250 --> 02:44:04.150
Already I was even further suspicious of the
new owners now.

02:44:04.150 --> 02:44:08.279
I had no idea what they were up to, the wild
theories in my head ranged from them being

02:44:08.279 --> 02:44:13.330
a group of sadistic serial killers using the
hotel to lure victims, or a shadowy government

02:44:13.330 --> 02:44:16.040
black ops team up to something nefarious.

02:44:16.040 --> 02:44:17.979
Enough was enough, I needed answers.

02:44:17.979 --> 02:44:21.430
I couldn’t wait for an opportunity, I had
to create one.

02:44:21.430 --> 02:44:25.790
While as few people were in the hotel as possible,
I snuck into the main office.

02:44:25.790 --> 02:44:30.081
Not to steal anything or even peek just yet,
but to break the legs off a desk; if they

02:44:30.081 --> 02:44:34.359
asked me to fix it, then I would be able to
spend longer in the office with no supervision.

02:44:34.359 --> 02:44:38.790
Sure enough, they soon asked me I’d be able
to fix the desk, and that was my in.

02:44:38.790 --> 02:44:43.220
I started rummaging through the filing cabinets,
rifling through whatever I could find.

02:44:43.220 --> 02:44:47.420
Old expenses reports, legal documents left
behind by Andreas, but there was nothing that

02:44:47.420 --> 02:44:50.830
could give me any inkling as to what was actually
going on.

02:44:50.830 --> 02:44:52.109
Until there was…

02:44:52.109 --> 02:44:57.040
You see, as much as it came as a surprise
to no one more than me, I wasn’t the only

02:44:57.040 --> 02:44:59.730
one looking into this strange group…

02:44:59.730 --> 02:45:02.670
One of the guests entered the office and quickly
realized what he was doing.

02:45:02.670 --> 02:45:06.740
At first, he seemed to imply that I was somehow
in league with the new owners.

02:45:06.740 --> 02:45:10.750
But I was able to talk him around, telling
him I just wanted to try to get to the bottom

02:45:10.750 --> 02:45:11.750
of what they were up to.

02:45:11.750 --> 02:45:14.149
That was when he revealed to me who he was.

02:45:14.149 --> 02:45:15.970
“Agent Clericson,” he introduced himself.

02:45:15.970 --> 02:45:19.030
“I’m here on behalf of the SCP Foundation.”

02:45:19.030 --> 02:45:22.730
After he brought me up to speed on what the
hell that all meant, we arranged to reconvene

02:45:22.730 --> 02:45:24.870
somewhere they wouldn’t overhear us.

02:45:24.870 --> 02:45:29.510
Meeting up at the lumber mill, he explained
that these four strangers were a cult, a subsect

02:45:29.510 --> 02:45:32.320
of a strange religion I had never heard of
called Sarkicism.

02:45:32.320 --> 02:45:38.010
But after Agent Clericson described their
practices, I wouldn’t soon forget; ritualistic

02:45:38.010 --> 02:45:42.120
sacrifices, cannibalism, and even far, far
worse than that…

02:45:42.120 --> 02:45:44.580
it made me feel sick just hearing about it.

02:45:44.580 --> 02:45:48.710
Over the next few days, Clericson agreed to
let me help in his investigation, offering

02:45:48.710 --> 02:45:52.970
me the chance to help the Foundation and make
it so that nobody in my remote little logging

02:45:52.970 --> 02:45:56.790
town would ever have to suffer the memory
of what the Sarkic cult were up to.

02:45:56.790 --> 02:45:58.710
Little did I realize he also meant me.

02:45:58.710 --> 02:46:03.610
I did my best to spy on the death and disease
worshipping cultists without drawing too much

02:46:03.610 --> 02:46:07.210
suspicion and reported my findings back to
the Foundation agent.

02:46:07.210 --> 02:46:11.720
But it quickly became clear that he was unimpressed
with my current method of just eavesdropping,

02:46:11.720 --> 02:46:15.380
so instead, he suggested a more direct approach.

02:46:15.380 --> 02:46:19.569
Clericson gave me a book on the Sarkic religion,
telling me to memorize as much as I could

02:46:19.569 --> 02:46:24.120
and then approach the cultists as if I shared
in their twisted beliefs.

02:46:24.120 --> 02:46:28.410
Even reading the teachings of Grand Karcist
Ion was enough to make my stomach turn.

02:46:28.410 --> 02:46:32.630
All the grotesque things that the founder
of this cult’s beliefs preached in order

02:46:32.630 --> 02:46:35.760
to achieve something akin to godhood… it
was horrific.

02:46:35.760 --> 02:46:39.350
What was much more horrific, though, was that
our plan went awry.

02:46:39.350 --> 02:46:43.550
I asked to speak alone with one of the cultists,
and instantly started explaining that I had

02:46:43.550 --> 02:46:47.979
observed them for a time and come to realize
that we were one and the same.

02:46:47.979 --> 02:46:52.270
In a state of panic, I regurgitated anything
I could still remember from reading about

02:46:52.270 --> 02:46:57.200
Sarkicism, their goal to achieve apotheosis
through will, the consumption of gods, and…

02:46:57.200 --> 02:46:58.200
sacrifice.

02:46:58.200 --> 02:47:01.750
But I must have said something wrong; one
of them snuck up behind me and knocked me

02:47:01.750 --> 02:47:02.780
unconscious.

02:47:02.780 --> 02:47:06.149
I woke up in a daze, a splitting headache
running through my skull.

02:47:06.149 --> 02:47:09.820
I was in a cage, in a part of the hotel I
hadn’t seen before.

02:47:09.820 --> 02:47:12.410
It was dark, absent of natural light.

02:47:12.410 --> 02:47:17.370
There were others too, I recognized one or
two of the hotel’s single guests, also trapped

02:47:17.370 --> 02:47:18.710
in cages.

02:47:18.710 --> 02:47:20.840
Agent Clericson was nowhere to be found.

02:47:20.840 --> 02:47:24.279
The Sarkic cultists spent the next day asking
me how I had found them.

02:47:24.279 --> 02:47:30.710
I told them the truth, and answered their
questions the only way I knew how, but I couldn’t

02:47:30.710 --> 02:47:31.710
make them believe me.

02:47:31.710 --> 02:47:32.710
“Liar!” one spat.

02:47:32.710 --> 02:47:33.710
“You did your homework, I’ll admit.

02:47:33.710 --> 02:47:38.860
But the Foundation is known for working in
broad strokes, missing out on the finer details.”

02:47:38.860 --> 02:47:41.380
“We’re Neo-Sarkites,” another explained.

02:47:41.380 --> 02:47:45.680
“You were spouting the teachings of the
Proto-Sarkic religion.

02:47:45.680 --> 02:47:51.300
We believe the only way of becoming a god,
achieving the apotheosis, is through sheer

02:47:51.300 --> 02:47:52.300
will.”

02:47:52.300 --> 02:47:55.670
“And in terms of sacrifice,” one of the
other cultists chimed in.

02:47:55.670 --> 02:47:59.569
“We’ll sacrifice many in service of us
few.

02:47:59.569 --> 02:48:02.620
But you’ll soon come to witness more of
that.”

02:48:02.620 --> 02:48:07.061
They kept me in the cage for days, without
food and barely any water.

02:48:07.061 --> 02:48:12.080
I felt so drained of energy before long, and
I soon realized why no one had heard the captive

02:48:12.080 --> 02:48:13.521
guests calling out for help.

02:48:13.521 --> 02:48:18.420
They were so weak that they could barely gather
enough strength to shout, and those that did,

02:48:18.420 --> 02:48:21.700
their voices were too weak to reach anyone
above the basement.

02:48:21.700 --> 02:48:26.330
The cultists came and went all throughout
the day, alternating between running the Woodsman’s

02:48:26.330 --> 02:48:30.479
Lodge and coming down into the dark, cramped
and disgusting room below.

02:48:30.479 --> 02:48:35.020
The stench was foul, yet not one of the Sarkic
cultists seemed to mind.

02:48:35.020 --> 02:48:39.930
They would just talk endlessly about the ‘new
age of Flesh’, before dragging their next

02:48:39.930 --> 02:48:43.529
victim out of their cage at night and taking
them into a back room.

02:48:43.529 --> 02:48:48.180
By this point, all the remaining hotel guests
that had been caged up with me were gone.

02:48:48.180 --> 02:48:52.550
I could never fully see what went on in the
other room, but I certainly heard a lot.

02:48:52.550 --> 02:48:57.500
There were muffled screams, followed by chanting
in a language I didn’t understand.

02:48:57.500 --> 02:49:01.950
Then came the noise of something slithering,
all the while the cultists – who had gathered

02:49:01.950 --> 02:49:06.590
in the room with their captive victim – kept
reciting their incantations.

02:49:06.590 --> 02:49:11.510
I had no idea what was going on in there,
but the hotel guests never remerged, just

02:49:11.510 --> 02:49:12.510
the Sarkites.

02:49:12.510 --> 02:49:15.359
I had read enough about their rituals to get
a pretty good idea…

02:49:15.359 --> 02:49:19.790
I had lost track of how long I’d been held
prisoner when Agent Clericson appeared in

02:49:19.790 --> 02:49:20.790
the basement.

02:49:20.790 --> 02:49:24.830
The cultists’ backs had been turned just
long enough for him to slip downstairs undetected.

02:49:24.830 --> 02:49:28.819
For a moment, I thought I was saved, only
for my heart to sink when he told me he couldn’t

02:49:28.819 --> 02:49:29.819
get me out.

02:49:29.819 --> 02:49:34.069
I pleaded with him, but he insisted that he
needed to know exactly what was going on down

02:49:34.069 --> 02:49:35.069
here.

02:49:35.069 --> 02:49:37.900
As he left me on my own, I cursed him under
my breath.

02:49:37.900 --> 02:49:41.100
Before much longer, the cultists decided it
was my turn.

02:49:41.100 --> 02:49:45.989
They dragged me out of my cage and into the
back room, strapping me to a chair.

02:49:45.989 --> 02:49:49.600
I had no strength left to fight back, but
I had plenty of fear.

02:49:49.600 --> 02:49:54.649
There were more cages back here, it was an
old storeroom that they were now keeping…

02:49:54.649 --> 02:49:55.649
nightmares in.

02:49:55.649 --> 02:50:02.320
The things in the cages were disgusting abominations,
masses of bloody, gooey flesh… flesh that

02:50:02.320 --> 02:50:04.000
had once been human.

02:50:04.000 --> 02:50:09.180
I realized these were the hotel guests, the
ones who traveled alone, who never checked

02:50:09.180 --> 02:50:13.061
out… whose fingernails had left scratches
on the floorboards as they were dragged out

02:50:13.061 --> 02:50:15.240
of their rooms by the cultists.

02:50:15.240 --> 02:50:18.939
Using a cattle prod, one of the Sarkites started
directing a creature towards me.

02:50:18.939 --> 02:50:23.859
The horrible mutant was getting closer, an
appendage resembling an arm reaching out at

02:50:23.859 --> 02:50:24.859
me.

02:50:24.859 --> 02:50:28.390
It was going to turn me into one of them,
and I couldn’t stop it.

02:50:28.390 --> 02:50:32.040
Scared for my life, I mustered the little
energy I had left to try and pull away from

02:50:32.040 --> 02:50:35.910
the sickening creature, but just as it was
about to make contact…

02:50:35.910 --> 02:50:37.410
someone kicked down the door.

02:50:37.410 --> 02:50:41.790
Agent Clericson had returned with reinforcements,
members of a Mobile Task Force that promptly

02:50:41.790 --> 02:50:46.080
arrested the Sarkic cultists and sealed the
room containing the monsters for the time

02:50:46.080 --> 02:50:47.080
being.

02:50:47.080 --> 02:50:51.120
He sat down next to me and explained what
had gone on, but I was barely awake enough

02:50:51.120 --> 02:50:53.160
to keep up with all the details.

02:50:53.160 --> 02:50:58.830
Something about “SCP-610 instances” and
“rare to see them this far out of Siberia”.

02:50:58.830 --> 02:51:02.760
But he promised that the SCP Foundation would
be ordering the creatures to be incinerated

02:51:02.760 --> 02:51:03.760
soon.

02:51:03.760 --> 02:51:07.529
The Woodsman’s lodge would have to be burned
down as part of the cover story.

02:51:07.529 --> 02:51:10.450
As for me, I just want to forget the whole
ordeal.

02:51:10.450 --> 02:51:14.930
The Sarkic cult, all the people they turned
into monsters, the horrifying things they

02:51:14.930 --> 02:51:15.930
do, the SCP-610s.

02:51:15.930 --> 02:51:18.530
All of it, I want it all gone.

02:51:18.530 --> 02:51:22.910
Luckily, Agent Clericson has given me something
he says will help with that.

02:51:22.910 --> 02:51:24.489
He called it an amnestic…

02:51:24.489 --> 02:51:27.030
What on earth is going on!?

02:51:27.030 --> 02:51:31.080
One minute, I was just doing my job, protecting
the President of the United States from any

02:51:31.080 --> 02:51:32.080
potential attacks.

02:51:32.080 --> 02:51:37.950
The next thing I knew, thousands of people
were dead, the world was thrown into chaos,

02:51:37.950 --> 02:51:43.280
and this so-called “SCP Foundation” had
declared war on the human race!?

02:51:43.280 --> 02:51:47.440
Okay, okay, let’s get back to Day 1 before
things get too confusing.

02:51:47.440 --> 02:51:48.440
Day 1.

02:51:48.440 --> 02:51:51.050
I’m a member of the President’s secret
service, so it’s my job to keep POTUS safe

02:51:51.050 --> 02:51:52.439
from all manner of potential dangers.

02:51:52.439 --> 02:52:04.510
My son, Thomas, was in the care of my ex-wife
across the state when everything went south.

02:52:04.510 --> 02:52:15.479
It started on the white house lawn, where
the president was giving a speech about his

02:52:15.479 --> 02:52:19.649
plans to shrink the deficit.

02:52:19.649 --> 02:52:23.200
That’s when he appeared.

02:52:23.200 --> 02:52:29.000
A stranger slipped into the press junket.

02:52:29.000 --> 02:52:34.330
He was dressed like a British butler.

02:52:34.330 --> 02:52:36.650
It was almost comical.

02:52:36.650 --> 02:52:45.490
Myself and the other agents immediately flagged
him as a potential threat and moved in to

02:52:45.490 --> 02:52:46.800
intercept him.

02:52:46.800 --> 02:52:50.090
That’s when things went nuts.

02:52:50.090 --> 02:52:59.290
This Butler was one of the greatest fighters
I’ve ever seen - He effortlessly disarmed

02:52:59.290 --> 02:53:04.760
the intercepting agents, and while we desperately
tried to wrestle the president off the stage,

02:53:04.760 --> 02:53:12.630
the Butler stole one of my fellow agent’s
pistols, and blew off the president’s head

02:53:12.630 --> 02:53:15.100
with one perfectly-aimed shot.

02:53:15.100 --> 02:53:24.979
Of course, we returned fire, mowing the Butler
down in a hail of bullets.

02:53:24.979 --> 02:53:28.820
But it was already too late.

02:53:28.820 --> 02:53:31.860
People were screaming and running.

02:53:31.860 --> 02:53:36.640
I could see, out in the city beyond, there
were explosions.

02:53:36.640 --> 02:53:37.640
Smoke rising.

02:53:37.640 --> 02:53:38.640
A cacophony of screams.

02:53:38.640 --> 02:53:40.080
In an instant, everything had changed.

02:53:40.080 --> 02:53:46.420
We all hunkered down in the White House to
protect the Vice President.

02:53:46.420 --> 02:53:52.300
That’s when, upon turning on the news, we
saw The Message.

02:53:52.300 --> 02:53:55.300
It’d been sent to every company, every station.

02:53:55.300 --> 02:53:56.300
It read:

02:53:56.300 --> 02:54:00.399
The following is a message composed via consensus
of the O5 Council.

02:54:00.399 --> 02:54:04.320
For those who are not currently aware of our
existence, we represent the organization known

02:54:04.320 --> 02:54:06.439
as the SCP Foundation.

02:54:06.439 --> 02:54:14.510
Our previous mission centered around the containment
and study of anomalous objects, entities,

02:54:14.510 --> 02:54:15.720
and other assorted phenomena.

02:54:15.720 --> 02:54:21.510
This mission was the focus of our organization
for more than one hundred years.

02:54:21.510 --> 02:54:25.870
Due to circumstances outside of our control,
this directive has now changed.

02:54:25.870 --> 02:54:33.149
Our new mission will be the extermination
of the human race.

02:54:33.149 --> 02:54:36.180
There will be no further communication.

02:54:36.180 --> 02:54:37.189
Day 2.

02:54:37.189 --> 02:54:46.790
Of course, I tried to get through to my ex-wife
and check that she and Thomas were okay, but

02:54:46.790 --> 02:54:50.830
by that time, communication had already broken
down.

02:54:50.830 --> 02:54:58.649
Strangely, the internet and major TV news
channels remained in operation, allowing them

02:54:58.649 --> 02:55:04.510
to spew a constant barrage of the horrors
going on outside.

02:55:04.510 --> 02:55:09.410
All over the world, hideous monsters and malicious
creatures had flooded the streets, killing

02:55:09.410 --> 02:55:11.109
human beings en masse.

02:55:11.109 --> 02:55:20.979
I don’t understand, is the SCP Foundation
behind all this?

02:55:20.979 --> 02:55:33.431
I hadn’t even heard of them until yesterday,
and now, they were apparently releasing living

02:55:33.431 --> 02:55:43.489
nightmares everywhere, apparently with the
intent of wiping out the human race.

02:55:43.489 --> 02:55:54.310
All I could do was stay in place, hope my
family was staying safe, and do what I can

02:55:54.310 --> 02:55:57.040
to protect the Vice President.

02:55:57.040 --> 02:55:58.130
Day 3.

02:55:58.130 --> 02:56:00.290
The Vice President is dead.

02:56:00.290 --> 02:56:08.689
The same could be said for the rest of my
team.

02:56:08.689 --> 02:56:20.240
Some terrifying, zombie-like man somehow breached
our security perimeter and melted down the

02:56:20.240 --> 02:56:28.600
president and six secret service agents into
a thick, black sludge.

02:56:28.600 --> 02:56:32.410
Obviously, not an ideal situation.

02:56:32.410 --> 02:56:43.819
At that point, the chain of command collapsed
entirely and we decided to make a run for

02:56:43.819 --> 02:56:44.819
it.

02:56:44.819 --> 02:56:53.750
Bullets didn’t seem to work on that rotting
old freak, and I’m not the kind of idiot

02:56:53.750 --> 02:56:57.960
who sees the point in expending a few extra
magazines on a lost cause.

02:56:57.960 --> 02:57:05.590
But by the time I was outside, it was already
nightfall, and the horrors were just beginning…

02:57:05.590 --> 02:57:06.859
Day 4.

02:57:06.859 --> 02:57:09.510
It’s hell on earth out here.

02:57:09.510 --> 02:57:19.109
I was wandering the streets of DC, trying
my best to remain scarce despite the chaos

02:57:19.109 --> 02:57:21.870
unfolding all around me.

02:57:21.870 --> 02:57:25.770
There were these creatures - abominations
of human flesh, twisted into unimaginably

02:57:25.770 --> 02:57:28.939
unnatural configurations - roaming around,
attacking any humans they saw.

02:57:28.939 --> 02:57:32.870
And anyone they touched started to transform
into more of them.

02:57:32.870 --> 02:57:41.670
I decided to sleep in the back room of a ransacked
post office that night.

02:57:41.670 --> 02:57:48.229
I didn’t want to move in the dark with these
things around.

02:57:48.229 --> 02:57:51.510
One touch, and I’d be dead.

02:57:51.510 --> 02:57:53.149
No thank you.

02:57:53.149 --> 02:57:54.250
Day 5.

02:57:54.250 --> 02:57:57.630
Why the hell did they do this to us!?

02:57:57.630 --> 02:58:06.250
These monsters at the SCP Foundation, flooding
the world with abominations intent on ending

02:58:06.250 --> 02:58:07.250
human life.

02:58:07.250 --> 02:58:09.330
One of those flesh monsters almost killed
me today.

02:58:09.330 --> 02:58:22.550
I was trying to sneak down a back alley when
I heard the chittering of bony legs following

02:58:22.550 --> 02:58:23.550
me.

02:58:23.550 --> 02:58:30.250
I turned and saw it, reaching out towards
me with fleshy tendrils.

02:58:30.250 --> 02:58:36.649
I raised my handgun and fired several times,
to no effect.

02:58:36.649 --> 02:58:49.811
I was preparing for death, or worse, when
some huge, metal vehicle I couldn’t recognize

02:58:49.811 --> 02:58:52.410
pulled into the alley.

02:58:52.410 --> 02:59:01.150
Men spilled out of it - Well, they weren’t
just men, they were cyborgs, in heavy, technologically-advanced

02:59:01.150 --> 02:59:04.960
armor and weapons straight out of Star Wars.

02:59:04.960 --> 02:59:08.890
They burned the creature to death with a volley
of concentrated heat beams.

02:59:08.890 --> 02:59:17.140
I thanked my rescuers profusely and asked
them their names.

02:59:17.140 --> 02:59:20.810
They simply told me:

02:59:20.810 --> 02:59:23.600
“We’re devotees of the Broken God.

02:59:23.600 --> 02:59:25.920
Stay safe out there, fellow traveler.

02:59:25.920 --> 02:59:27.470
Mekhane be with you.”

02:59:27.470 --> 02:59:28.470
Day 6.

02:59:28.470 --> 02:59:33.270
I wasn’t just surviving for my own sake,
you need to understand that.

02:59:33.270 --> 02:59:37.880
I wanted to get back to my ex-wife and son
as quickly as possible, knowing all the horrors

02:59:37.880 --> 02:59:45.939
that the SCP Foundation had unleashed onto
the world for no logical reason.

02:59:45.939 --> 02:59:50.010
As morbid as it sounds, even if we all died,
I’d rather we die together than apart.

02:59:50.010 --> 02:59:58.250
But every day, I developed a new reason to
know this journey wouldn’t be easy.

02:59:58.250 --> 03:00:06.220
While trying to escape from one of DC’s
more densely-packed urban areas, I encountered

03:00:06.220 --> 03:00:07.750
a horrific monster.

03:00:07.750 --> 03:00:17.450
It looked like a giant, mutant crocodile the
size of a bus, vicious as hell, and it could

03:00:17.450 --> 03:00:18.450
talk.

03:00:18.450 --> 03:00:25.560
When it clapped its eyes on me, it growled
“Disgusting…” and started running towards

03:00:25.560 --> 03:00:26.560
me.

03:00:26.560 --> 03:00:32.420
But the actual physical structure of DC saved
my ass this time.

03:00:32.420 --> 03:00:41.109
You see, the streets of DC are designed to
give in and collapse if ever huge military

03:00:41.109 --> 03:00:46.950
vehicles roll over them, in order to prevent
the capital from being invaded by a military

03:00:46.950 --> 03:00:47.950
force.

03:00:47.950 --> 03:00:56.729
The reptile clearly didn’t know this, cause
it seemed awful confused when the ground gave

03:00:56.729 --> 03:01:14.609
out underneath it, and I was able to run to
safety while it tried to scramble up the walls.

03:01:14.609 --> 03:01:16.470
Day 7.

03:01:16.470 --> 03:01:29.470
A week into this hellish new world, and against
all odds, I’m still alive.

03:01:29.470 --> 03:01:44.330
Though it’s not for lack of trying from
the gaggle of killer freaks the monsters at

03:01:44.330 --> 03:01:52.870
the SCP Foundation have unleashed to terrorize
the streets.

03:01:52.870 --> 03:01:56.939
Today, I saw something terrifying.

03:01:56.939 --> 03:02:08.390
A man was lured into a dark corner by the
voice of a young child, claiming to be his

03:02:08.390 --> 03:02:09.390
daughter.

03:02:09.390 --> 03:02:18.899
He approached the dark with tears in his eyes
when a pack of monsters jumped out and began

03:02:18.899 --> 03:02:21.029
devouring him alive.

03:02:21.029 --> 03:02:31.840
Their long, sharp fangs and raw red hides
are going to linger in my dreams forever.

03:02:31.840 --> 03:02:35.410
You can’t trust voices anymore.

03:02:35.410 --> 03:02:38.270
You can’t trust them.

03:02:38.270 --> 03:02:39.700
Day 8.

03:02:39.700 --> 03:02:49.319
I’m almost at the edge of the city, but
the nightmares continue.

03:02:49.319 --> 03:02:58.850
I was able to narrowly dodge a group of men
walking in perfect step, like one of those

03:02:58.850 --> 03:03:03.880
flash mobs that always used to pop up.

03:03:03.880 --> 03:03:11.690
In the middle of them, there was a woman with
what looked like some kind of strange skin

03:03:11.690 --> 03:03:12.690
condition.

03:03:12.690 --> 03:03:22.479
They didn’t seem all that aggressive until
they came upon a pair of women also trying

03:03:22.479 --> 03:03:25.700
to make their way out of the city.

03:03:25.700 --> 03:03:36.670
The men in the trancelike state charged at
them, holding the women in place and seeming

03:03:36.670 --> 03:03:45.400
to strangle them while the woman with the
skin condition just laughed.

03:03:45.400 --> 03:03:57.189
I would have charged in there to help, but
there were maybe twenty of the hypnotized

03:03:57.189 --> 03:04:15.110
men in the group, and with one magazine of
handgun ammo left, even with perfect aim I

03:04:15.110 --> 03:04:17.760
couldn’t drop them all.

03:04:17.760 --> 03:04:35.180
But the guilt of leaving those women to die
will stay with me forever.

03:04:35.180 --> 03:04:41.319
Over the next nine days, I encountered zombies.

03:04:41.319 --> 03:04:50.140
I kid you not, genuine, honest-to-god zombies,
the Walking God Damn Dead, infesting a road

03:04:50.140 --> 03:04:57.399
on the edge of town.

03:04:57.399 --> 03:05:06.319
There was a huge crowd of them, gurgling and
growling, skin looking green and rotten.

03:05:06.319 --> 03:05:14.740
I’ve seen so many zombie movies, but seeing
the genuine article in person was still utterly

03:05:14.740 --> 03:05:15.740
revolting.

03:05:15.740 --> 03:05:30.250
When they spotted me and started running towards
me, I instinctively pulled out my weapon and

03:05:30.250 --> 03:05:32.979
fired several times.

03:05:32.979 --> 03:05:43.930
I scored a couple of respectable headshots,
but they just kept coming.

03:05:43.930 --> 03:06:00.870
The only thing I could do was run into a nearby
gas station and lock the door behind me.

03:06:00.870 --> 03:06:13.050
For a second there, I thought I was doomed
until I suddenly developed the wherewithal

03:06:13.050 --> 03:06:19.040
to check behind the cash register - Where,
to my immense luck, the clerk had apparently

03:06:19.040 --> 03:06:24.220
left a pump-action shotgun with several shells.

03:06:24.220 --> 03:06:34.739
With the extra firepower, I snuck out of the
back of the gas station, approached the zombie

03:06:34.739 --> 03:06:45.340
horde from behind, and executed them with
a series of well-chosen shots.

03:06:45.340 --> 03:06:53.350
I managed to steal and refuel a car to get
out of town.

03:06:53.350 --> 03:07:08.020
On the way out, just to add to the surreality
of this whole situation, I saw a witch in

03:07:08.020 --> 03:07:09.600
a ditch.

03:07:09.600 --> 03:07:28.570
Sounds like I just made it up for the rhyme,
but no, there was a genuine witch hiding in

03:07:28.570 --> 03:07:35.370
the grass, chewing on a dead body.

03:07:35.370 --> 03:07:47.319
That was the moment that I knew, on some level,
that things would never be normal again.

03:07:47.319 --> 03:07:49.630
The best I could hope for was survival.

03:07:49.630 --> 03:07:51.960
The 21 days after that were incredibly eventful,
to say the least.

03:07:51.960 --> 03:07:57.439
Thank god I had the foraged shotgun with me,
or I would have been a goner, because almost

03:07:57.439 --> 03:08:02.399
as soon as I left town, that huge, monstrous
lizard appeared again, carrying a dead deer

03:08:02.399 --> 03:08:03.399
in its mouth.

03:08:03.399 --> 03:08:07.600
Had it followed me, or was it just a coincidence
that I ran into it again?

03:08:07.600 --> 03:08:10.760
Hell, maybe there was more than one of those
horrible dinosaurs out there.

03:08:10.760 --> 03:08:14.680
But the second it noticed me driving by, it
immediately began to chase me.

03:08:14.680 --> 03:08:20.570
I put the pedal to the metal and hoped beyond
hope that this monster wouldn’t catch up

03:08:20.570 --> 03:08:25.790
with me, but I kept seeing it get bigger and
bigger in my rear-view mirror.

03:08:25.790 --> 03:08:33.580
That creature was impossibly fast for its
size, and believe me when I saw it was getting

03:08:33.580 --> 03:08:34.870
close, I started sweating.

03:08:34.870 --> 03:08:39.279
With a sharp turn at a crucial moment, I was
able to gain a vital lead and lose the beast.

03:08:39.279 --> 03:08:44.569
Soon enough, it was no longer in my peripherals,
which was exactly how I liked it.

03:08:44.569 --> 03:08:50.430
Then I ran into a gaggle of horrific creatures
that I can hardly even explain.

03:08:50.430 --> 03:08:55.870
I was getting out of my stolen car to forage
for food in a nearby supermarket when suddenly

03:08:55.870 --> 03:08:59.940
I was swarmed by what I can only describe
as a group of…

03:08:59.940 --> 03:09:01.130
Human chicken monsters?

03:09:01.130 --> 03:09:07.350
As stupid as they sound compared to the other
things I’ve faced, they were fast and incredibly

03:09:07.350 --> 03:09:08.350
aggressive.

03:09:08.350 --> 03:09:15.141
I was lucky to dodge a few of their brutal
charges and retaliate with a few blasts from

03:09:15.141 --> 03:09:21.850
the shotgun, which at least bought me time
to escape - Even though I had to abandon my

03:09:21.850 --> 03:09:22.850
car.

03:09:22.850 --> 03:09:28.110
Easy come, easy go seem to be the rules of
this terrible new world.

03:09:28.110 --> 03:09:33.040
Are they ever going to run out of horrible
beasts to throw at me?

03:09:33.040 --> 03:09:34.040
Let’s see…

03:09:34.040 --> 03:09:39.569
Over the eleven days that followed, between
periods of pure hiding, I actually had a first-hand

03:09:39.569 --> 03:09:43.470
confrontation with agents of the SCP Foundation.

03:09:43.470 --> 03:09:48.670
After the things I’ve seen, if I could punch
every single one of them so hard that they’d

03:09:48.670 --> 03:09:50.520
never wake up, I’d do it, in a heartbeat.

03:09:50.520 --> 03:09:55.540
It was their fault that all of this was happening,
and we’d never even know why.

03:09:55.540 --> 03:09:57.300
Well, that’s not entirely true.

03:09:57.300 --> 03:10:01.080
I was able to get myself some answers the
hard way…

03:10:01.080 --> 03:10:06.510
I saw a helicopter circling above while I
was hiding behind a small collection of buildings.

03:10:06.510 --> 03:10:11.160
They were shining a spotlight around, and
any time they saw a person walking out into

03:10:11.160 --> 03:10:13.100
the open, they ruthlessly gunned them down.

03:10:13.100 --> 03:10:18.600
When one person managed to duck away and run
through a narrow alley, the helicopter landed

03:10:18.600 --> 03:10:23.240
and several men in tactical gear with assault
rifles stepped out.

03:10:23.240 --> 03:10:30.130
When I recognized the SCP Foundation logo
on the side of the chopper, I felt my blood

03:10:30.130 --> 03:10:31.130
rising to boil.

03:10:31.130 --> 03:10:34.359
These were the ones who killed all these people.

03:10:34.359 --> 03:10:41.410
As they began to disperse, I approached from
behind with murder in my eyes, and used the

03:10:41.410 --> 03:10:44.280
shotgun to kill several of them.

03:10:44.280 --> 03:10:52.529
Before any of them had a chance to retaliate,
I grabbed one of their fallen assault rifles

03:10:52.529 --> 03:10:54.819
and fired back, killing or injuring them all.

03:10:54.819 --> 03:10:59.680
The ones that were heavily injured were incapacitated,
but oddly, they didn’t seem to be in pain.

03:10:59.680 --> 03:11:07.149
I aimed the gun at one of their heads and
told them to tell me why they were doing all

03:11:07.149 --> 03:11:08.149
this.

03:11:08.149 --> 03:11:12.960
That SCP Foundation foot soldier just smiled
and said, “You’re never going to know

03:11:12.960 --> 03:11:14.899
how much we helped you.

03:11:14.899 --> 03:11:16.069
But that’s okay.

03:11:16.069 --> 03:11:18.779
We’re used to dying in the dark.

03:11:18.779 --> 03:11:20.250
You’ll have to learn.”

03:11:20.250 --> 03:11:22.830
So of course, I shot him in the head.

03:11:22.830 --> 03:11:23.830
Hey, wouldn’t you?

03:11:23.830 --> 03:11:24.830
I was better armed than ever, soon enough.

03:11:24.830 --> 03:11:33.080
I had a new sidearm and an assault rifle,
along with a hell of a lot of ammo, from the

03:11:33.080 --> 03:11:34.160
fallen SCP Foundation agents.

03:11:34.160 --> 03:11:37.470
I took a little joy in the irony that the
SCP Foundation, the ones behind all this,

03:11:37.470 --> 03:11:40.529
had provided me with the tools to defy their
reign of terror.

03:11:40.529 --> 03:11:42.899
But none of that prepared me for that maniac
in the mask.

03:11:42.899 --> 03:11:47.760
After getting a few days' rest in a basement
apartment I broke into, I emerged to continue

03:11:47.760 --> 03:11:48.760
the journey.

03:11:48.760 --> 03:11:53.880
I was maybe about fifty miles away from where
my ex and son were living, if they’d managed

03:11:53.880 --> 03:11:56.859
to somehow stay put and survive all this time.

03:11:56.859 --> 03:12:01.100
But when I left the basement apartment, something
was waiting for me.

03:12:01.100 --> 03:12:06.050
It was a man wearing a white comedy mask,
dripping with this strange, black gunk, and

03:12:06.050 --> 03:12:07.420
carrying a fire axe.

03:12:07.420 --> 03:12:10.239
“Well hello there, good sir!” he said
with a laugh.

03:12:10.239 --> 03:12:11.790
“How lovely to meet you.

03:12:11.790 --> 03:12:15.200
It’s been a while since I’ve seen another
one like you.

03:12:15.200 --> 03:12:20.350
You look like you’re carrying a lot of weight
on your shoulders, but don’t worry!

03:12:20.350 --> 03:12:22.470
I can help with that!”

03:12:22.470 --> 03:12:27.290
He gave a diabolical laugh and swung the axe
at my head.

03:12:27.290 --> 03:12:30.399
I was able to narrowly dodge the blow and
return fire into center-mass with my rifle.

03:12:30.399 --> 03:12:31.399
He seemed unfazed.

03:12:31.399 --> 03:12:33.910
He just laughed and started charging forwards
with another swing from the axe.

03:12:33.910 --> 03:12:38.390
Rather than shooting him again, and potentially
attracting some unwanted attention, I decided

03:12:38.390 --> 03:12:40.120
to just run for it.

03:12:40.120 --> 03:12:42.600
Every day, there are new surprises out here…

03:12:42.600 --> 03:12:46.810
I made my way through a small town that had
been utterly decimated.

03:12:46.810 --> 03:12:48.420
Homes and buildings were empty.

03:12:48.420 --> 03:12:52.979
A huge stack of human corpses sat in the center
of town.

03:12:52.979 --> 03:13:01.069
On top of the pile there stood a heavily tattooed
man, carrying a huge, black blade in each

03:13:01.069 --> 03:13:02.069
hand.

03:13:02.069 --> 03:13:04.700
He was clashing the blades together and calling
out, “This world has no worthy opponents

03:13:04.700 --> 03:13:05.700
for the mighty Able!

03:13:05.700 --> 03:13:11.140
Step forward and meet my blade, so at least
you can die with honor, you sad, timid little

03:13:11.140 --> 03:13:12.140
sheep!”

03:13:12.140 --> 03:13:18.120
I didn’t feel like dying with honor that
day, so I instead chose to discretely make

03:13:18.120 --> 03:13:21.670
my way across the town, hoping that the “Mighty
Able” wouldn’t notice me.

03:13:21.670 --> 03:13:26.910
And this paid off in the end, as I was able
to find an abandoned motorcycle, and high-tail

03:13:26.910 --> 03:13:30.311
it out of there with all of my scavenged weaponry.

03:13:30.311 --> 03:13:38.400
At long last, I was so damn close…

03:13:38.400 --> 03:13:40.439
Just before day 90, that monstrous reptile
came back.

03:13:40.439 --> 03:13:45.069
I swear to god, that monster is following
me, it’s more than just a coincidence now.

03:13:45.069 --> 03:13:51.670
I was no more than twenty miles from my ex’s
place, driving at great speeds on my newly

03:13:51.670 --> 03:13:57.670
purloined motorcycle when the giant, scaly
monster emerged from the side of the road

03:13:57.670 --> 03:13:59.430
and knocked me off my bike.

03:13:59.430 --> 03:14:03.770
If I didn’t think quickly and rely on my
training, I would have been killed - I’d

03:14:03.770 --> 03:14:07.189
done two tours with the marine corps in Afghanistan
before being selected for defending the president,

03:14:07.189 --> 03:14:09.370
a job that, technically, I’d failed miserably
at.

03:14:09.370 --> 03:14:14.670
But those skills might be the only thing that
could let me stand a chance against this monster.

03:14:14.670 --> 03:14:17.910
It reared up and tried to attack me again.

03:14:17.910 --> 03:14:25.960
I used my stolen assault rifle to concentrate
fire on its eyes, trying to at least distract

03:14:25.960 --> 03:14:26.960
it.

03:14:26.960 --> 03:14:35.340
Its wounds healed quickly, and the pain of
the blasts doesn’t seem to have affected

03:14:35.340 --> 03:14:36.810
it in the slightest.

03:14:36.810 --> 03:14:42.710
But every time it lunges for me, I’m lucky
enough to have the room to dodge it.

03:14:42.710 --> 03:14:50.750
This isn’t sustainable - I know that it
will have energy long after I tire.

03:14:50.750 --> 03:15:04.540
What I need is a distraction so I can get
the hell out of here.

03:15:04.540 --> 03:15:07.200
Then, an idea hits me!

03:15:07.200 --> 03:15:26.820
I turn the gun on
the motorcycle’s fuel

03:15:26.820 --> 03:15:33.130
tank and fire off a few rounds.

03:15:33.130 --> 03:15:34.130
BOOM!

03:15:34.130 --> 03:15:36.270
A nice, big, distracting fireball.

03:15:36.270 --> 03:15:49.340
It buys me a few seconds, but that’s enough
to let me get away down a narrow alley and

03:15:49.340 --> 03:15:51.310
make myself disappear.

03:15:51.310 --> 03:16:05.330
All I can say is, I really hope I don’t
see that monster again.

03:16:05.330 --> 03:16:09.729
Isn’t three times enough for one life?

03:16:09.729 --> 03:16:13.240
Just when you think things can’t get any
stranger, you see something that almost breaks

03:16:13.240 --> 03:16:14.710
your brain with its sheer surrealness.

03:16:14.710 --> 03:16:34.330
While traveling through the town next to the
one where my ex and son live, I saw a heart.

03:16:34.330 --> 03:16:46.699
Like a giant, beating heart, on spider legs
with a scorpion tail, destroying everything.

03:16:46.699 --> 03:16:52.870
It was tearing buildings apart and somehow
setting people on fire with its caustic venom.

03:16:52.870 --> 03:17:06.040
What on earth was the SCP Foundation that
it even knew about all these things?

03:17:06.040 --> 03:17:10.200
I just kept walking, avoiding attention.

03:17:10.200 --> 03:17:12.390
It’s all I could do.

03:17:12.390 --> 03:17:13.739
It’s all anyone can do.

03:17:13.739 --> 03:17:17.800
No one is saving a world like this, they’re
just avoiding getting killed in it.

03:17:17.800 --> 03:17:20.210
Or helping someone they love to avoid getting
killed.

03:17:20.210 --> 03:17:22.380
Speaking of, only one day off now.

03:17:22.380 --> 03:17:24.210
Only one day off…

03:17:24.210 --> 03:17:25.210
Day 100.

03:17:25.210 --> 03:17:29.340
I arrived.

03:17:29.340 --> 03:17:43.660
After all this hell, after seeing all this
nightmarish carnage that I’ll never be able

03:17:43.660 --> 03:18:11.080
to unsee, I’ve reached the home of the ones
I love.

03:18:11.080 --> 03:18:13.060
The ones I’ve been surviving for.

03:18:13.060 --> 03:18:39.050
I walk up to the front door, step inside,
and find that the house is…

03:18:39.050 --> 03:18:45.479
Empty.

03:18:45.479 --> 03:19:04.620
Not a soul in sight.

03:19:04.620 --> 03:19:13.760
For a moment, I’m terrified.

03:19:13.760 --> 03:20:09.479
Had they

03:20:09.479 --> 03:20:16.970
been killed?

03:20:16.970 --> 03:21:01.510
There was no blood.

03:21:01.510 --> 03:23:11.090
No bodies.

03:23:11.090 --> 03:23:12.790
No sign of anything out of the ordinary.

03:23:12.790 --> 03:23:15.810
That means that, like myself, maybe they were
on the move.

03:23:15.810 --> 03:23:18.329
Maybe they were fleeing to find safety of
their own.

03:23:18.329 --> 03:23:22.100
And if that was the case, I had a pretty good
feeling that I’d know exactly where they

03:23:22.100 --> 03:23:23.100
are.

03:23:23.100 --> 03:23:32.649
But my thoughts were interrupted by a loud
rumbling noise, coming from the house’s

03:23:32.649 --> 03:23:38.779
basement…

